(39, 26, 'Solana Live Call', 'This call was realted to a project being developed by a member.', 'This call was realted to a project being developed by a member.', 'text', 102, 6, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/LLBxvyLMKTA', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-28 20:34:37', '2025-08-11 21:12:19', NULL, NULL),
(40, 26, 'Austin Clark\'s Gemini Masters CLI Functionality', 'This live presentation covers Austin Clarks functionality he added to the Gemini Masters CLI', '', 'text', 7, 20, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/Nyw-GgXzQXY', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-28 20:37:29', '2025-07-28 20:38:14', NULL, 'Okay, great. Yeah, thanks uh Ed and Joe for having me here. Excuse me. I\'m feeling a little under the weather today. Um but we\'ll get it done. I\'ll make this really short and sweet, too. Uh yeah, so the challenge that uh Ed, just to lay a little context, the challenge that Ed presented to us in the pro community was to, you know, take this CLI uh Gemini master CLI and improve it in any way that you see fit. So for me, it was real easy. I just said, \"Okay, I\'m using the claw co I\'m using cloud code right now. Um, what are some disparities between those two?\" And uh went online, found that one of the things that people were requesting was the ability to to create custom commands which did not currently exist or does not currently exist in Google uh CLI natively. So, uh really I actually used the CLI and in addition to cloud code to kind of help me give a little bit more scope to um the task. I used uh both of those tools to effectively vibe code the whole thing, right? I didn\'t actually do anything anything myself and uh it actually did quite a quite a good job. I think I\'ve sent the request to share my screen here in just a moment and I can demo it for you. It\'s not incredibly impressive and you know it\'s really quite simp simplistic right now more as a proof of concept. Uh but here let me show you exactly how it works. So I I had to stop my share first. Yeah. No, you\'re totally fine. You\'re totally fine. Let me share my other screen here. Okay. So we\'re all seeing the uh cursor interface here, right? So I tried to initially hijack you know just the slash command but uh that was giving me some problems. So really the custom command now the way that it works is you can type in it uses this custo Gemini commands folder right and you just put in any the idea for the command is you basically it\'ll allow you to pass a prompt to the LLM that you would regularly use right something that you might find yourself using frequently you could basically take that context and create a custom command out of it so you can reuse it over and over again across various projects whatever the case may be Uh, so it uses this custom command and you can feed it in multiple formats here, right? Pretty much any way that you would feed a a prompt to an NL LLM normally, whatever format you would do, you could use that here. You could pass it a JSON, you can pass it markdown, or you can pass a text file, whatever you want to do. And the way it works is pretty simple. And uh, you can see here I have an example of one of these here, this hello world, right? I just want it to be this is just a prompt that I\'m going to pass to it. So I\'m going to do is pass the name of the file. This is how it recognizes it in the code. It\'ll say, \"Okay, your custom command is the name of the file.\" So, if you have duplicates, that\'s what I haven\'t tested yet. If you have duplicate file names, I don\'t know how it\'ll handle that. We\'ll see. It\'s not definitely not ready for prime time yet. Let\'s just try this one. Right. So, what I\'m going to do is pass, say this is just a normal uh prompt that I find myself using regularly. I\'m going to pass that to the L or to the CLI. It\'s going to process it, pass that to the LLM, and then it\'ll give me and it\'s using the tools that already exist in the CLI, right? These read read file tools, these I think there\'s another one that reads like multiple files, and it\'ll give you back the response. What it\'s actually really what I actually really like about this is that one of the things that I had it do when I was vibe coding this was you can do I have this explain one here, right? And this explain custom command will give you it\'ll read the code if you want it to read code but it\'ll read files if you want it to read files too. And I think this is where it\'s kind of powerful is because the the usefulness in having a custom command in this context is not necessarily uh the command itself but how it interacts with other tools that you can be implemented in the CLI. So if we want to explain and then I can use this other command to say let\'s just say like the read me right uh and then that way you can get a synopsis and you can do that with anything like I added a job title a job description in here and it it\'ll give you a synopsis of the job title it\'ll summarize data for you however you want you can pass it uh documents in any format and having a custom command that will go out and find those for you and then utilize other tools that are already built into the CLI I think is really where the value of a custom command passed to the prompt or you passed to the to the whatever large language model you\'re using is is really where it becomes quite powerful. Yeah. And actually one of the things I really like about this is something that I ran into after like 12 hours in Claude code was that Claude forgot how to deploy npm commands. So I and I\'m on I\'m talking about when I\'m on build number 36. Okay. And by then it had compressed the context a couple times. And the last build I did took me a really long time. And if we could actually have something where you actually have a deploy or you have the ability to remind the LLM of how it did something because you know we were I was pushing to npm which isn\'t necessarily something I know how to do already and then it forgot. So I couldn\'t like say, \"Hey, do these commands to push to MT MPM because I didn\'t actually know how to do it.\" So something like this and actually Austin, I think one of the other features that would be awesome to add on to this is to be able to create those files on the fly when it figures out how to do something. So like if we said, \"Hey, um, push to push to npm.\" And maybe it takes you three attempts to push to npm and then you say, \"Okay, save these instructions to a custom command.\" So, oh, you can do that. Yeah, you can do that. I actually that\'s that\'s how I created one of these. I I can demo it right now. Let\'s try it. Let\'s I mean, we\'re kind of uh this is still, you know, not ready for production, but let\'s go ahead and give it a shot. So, um Oh, well, now now I\'m not even sure what we should do. Like, what what should we pass to the to the prompt? What should be our initial prompt? Um wow. I um I got like anybody got any ideas? Well, yeah, I don\'t have any ideas right now. Maybe we won\'t do it right now. Yeah, but the point is is you can do that and I did do that already. You you So, if you and you can just say, \"Hey, use my last prompt to create a custom command and it and it\'ll do that.\" Okay, beautiful. Using using the other tools that already exist. Yeah. See, this is Yeah. See, this is this is actually what we\'re talking about. So being able to take something and just keep adding to it is wildly powerful, you know, and so some of the other things we need to do is we need to because we only have 32K in the context window, we need fine grain control over the context window. Um, one thing is being able to actually see what is in the context so we can pull things out of the context if they don\'t belong. But uh, so we got one hand raised. So uh, SAD, did you have a question?');
