her topic. The next topic I want to talk about now that I\'ve talked about your your hardware for your development environment is how large language models actually work. Now the important thing here is really you don\'t need to know about the transformer model. You don\'t need to know about lojits and softmax and the difference between relu and you know all these learning models. You don\'t need to know those things. What you do need to know is that there are differences from one large language model to the next. Now large language models are built on the transformer architecture which comes from the paper attention is all you need that came out I think in 2018. That paper is the model for all transformers and LLMs. Chat GPT is a generalized predictive transformer. That is their implementation of the transformer model. There are different implementations. Claude Grock, they all are implementing the transformer model, but they\'re doing it differently. So, GPT, when you call something a GPT, that is open AI\'s version of a transformer model. that is not necessarily everybody\'s um version of a transformer model. Now the first type of model is oneshot models or I\'m sorry dense models. Now with a dense model when you run inference all the parameters in the entire LLM are activating. And so that means it\'s sort of like I see something and my whole brain is going to work on solving that problem. That is not necessarily the most efficient way to go, but that was how neural networks started out. And so a lot of the LLMs that you see are dense models. And that is because they\'re they\'re basically transformer neural networks. When you ask it a question, it\'s going to use all of the parameters, whether it\'s 32 million parameters or 1.7 trillion parameters. All of the tr all of the parameters, which are the weights and the biases, are going to be used in coming up with the answer. Now, the third one down I have on this list is called mixture of experts. Mixture of experts was an innovative way to not have the models use all of the parameters. It\'s more like showing up at a college campus and the first place you go to is the reception desk who tells you what buildings you need to go to. So with the mixture of experts models, which mixtrol is one of those, it is going to use it. It takes the entire model and it breaks it up into call it departments. So it\'s like I\'m going to ask a question. It\'s going to go to something like a router and that router is going to say, \"Oh, you need chemistry and physics, go that way.\" Oh, you need uh uh human studies and something else, go that way. Now, what mixture of experts always does is it recommends two areas to go into so that they can fact check each other. Now, what makes Mixtixure of Experts models so powerful is they run much more efficiently and with much less power. So, when you\'re getting to models that have let\'s say 5 trillion parameters in them, a mixture of experts model is going to use far less electricity and you\'re going to be able to run it on far less hardware than you would if you\'re running a dense model. Because a dense model, the entire LLM has to fit into into GPU memory for you to run it. Whereas with a mixture of experts model, it\'s going to come back slower, but it\'s going to load the sections into the GPU as it needs it. Now, the second thing I have on this list are the thinking models or the reasoning models. and chat GPT40 came out to be the first and then Deep Seek came out and kind of said, \"Hey, we\'re here and you\'re you\'re we\'re here to stay.\" The thinking models are really taking over because the biggest thing about them is they eliminate hallucinations by attacking a problem from multiple steps and actually thinking through the reasoning of the answer. Now chat GPT03 is one of is their big reasoning model and then this the claude uh claude for opus is their big thinking model and the thinking models are incredibly powerful but they\'re also in