o do is initialize the project. Now the next file that\'s really important is requirements.md. This is your project plan. Now, it\'s an MD file, which means it\'s a markdown file, which means it\'s in this format. Now, if you\'re ever confused or you\'re ever not sure if your MD file is in the right format, just ask Claude. You could go in here right now and I could go into Claude and I could say, is my MD file in the right format? I I go off I run it and it is going to tell me that it is in the right format because I asked it to or I asked it to write it. So it is in an excellent format. It\'s well structured and comprehensive. So th in this case, I wanted to build a stock tracking application, something that could could track a stock that would use real- time APIs. So I had a pretty long conversation with Claude on Claude desktop to determine what functionality this stock application would have based on other stock tracking applications that are out there. So it is it is building this requirements.mmd for me. And then the next file is text stack. So in textstack.mmd I decided that I wanted this to be written in nex.js and I have an entire text stack of what dependencies I want to use, what I what API keys I\'m using, what environment variables I need to say. I create that in textstack.mmd. Now, if you notice, if you if you\'re familiar with cursor, we have not pressed go yet at all. And we\'ve done a lot. And really, Claude is about planning the project out ahead of time much more than actually executing it. Now, when it executes it, it\'s you\'re it\'s going to go it could go off for hours, but the more time you spend at the planning stage, the better off you are. Then this next file here is called design notes. Now I got this design notes using one of the coolest tools in um that Claude has which is the ability for me to go to a website I like. So I went to one of my Angular websites on my Mac. I went shift command4 drew a picture around the website and pasted it into claude and said please to please use this as your inspiration to create a CSS file for my application. So what it did is it developed this design notes.md based on an interface called absc which is a project I\'m working on. So in this case, it actually was able to look at my screen, see what my font hierarchy was, what fonts I was using, what colors I was using. And so now when I design this, when I let Claude run this stock trading application, it\'s going to use the design principles that I gave it by going into inspiration mode. So then we have our API keys.mmd which is basically do not remember to add it to get ignore if you put your API keys in your uh your if you create an API keys.mmd make sure you do not send it to get and that is security issues have been the the thing that is holding back vibe coding more than anything else. Okay, so this is the claw rules file that we already went through. Now there is a thing called plan mode and you get to plan mode by hitting shift tab twice uh from the command. Okay, it now says I am in plan mode. Now I want to also do model opus. Now I\'ve just set my model to the expensive thinking model. Okay, what you want to do is when you\'re planning, use Opus. When you\'re executing, use Sonnet. Now, Sonnet co sonnet and co Opus write code equally as well, but Sonnet is 12th the cost of Opus. But when you\'re planning, Opus plans way better than Sonnet. So, do your planning with Opus. Now that you\'re in plan mode, it is actually writing to all of these files. It is your planning. So I got, you know, basically plan mode is what got me my requirements MD. It got me my um it got me my text stack. This was all done in plan mode. Then all I have to do is shift tab back and now I\'m out of plan mode and I\'m ex in execution mode. So now I\'m going to type in model sonnet. And now I\'ve set the model to sonnet, which means now I can um do my builds, write my code, do it all in in sonnet. Now, um when you\'re in plan mode, do not be afraid to ask Claude what it thinks. If it is Claude is the LLM that if it if there was any LLM that I thought had a personality, it would be Claude. You Claude has no problem telling you if you did something stupid. Whereas Chat GPT is always going to tell you you\'re a genius. Claude has a tendency to not tell you you\'re a genius if you\'re doing something wrong. But it also follows instructions. So this is also a good time to talk about coding models. Chad GPT way too prone for hallucinations. Gemini doesn\'t have hallucinations, but it\'s very opinionated. And Gemini will and it also has a tendency to gaslight you. I\'ve had certain situations where Gemini Pro 2.5 has told me there\'s a calendar on the screen. I go to the screen, there\'s no calendar, and it basically tells me basically I\'m an idiot that there\'s a calendar on the screen. So, I have gotten into situations where I could not get any further with Gemini Pro. So, that is why as of today, Claude is what I\'m recommending now. But don\'t be afraid to ask Claude what it thinks. Now, I\'ve already talked about running Claude from cursor or VS Code. One of the reasons is you want to checkpoint often. Okay, it is not like cursor where you can checkpoint your code and roll back your code in the IDE. Cursor, what I do is every time something works, I get to a stage where something\'s working, I don\'t have bugs in it, push it to get. Okay. Um, now I\'ve also found that if you plan right, you may never need your checkpoints, but definitely push to get as often as you feel appropriate. Overuse Git that you\'re going to find um if you do go down a bad road, you\'re going to need it and and starting over is not not what you want to do. And I have had to start over several times anytime I\'ve used Gemini. And in my experiences with Gemini CLI, I\'ve also had to just abandon projects. So the the next thing you can do is um inspiration for UI design. So if I were to hit now you can paste images into claude which I find incredible. Okay. Now the two reasons to do it is inspiration for the UI design and debugging. If you get to the point where you\'re running out of the browser, you\'re running independently. Joe and I had run into a problem where something worked fine running locally, but when we pushed it to Apache, it didn\'t work anymore. Well, the easiest thing you can do is shift command 4 on a Mac, cut out your image, paste it into Claude, and say, \"What\'s going on here?\" And then if it\'s really complicated, that\'s actually when I switch back to Opus because Opus is going to reason through the bugs it sees pretty well. It\'s going to it\'s actually going to think it through. Now, the other thing you want to do often is when you\'re in here, do a forward slashcle. Anytime your context window starts to get full, you\'re going to be subject to hallucinations. And the reason you get the hallucinations, especially if you\'re coding in something like Angular, you\'re jumping back and forth between programming languages. It\'s writing TypeScript, then in my case, it\'s writing PHP, then it\'s jumping back and it\'s writing um HTML templates. And when you\'re doing that, as the context window gets bigger and you got more and more in the context and it because it\'s remembering all the prompts that I\'ve given it. So as you get further and further down into your project, you hit clear often. Anytime you\'ve got a feature to work, you\'re moving on to a next feature, just hit clear. That will clear your context window. It\'s going to eliminate the hallucinations and it\'s going to save you money because every time you do something it even if the tokens are cashed you\'re still paying for the tokens that are being used. Then the next thing you want to do is security checks. Now I don\'t know I have a prompt here and I\'ll post it in the school before you deploy anything or even take it off your computer. This is a prompt to give it that basically says please check through all the code and make sure it follows security best practices. Make sure there\'s no sensitive information in the front in the front end and that there are no vulnerabilities that can be exploited every time you before you push anything run that. Okay. And it will go through it will look at your code. It will make sure you can\'t do SQL injections. that will make sure that there\'s no APIs that are being exposed on the front end that somebody could use inspect element and see. Uh this is why vibe coding got a bad name to start off with because people were not running uh security checks. And Ed, I I would also add on to there um about uh removing all council log messages that were used for debugging. Um, a lot of times, um, I guess probably not as much in Claude, but in cursor, it\'ll put in a lot of console log debugging messages to help you figure out where your errors are at. And then you need to remind it to, hey, get rid of those to make your code production ready. Good, good point. And then my second to last thing to talk about is learn what claude code does. Okay, in my case, I\'m just now learning Nex.js. And when I say I\'m learning Nex.js, JS. I\'m just following the bouncing ball because I never plan to code it from scratch. But it is important. There is a prompt you can put in and I I\'ll post this also. Please explain the functionality in the code you just built. Walk me how you changed it and how it works. Act like you\'re a senior engineer teaching me to code. Now, what I recommend you do is you run that after every big after every big set of builds. you save that and you make that part of the project because at some point here you\'re going to want to turn this over to a junior dev. And what you\'re doing is you\'re turning over your claude code context your environment to a junior dev. So run this prompt summarize the results and save it for the next time you work on code. Now the one last thing I am going to add is do not doom scroll while you are waiting for Claude. So most of you found me on Instagram which means you probably all like to scroll through your Instagram. The one way to kill a day is to set off claude off on an adventure and the 30 minutes it takes you\'re sitting here doom scrolling on Instagram which is fine because that\'s how you found me. but it is going to kill your productivity. What I do, and this is a recommendation that was actually given to me by another creator, which is open up a new chat window while while Claude is working, and have a conversation with Claude about the project to get ideas about what you should do next, what other applications of the same type, also what kind of functionality you should add. Just don\'t pick up your phone. Don\'t start checking your emails. Have a conversation with with Claude about the project that you\'re working on because it will give you improvements. And this is um the hardest thing for me at first before I I found this was that I would start Claude, it would go off for an adventure and when it was done, it\'d be done 20 minutes later, but it would be two hours before I would look at the code again. So, this is just a pro tip that I totally recommend. Um so we actually in the pro community we have a uh a live event tomorrow for any of you who do actually want to join. Um we also have regional pricing on that because we did get we have a lot of people from all over the world and the US dollar um is not representative of the cost of living around the world. So DM me if you\'re uh in Southeast Asia or someplace like that. Um, but we\'re going to go through a lot more of the build process. This is stock application that I\'m actually wrote the requirements for. We\'re actually going to build it tomorrow. Um, the other thing is if you join the pro community, um, we\'ve got some projects. There\'s a there\'s a lot of I mean, we just have so many kids who are just getting out of college who are really having trouble. Um, this is a this is about building your resume also by building projects that actually have use. We have some applications that we handcoded that we want to rewrite in uh cursor and in cloud code. We\'ve also got some project managers who are in the pro community that are learning project management of um AI projects. Uh being a project manager, uh I got 4,000 followers on uh Instagram by trashing agile methodology. So we know that developers don\'t like project managers very much. And I\'m very curious to find out can I teach a developer to be a project manager by writing these requirements docs or a project manager as a developer. I\'m really curious. So we are going to we are going to explore that. So I want to thank you guys all. Um, I think this was one of the very few times I my time management was correct and the length of my uh and the length of my PowerPoint was the length of the um the presentation. I want to thank you all for attending. Next week, um, we haven\'t figured out what we\'re going to do next week, but is probably going to be MCP. I really want to get into building some MCP tools so people see exactly how we build MCPS. So, all right, everybody, have a great day. Does that um you have any um so things that came up are um from the chat that I saw were um specifically the claw.md file to post um I said all those texts will be posted in the school community and pinned so they should be in the top four or five posts on the board. um regional pricing um of the external community. Um I said that it was created but it\'s not uh it\'s not 100% fleshed out and set up ready to go to accept anybody in there yet. So we\'re still figuring that out. I\'ll I\'ll just change on that. Okay. So, it\'s currently manual, which means Joe and the rest of the team have not created the automations that if you if you sign up for the um the it\'s it\'s basically AI masters uh Southeast Asia is the name of the regional pricing community and if you join we have to move you manually but um eventually we\'ll we\'ll probably build a Zap year automation to do that. Um, and if uh if anybody is interested in learning Zapier, um, just post in the community that you are because that is uh that is something that Joe has been going down. He\'s been automating a lot of the stuff that we\'re doing with Zapier. Um, if you\'re interested in that, that is that is a real thing. Um, what else? Anything else on there, Joe? Um the I posted it a few times, but if you could flash it up on the screen again right now, the pro community link. And um a couple people asked about your thoughts on Windserve. You mentioned it before, but not at this meeting. So, okay. Honestly, I do not know enough about wind surf to be to give you an educated um view of wind surf.');
