(45, 27, 'Beginners Live Event 7/16/26', 'An introduction to Internet and Web Topics.', '', 'text', 0, 1, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/m9IG19J6Hcs', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-28 20:56:40', '2025-07-28 20:56:41', NULL, 'Yeah, the AI companion is running. Um, and the recording will be available for future reference as well. There is a new um there\'s a new uh course for those. I don\'t know if Ed, if you want to I\'ll tab and show that new course where you\'re posting the live ones. [Music] Yes, I got all this stuff in my way. Um, I\'ll just go to this one. And while you\'re there, I\'ll tell you about the other one that I added at the bottom as well. Right next to yours down here. Yeah. See, at the bottom we have the live event replays. Um, that\'s where all live event recordings will go. And then I added uh community links. These were a couple links that people were asking for. school has the issue of only being able to pin three posts. So, um I had a lot of uh direct uh DMs about asking for these links and also in various chat post. So, I just put them in there. It has the the link to the git, the link to the reinforcement learning and a link to the um uh Stripe payment portal for those that did not subscribe through school. And for the record, um I\'m not that thrilled about school as a platform. So what I\'m vibe coding over here in my free time is the ability to actually link courses to projects. So when you create a project, we can then say these are the prerequisite courses you should take if you\'re going to work on that project. So if you want to build out let\'s say the the Gemini CLI, these are the four or five courses you should master the skills it before you try to do that. But let me get started since uh it is 7:01. So I\'m going to play from start. Okay. So this is our beginner background series. And the idea here is that if you\'re going to do artificial intelligence, most of it is communicating with the with the AI. So, you\'re going to interface with your artificial intelligence through prompting, but without proper vocabulary and knowing what you\'re talking about, you\'re not going to be able to communicate effectively with the AI. And that really becomes very obvious when you\'re vibe coding with something like cursor or with claude code or Gemini CLI because as you\'re debugging and you get to the point where you you\'re trying to get the point across to the LLM that it needs to move this card over to the right. If you\'re using the proper terminology, you know, if you\'re saying move this div tag or move this, move any div with a class of this, if you\'re using the proper terminology, then you\'re going to be able to communicate with the AI a lot better. You\'re going to be able to get your point across and you\'re going to spend a lot less time debugging. And so there\'s certain basics that where you just kind of need to know both to use the tools because some of these tools are actually really more complicated. They\'re they\'re tools that really help you out, but when you use them, there\'s a lot of background information about both traditional tech and artificial intelligence that you um you really need to know in order to be that. So the goal of this today and or this the beginner series in general is to learn enough to start doing simple projects and then work your way up. So the more you learn, the more um the more projects you can do. And we really want this to be a project driven education. You\'re going to learn by doing versus learn by listening to me ramble for an hour at a time. So the idea at the background is is we want you to understand what the AI is doing when it writes code. We want you to understand what it\'s doing when it\'s doing an automation. If it\'s building an MCP tool, whatever it\'s doing, we want you to have that understanding of what the AI is doing. So it\'s not just off running wild without you having some idea of what it needs to do. This is also to give you a basic understanding of the ecosystem that supports it because when we talk about local models versus cloud models, we really should know the difference between those and so it\'s a lot of this is getting the knowledge that is necessary to understand the tools. So the first place I am going to start is with traditional computing and I am going back a ways and I apologize to everybody who already knows all this stuff but I think it\'s something that we really need to just make sure that we have the foundation and that we\'re all at the at the same place. So the way computers work is it goes back to a transistor. So transistors were uh invented in like the 1940s and it basically allows you to have ones and zeros. So if electricity is going through the transistor, it\'s a one. If electricity is not going through the transistor, it\'s a zero. So with putting together a bunch of transistors, you can then represent letters and numbers in binary code, which is ones and zeros. So in this case, everything in the computer is represented as bin binary. So a character A is 0 1 0 0 0 1 and the number five is represented by 4 0 1 0 1. So you can represent data, you can represent characters, numbers, anything you want to represent. You can represent them as big long strings of ones and zeros. Now in addition to representing things as ones and zeros, there\'s a thing called a logic gate. And those those say and or or not. So if you if you look at boolean logic, you know, A and B, A or B, not A or not B, you\'ve got the ability to basically create um process flow. So between by having the ability to represent everything in ones and zeros and basically represent it in your computer and then having logic gates where you have and ors or knots that allows the creation of traditional programming languages. Now there\'s different kinds of programming languages and the first programming languages that came out were procedural programming languages and for most web development you can kind of get away with using procedural programming languages. PHP which is my language of choice whenever I want to do a lot of code and I want to do it fast is a procedural programming language. The things you need to do are organized as procedures, functions, and subutines. So I might write a procedure that says delete record or get this or do this and those would be procedures. And procedural programming languages follow a linear flow. They just it goes through the code. Now you have you could have loops and conditionals, but it really is focusing on how a task is performed. So procedural languages are pretty much all we had up until like the early 198 or probably the late 1980s when we got object-oriented languages. Now object without objectoriented languages today there would be no artificial intelligence and object-oriented languages better represent how the world is structured. So you have these things called classes and classes are basically a blueprint. So let\'s say you have a class called a car. Okay. And objects. So you have a class called the car. And the class is the blueprint. Everything that is a car has these attributes. It has these things about it that make it a car. Objects are instances of a class. So if I have a 100 cars, we have a hundred objects of the car class. Now this is this gets really interesting when you look at these things called inheritance and polymorphism. So uh an electric car is also a car. So an electric car let\'s say a Tesla that is inheriting all of the attributes of a car which means it has four tires. It has uh a motor of some sort that makes it go. It can go forward and in reverse. It has all the things that a car has. but because it inherited what a car is, but now it\'s overridden some of the the the things that make a car a car. So instead of having a gas engine, you\'re replacing the gas engine with the electric motor. And that is where the idea of classes and um objects, that\'s how the whole thing works. So what it allows us to do is build on each other\'s code. And what makes that super important in artificial intelligence is the fact that now you don\'t need to know complex math. You don\'t need to know uh linear algebra. You don\'t need to know gradient descent. You don\'t need to know all these things these that these mathematicians put together. Now I personally am somebody who doesn\'t really not really good at math. Okay. I I got a degree in math. I I learn it well enough to get through it, but I don\'t do it very well. Okay. But I don\'t have to because I know I know what I\'m doing. I don\'t necessarily know how to actually do it. So when you have these functions and you have functions that can build upon each other. So for instance, PyTorch is a library that was created by Google for artificial intelligence and it has a bunch of classes in it for doing different things. So if I want to do a ukitian diff distance between two vectors, all I need to know is that I want to do a ukitian distance between two vectors. I happen to know that shows how similar those two vectors are or but I don\'t actually have to know how to do the math and that\'s how over the period of let\'s say the last 10 years artificial intelligence has gotten really really powerful because there are people who write these classes and they write these class libraries and then there\'s other people who don\'t necessarily know how the classes work but they know how to include them in their code to make things better and better and better. So that\'s how objectoriented languages are making artificial intelligence move so fast because you don\'t have to know everything. You just have to know what to grab and what to use. Now once you get to this point this is where data science comes in. Now in traditional programming you are talking it\'s mostly if then logic. So, if I type the word um if I type a word into the terminal and it\'s and I have a save button, it it knows through ifs and thens and else\'s what to do. Data science is the art of turning raw data into useful information. What you\'re doing with data science and machine learning is you\'re working in the opposite direction. You\'re taking a big pile of data and you\'re looking for the patterns in the data and then you\'re training the model in machine learning to do to to replicate basically it\'s writing the code from the data instead of you writing the code that generates the data. So data science is basically the foundation of artificial intelligence. So, we got to this point where we had computers, we had object-oriented programming, and now we\'ve got data science where you\'re talking about all these different mathematicians who came up with these ideas of decision trees and random forest and convolutional neural networks. And that was the basics of artificial intelligence, which is where you have these things called neural networks. Now, neural networks have been around for a while, but it wasn\'t until maybe the last 15 years or so that that computing power was really good enough to do what you wanted to do with neural networks. So, this picture I have is a picture of a neural network. And each of the circles is called is called well, it\'s a neuron, but it\'s actually called a node. Okay? So, you have weights and biases. Okay? So each of these um layers are the weights and that is a a number between zero and one and then each of these lines is a resistor. Now Frank Rosenblat back in 1958 realized that if you this is what\'s actually called a feed forward neural network. And if it kind of like works like your eyes do. If you put in impul inputs into the input layers, it goes through all this mathematics and then figures out something on the other end. So when you hear the term we\'re training the model, training the model means determining the weights and the biases for all these lines and all these dots to to make it know things and to make it do things. So the reason when you talk about training a model, it might take two or three million dollar worth of electricity to train a model, the number of these circles plus the number of these lines is the number of parameters. So when somebody says I have a 1.7 trillion parameter model, that means there\'s a combination of these dots and these lines that adds up to 1.7 trillion of them. So you\'ve got some really um some really complex math. There\'s uh linear algebra and some calculus related to gradient descent that makes this possible. Now this was not possible for the most part until GPUs came into existence. And GPUs are the graphics processing unit. and they are much better at doing a certain type of mathematics in wildly parallel form. So a CPU if you think so a CPU versus a GPU think of a a CPU as an expressway with eight lines or eight lanes uh an 8 core CPU would have eight lanes of traffic. A GP but and it it can handle anything. It could take trucks. It could take uh tractors, cars, cars of any size. Anything can be on a CPU. It\'s a very generalpurpose highway. A GPU on the other hand does only one certain type of calculation, but it does millions of them at the same time. So when you are calculating the all these weights and the biases in the neural network, you need this massive parallel processing. And one of the big barriers to entry of being a somebody who creates foundational models is having the GPU resources to be able to train the model and calculate all these weights and biases. Now in 2018 everything changed. Now, I wish that we would have Joe and I would have known about this paper in 2018 because in 2021, we were taking a threemonth class at MIT that was completely made obsolete by this 2018 paper. We had absolutely no idea that everything that we were learning about data science was completely obsolete already. uh but nobody had released Chad GBT. So the transformer model is basically the model that all large language models are based on. Now it doesn\'t matter whether it\'s open AIS with Ch