(69, 26, 'Pro Live - 7/27/25', 'This is the replay of the Pro Live from 7/27/25', '', 'text', 90, 8, 'published', 1, '', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-28 23:40:53', '2025-08-11 21:13:47', NULL, NULL),
(70, 9, 'Brief History of AI', 'In this presentation I discuss a brief history of how we got to where we are in AI.', '', 'text', 15, 1, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/-ks0yIIuMnc', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-29 00:12:04', '2025-07-29 00:12:05', NULL, 'hey everybody welcome so this section is called the brief history of an artificial intelligence you\'re probably wondering yourself why is Ed focusing on history i\'m not here to learn history i want to learn how to do AI i want to learn how to do machine learning i want to build things i want to build agents i want to build tools what does history matter well the reality is artificial intelligence is all about context and if we I\'m going to I want to go through the history of artificial intelligence to tell you how it was so important that about 5 years ago there was a white paper written I guess actually I guess it was longer than it was about 2018 but it was more like 5 years ago that it became really important and people realized what to do with it and there was a paper called attention is all you need and attention is basically context and what it was is it was a way to determine what something means and then that is where this whole AI I thing kicks off of so what we really want to talk about is what is artificial intelligence and then why does the history matter and the history matters because when you\'re working on projects and you run into problems or there\'s something that you want to do and you don\'t know how to do it it\'s the context of what you\'re trying to do through the whole scope of artificial intelligence that\'s really important okay so you\'re going to run into problems there\'s going to be issues and there might be something that\'s in this history that says \"Oh well now I understand why this was important.\" So let me talk a little bit about why it\'s important as far as context goes so let\'s have a sentence and the sentence is uh I swam across the river to get to the other bank now let\'s look at the word bank okay now if you just take that word specifically it could mean a financial institution and in English it\'s most likely going to mean a financial institution if you say the word bank 90% of the time it means financial institution but when you put the words in front of it I swam across the river those other words tell you what the meaning of the word bank means so I swam across the river to get to the other bank bank alone means nothing but bank with swam and river pretty much tells you what the word bank means and that\'s why it\'s so important to know as much as you can about the foundations of artificial intelligence how we got there and then that way when you run into a problem that problem you\'re going to be able to see it in more wider context it\'s really hard to just dive in the middle of something and then think you\'re going to understand why something is the case because a lot of cases it\'s not just um what you do it\'s why you\'re doing it so the ancient uh roots of artificial intelligence really come back to understanding how the world works okay now if you were in it for any time in the last 40 years everything was binary there was a certain way we represented things inside of computers and it was with ones and zeros it\'s ons and offs is the voltage high or is the voltage low now that is a way to represent things and you can represent a lot of the world by representing it in ones and zeros but in reality the world is represented as a as neural networks and the interesting thing about math is that the entire world can be described with math now not by me not by most people and more and more people are learning that even how your brain works there\'s mathematical formulas that represent how your brain works math is a representative uh it represents how something works so going back all the way to Greek mythology everybody was trying to figure out how things worked and because of the technologies at the time they had a little trouble figuring out how things work and one thing I need to remind everybody is you know 5,000 years 10,000 years ago the people were just as smart as we are today they had as much ability to do intellect they can as much ability to figure things out but they didn\'t have the history and they didn\'t have the context and they didn\'t have the fundamental building blocks to know how to do these things so if we go back all the way to 1936 there was a thing called the Turing machine okay from Alan Touring and I\'m not going to go too far into detail with it but basically it was the first time we tried to model uh the way things work around something other than binary ons and offs yes and nos so in 1950 there was a thing called the Turing test and that was where there was this idea that machines could think now again we are 75 years later and machines still aren\'t thinking now they\'re looking like they\'re thinking but they\'re really not thinking so in 1956 at at a Dartmouth con conference AI was born so these three guys these McCarthy Minsky Simon and Newell they came up with this idea for artificial intelligence now the problem was back in the 1950s we didn\'t have computers and we sure didn\'t have GPUs so the idea was there the math was there they were starting to see how the math pulled together but it really didn\'t you know there was nothing for them to do because you didn\'t even have computers okay back then so um from from the 1950s to the 1960s there was all this interest in what\'s now artificial intelligence but the technology wasn\'t there so when you got to the 1970s and the 1980s you got to this thing called uh the AI winter and that\'s just when mathematicians were off doing their things but they were doing basic research nobody was really worrying about artificial intelligence and for about 20 years nothing really happened there was no funding for it u there was huge limitations in the way they were going about things like with everything if you take the wrong approach to something you you might end up with a problem so there was a lot of issues from 1970s to 1980s which meant artificial intelligence kind of went away you didn\'t hear about it but then we got into the 1990s and the there was this move from rules-based to statistical approaches now a rules-based approach is if this then this or uh a switch statement this or this or this or this it\'s a very binary it\'s a rules you just basically you programmed a computer by giving it a series of rules and you teached it a a bunch of rules and if it you know and it just was a decision it just said if it\'s this then we\'re going to go this if it\'s this and then you had math and so u in the 1990s people started to say well you know what statistics are are really interesting and now we\'re starting to get the computers that can do the kind of math that is with statistics now when I was in college computers couldn\'t really do anything with statistics and it was all done by hand so a lot of the math I learned in college was like you either did it by hand or it didn\'t get done so you got to the 90s and the 2000s all of a sudden you could start to rep um represent the statistics and the math behind things so uh in 1997 and I remember this IBM\'s uh Deep Blue bait Casper off in a game of chess doesn\'t seem like much now because computers beat people in chess all the time but back then it was a huge deal okay and so and I was actually working on a project with IBM at the time and it was the big it was the big news okay everybody wanted the computer that could play chess so over the 1990s and the 2000s you had this growth of data and computational power and so you got into things like decision trees SVMs and ensemble learning so you basically were getting to the point where you were starting to use statistical approaches in computers now in 2012 there was a thing called AlexNet and it was image recognition and it was the first time a computer could look at a picture and figure out what was in the picture now the way it did it now we would call that ancient history we wouldn\'t do it today but the fact that it could be done told everybody hey we we we\'re on to something here if this works there\'s a lot of other things we can do so in the two in the 2010s all of a sudden GPUs became a big thing and we\'re gonna I\'m gonna give a little credit to cryptocurrency there because you you got into this whole mining and GPUs the idea of using a GPU for for something other than graphics power on you know displaying graphics on your screen suddenly became a thing so in the 2010s all of a sudden there\'s GPUs and then in 2016 there was something called Alph Go and if you\'re not familiar with the the game go it is way it is an order of magnitude more complicated than chess when it comes to the number of possible moves cuz up until this point you could play chess through brute force you could basically say this is what the this is what the board looks like right now let\'s play through every possible combination and come up with what\'s the best next move based on just playing the game millions of times alph Go with Go you can\'t do that so the fact that Alph Go beat um the Go the Go champion was a huge deal because you couldn\'t just do that through brute force so throughout the two the 2010s AI was powering speech recognition vision and we started getting into the self-driving tech now the biggest thing was Transformers in 2017 now it\'s funny because I was taking courses in data science in 2021 and nobody had heard of the paper uh that was the big seismic shift like we were talking about convolutional neural networks decision trees all these other things but we were not talking about transformers so in all of a sudden there was a GPT2 which was a hallucination central but it proved that things could be done and it moved all the way in the 2020s from GPT2 to GPT4 now you have claude you have gemini and it all goes back to this paper called attention is all you need and it\'s where the transformer model uh came about and so at one point you would hear people say everything now is a transformer former so what transformers can do text images code generative AI it\'s all because of this transformer model and this one paper so consider yourself lucky to be starting artificial intelligence now because if you were doing it in the late 201s you were learning about a whole bunch of stuff that\'s not all that important right now or it\'s kind of nice to know in the history but I\'m not really going into any detail in it because it\'s just kind of reference material it\'s not stuff that you absolutely need to know so now we\'re talk now I\'m going to just talk about some of the fluffy stuff that I got to throw in here because you know there\'s a lot of people watching this you know there are things with artificial intelligence that we have to take into account okay bias and fairness is your large language model bias against certain people is it fair now one of the things I\'m starting to see is that as the model gets bigger it gets pulled towards fairness it can\'t the more it knows the less it has bias and even when you\'re trying to put bias in larger models it\'s hard to do because the models are trained on so much data it just kind of pulls it into fairness and then explainability and hallucinations now I ran into explanability in the early 2020s where I had models that were doing things and then people were saying \"Well we\'re making million-dollar decisions based on the what this model is saying explain to me how it works.\" And you say \"I can\'t explain it because it\'s a black box it\'s AI it\'s doing it inside of the model how am I possibly going to know how this works?\" And so you couldn\'t explain the answer well simultaneously AI is generating these wild hallucinations and and the way the probability of the models work you could get these crazy hallucinations coming out of it where the model was just making stuff up so the combination of explanability and hallucinations were a real problem you had you couldn\'t explain what the model was doing and it was doing stuff blatantly wrong sometime then you have things called alignment and control you know every society wants the models to be aligned to their social values and uh so you see different things you ask different models different questions and I\'m not going to say what\'s right or what\'s wrong but if you ask some international models different things than US models you get different answers or there\'s a heck of a lot of history on some subjects in one part of the world and there\'s other history about these subjects in the other parts of the world so alignment in control is going to kind of be a big issue and then we get into the regulatory and the ethical debates and that\'s this is going to be hugely problematic in the future because the more AI can do things and the more AI becomes accessible to the public you\'re really going to you\'re going to need some regulations but who\'s doing the regulating and I know in the United States Congress knows nothing about computers so you you start out with the fact that hey I got to tell you what a computer is and then and I got to teach you about it so that you can tell me what we\'re allowed to do or not so there\'s going to be some huge issues with that in the future now again I wanted to go through this history and you\'ll start to understand in the future when I start talking about other things why this is important so just take all this in if you need to watch it a couple times if you can go through it a couple times maybe a 2x speed uh just to remember that there is a history that started artificial intelligence and then we\'ll move on to the the next topic in the next section');
