(71, 9, 'Types of Artificial Intelligence', 'There are many different type of AI.  In this video I discuss the different types and categories of Artificial Intelligence.', '', 'text', 12, 2, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/6kOmiNcjiBU', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-29 00:15:03', '2025-07-29 00:17:47', NULL, 'hey everybody welcome so in this section we\'re going to go through uh AI foundations and uh and a lot of background in artificial intelligence and what I\'m basically doing in the next 15 minutes and trying to distill everything I\'ve learned since I graduated college related to AI into 15 minutes so we can refer to it later now this is where a lot of the reinforcement learning is going to come in for the technique I\'m trying to experiment with for on how to teach you AI faster than any college can ever do it so this is going to be an interesting uh this is going to be an interesting section but what we\'re talking about here is we\'re going to talk about the different types and techniques of artificial intelligence and kind of how they evolved over time so I\'m going to introduce a couple of things that were really big a while ago um and when I say a while I mean like five years ago and that are obsolete today but you still may run into them one of the things you have to understand is there are a lot of people in artificial intelligence who\'ve dedicated their lives going down a certain path only to find out in 2021 2022 that that path was the wrong path so you do run into a lot of people especially if you work in a science community of people who are grasping old technologies so I want to introduce those old technologies so you\'ll have context and hey if I had to spend a ton of time learning this crap you might as well have to sit through some of it so we are going to talk about um what is artificial intelligence and why we category categorize AI models so there have been different models that have evolved over time think of AI as as constantly moving and with anything in technology in general you come up with an idea somebody releases it the masses jump on on board with it and then they point out everything that\'s wrong with it okay so you do this great work you release this AI model you release chat GPT 3.5 the masses get a hold of it and within six months every problem it has has been defined and then people start fixing those problems and then you evolve and sometimes the fixes of those problems introduce new problems and then you fix those new problems and you\'re kind of playing whack-a-ole where you\'re just always searching for the solution so we\'re going to go through how we got to where we are and so you can understand because a lot of times people look at things and go why the heck are you doing it that way and it\'s a historical reason for doing it that way now the other thing is is I am not going to bring up the math now when I took my one graduate level uh and I won\'t go with the three-digit uh college that offered it um in Massachusetts um it was all math okay it was crazy math it was It was math to intimidate you and math to punish you it was basically we had 1500 people in a program and you better know your math okay so my chief technology officer Joe and myself we took this thing and I will say I think he was in the top 10 and I was in the top 15 but it was brutal we\'ll never do that again and I\'m here to spare you from that so let\'s just look at the highlevel artificial intelligence model categories so the first one is basically everything that we had up until like 2020 which is um rulebased and and I got that a little bit wrong because convolutional neural networks and the the timing of things we you\'ll you\'ll understand where I\'m going with this rule-based reasoning systems it\'s a set of rules and it\'s really based on data science and you create these rules and you follow the rules and they basically have rule-based reasoning if I did this and I did this then I did this it\'s kind of that stuff that they they teach you like in grade school if so and so is so and so\'s mother who was so and so it\'s like basic logic okay that\'s symbolic artificial intelligence it\'s rules-based reasoning and these are called expert systems now we go to something called machine learning and machine learning is where it\'s really getting fun now think of your brain and your brain has neurons and synapses okay the neuron let\'s just say your neuron has got a piece of electricity between zero and one and you have 100 trillion of those and they\'re interconnected with each other with synapses and think of synapses as little resistors okay so the amount of electricity that can go from one neuron to the next neuron is regulated by this thing called a syninnapse and there is a mathematical formula that rep represents that and you you\'re it it\'s in layers okay so all of these synapses and neurons are all interconnected and you model that in a computer and then you start showing the computer things and what it is and that\'s called machine learning so what you do is you take a big set of data you take like 10% of it you set it off to the side and then you just keep teaching and teaching and teaching and then you take that 10% back and you go well how well did I do i didn\'t do that good let\'s do some more and that\'s called machine learning and it\'s called and you\'re doing basically you\'re learning how the neurons in the synapses and in this case they\'re called weights and biases how they\'re interrelated to each other by just feeding it data feeding it data and you keep trying new things and it\'s a lot of trial and error with some mathematics from calculus and linear algebra that lets you adjust the weights and the biases until it learns things and that is machine learning now there\'s a lot of intricacies to machine learning and it\'s you\'re going to be really happy that you did not have to go through uh all the math and all the different trial and errors that happened with machine learning and pattern-based matching to get to where you are and so that is basic machine learning and then we get into neural networks and represent representation learning now machine learning is still neural networks okay um but it\'s learning the patterns based on just data deep learning is much it\'s deeper i guess that\'s the right word to use and you\'re basically this is everything today that you see is really based on deep learning so if we look at these things called neural networks and and that\'s what I started to explain it\'s kind of a it\'s kind of a computer representation of basically how your brain works i mean we\'re finding out more and more that it\'s not exactly this way but you have an input so let\'s say your eyes are your input okay then but you see something it goes into your brain and now there\'s layers okay and there\'s an there\'s an input there\'s layers and then your final result let\'s say moving my hand or moving to the left so I get an input we have layers of these neurons and synapses that determine something and then I have an output so you have uh input layer hidden layers that are doing the the math and then output layers and so that\'s the basic structure of a neural network and there\'s a lot of different kinds of neural network what I just described is called a feed forward neural network it comes in and it moves forward and then it goes to the output and that\'s uh in the history it used to be called a multi-layer perceptron because it\'s you\'re perceiving something and then it\'s going through multi-layers okay so that\'s the structure the basic structure of a neural network now to train the neural network you have this thing called gradient descent and it\'s basically it\'s back propagation so what you do is let\'s say I\'m teaching you to recognize a number so I show you a one and I just guess that it\'s a six and then it comes back and says \"Nope that\'s not a one that\'s not a six.\" So it readjusts all my weights and my biases and then it tries with another number and it will just keep doing that over and over and over again and part of this to in the math are these things called activation functions because in computers things are ones and zeros but in your brain it\'s 0ero to one okay it\'s the one being the most amount of electricity in your neuron zero being the least amount of electricity in your neuron so there\'s something called activation functions and different activation functions are better at training different types of models and that\'s as far as I\'m going to go into it i mean I will tell you we spend a whole lot of time on Rayu Sigma and Tanh as to when you would use each one this is not the this is not the place for that so then what you get with the use case of this is basic classification and regression so let\'s say classification is this a number one or is this a number six i\'m basically taking something in and I\'m determining what it is and that\'s what we\'re going to do basically with a neural network now one of the first really popular really important neural networks was called the convolutional neural network and if you\'ve gone into a hospital any time in the last 15 years and you\'ve had either an MRI or a CT scan there used to be a time when they would take this picture they would send it off to a room full of doctors and they would actually look for things well a convolutional neural network is image recognition and it uses something called the convolution which is it basically scans and goes through it and it does it at different levels and it\'s it\'s a really cool technique but what it got good at is training models to learn things okay training models that there\'s a carcinoma in there and now when you use a convolutional neural network and this you can actually determine a whole bunch of stuff you weren\'t even looking for so within seconds you can put an MRI or a CT scan in and it\'ll determine everything that you need to know so the use cases for this are object detection and image classification is there a cat in this picture yes and the convolutional neural network is what is good at doing that so convolutional neural networks are good at taking something at a point in time and determining what\'s in it well now what one what happens when we want to predict the future predict the next word or predict is the stock price going to go up or is the stock price going to go down that\'s called an RNN that\'s a recurrent neural network that is related to processing data sequentially we have a one we have a two we have a three okay the next number is going to be a four and now recurrent neural networks because they\'re timebased there\'s some real limitations to them because the as things get more complicated you need to be able to look further back in the past to help detect what the future\'s going to be and as you\'re doing more and more complicated things with rec recurrent neural networks you ran into this thing called a a vanishing gradient problem which basically would mean the recurrent neural network would get confused and it couldn\'t predict the next number so in order to fix things now let\'s keep going back to our process the way AI has always worked is we come up with something it\'s really cool we see a bunch of limitations then everybody jumps through hoops to try to fix those limitations well the because of the vanishing gradient problem they came up with something called called long shortterm memory which is basically enhancing the RNN to have more memory so it can see further back okay and then the other thing was GRU and of course I\'m not remembering what GRU is right now it GRU was a was an enhancement it was a simpler method of long shortterm memory it related to long short-term memory but it was just a simpler way of doing it so what we were doing here is RNN\'s had a problem let\'s fix it with LSTMs and GRUs then we get to the next thing which is natural language processing and that\'s really the precursor to large language models we\'re talking about text now we\'re talking about figuring out what something means now if you go back to the original translation uh programs they were great because all they basically did is word for word you translated the one word the next word the next word well the problem was the sentence structure from one language to another is different we have a very weird uh sentence structure in English compared to like the Slavic languages or the Asian languages so you can\'t just take word for word and say I\'m going to the the word bank for instance uh it it could mean a the shore of a river it could mean a financial institution so natural language processing was using um artificial intelligence to basically turn words into numbers and then have mathematical ways to determine what something means based on the sentence structure and that\'s where things like word embeddings called word devec and glove came about and then you had these traditional versus uh neural NLP models so natural language project uh processing was again that progression we had RNN\'s now we\'re going into NLP now everything changed in 2017 when this paper came out of course it changed in 2017 but we didn\'t really know about it until chat GPT35 came out or ChatGPT3 came out because they\'re all working off working on things and this is from a paper called attention is all you need and that is where you created they created this thing called an attention mechanism and that\'s where you look at each word and you look at the words around it and based on not just the word itself but what the other words around it mean is what does that word mean and that\'s how you get the context or the meaning of something and when we talk about the context window that\'s the number of words or tokens that the attention mechanism is looking at so when you hear the early uh the early GPTs or the transformer models had like 2,000 tokens that would be the number of tokens that it looked at for each token now we\'re talking about uh LLMs that have a 10 million token context window that means each word is being compared to the other 10 million tokens in the context window that\'s why it\'s not always best to make your to give the context window a ton of stuff in your prompting because if you have a 10 million token context window every token is going to get analyzed against every other token so you know if you\'re going against two that\'s one thing if you\'re going against four that\'s another if you\'re going against 10 million that\'s a lot of math so this led into everything we\'re doing today which is the encoder decoder architecture where you\'re taking a sentence and then you\'re encoding it into these vectors into these um basically that\'s what it is it\'s a vector is a list of numbers so the transformer model is what most large language models and most artificial intelligence that we\'re talking about in 2025 you know is based on so that is your um your model that you\'re off off of so convolutional neural networks it\'s still got a specific use case rnn\'s not so much nlp people are still trying to use it but for the most part the transformer model is the model that you need to know now how do you train this and this is where reinforcement learning comes in so if we look at the way we build a model so they what they do is they go out to they do this thing called pre-training and you just take as much stuff as you can get and you encode it and you do the you put the attention mechanisms in and what you\'re basically doing is you\'re teaching the model the basics the this is a cat this is a dog a dog\'s kind of like a cat you\'re just you\'re you\'re training it in all of the basics but what happens is that it doesn\'t mean that the model can do anything it just knows a lot of stuff now where reinforcement learning comes in is that\'s where it\'s training the model to do stuff it\'s training the model of the interactions between this thing so it\'s basically it you\'ve got an agent okay and this agent is teaching the model something and then it\'s giving it a reward based on how good of a job it did so initially for chat GPT35 it was reinforcement learning through human feedback they had rooms full of people they would ask GPT a question gpt would give it an answer and it would say no that\'s not a good answer try again okay that\'s a better answer and you would just keep doing it until it got better and it was basically a reward structure and so over time the model would get better because you would keep asking it questions and then telling it was it good or bad and it would use that to learn and improve itself now that\'s done through agents okay that\'s done through technology so it\'s basically playing a game and that\'s kind of how I\'m teaching in this course is that we\'re playing a game the if you get it right you get a point if you don\'t get it right you lose a point so it is a reward state thing so you\'re basically teaching the model to get better at things by teaching it by making it try more and more and more basically the more reinforcement learning the better the model is and that\'s where it comes into that\'s where models get really expensive because you\'re spending so much time doing this reinforcement learning so that it\'s good at everything okay so now let\'s just talk about this all in um in a in a review so AI starts with neural networks and that is basically layers and weights and biases so when you talk about training you\'re talking about determining what the right weights and biases are between basically in your brain it would be this the neurons in the synapses so when you\'re training you\'re using this technique called back propagation where you say this is what we did was it good was it not if it wasn\'t good you adjust the weights and biases a little bit and then you try it again and you just keep doing that over and over and over excuse me so a neural network is feed forward so the the perception comes in it goes through the different layers and then at the end you have an output and that\'s usually a classification so is this a number one or is this a number two based on what it sees it your neurons and your synapses the neural network basically classifies cnn\'s are convolutional neural networks and those are called convolutional layers and that\'s where it may look at a 3x3 then a 6x6 and then it looks at things and that\'s really good for vision tasks next we have RNNs so we talked about detecting things now we talk now we\'re talking about predicting things so what\'s the next word so that is where recurrent neural networks and long short-term memory came in so you may still hear about that but they\'re sequence models but that\'s really the the precursor to natural language processing which is the basics of transformers where you\'re talking about embedding transformers chat bots and translations and then we get to reinforcement learning which is where we are today so every time a new model comes out it goes through some base training tasks and then it goes through reinforcement learning and the difference between models is how the reinforcement learning is done so a reasoning model has reinforcement learning done completely different than a non-reasoning model because in this case you\'re telling the model that it you want it to stop and think and you want to stop and evaluate then you get into things like mixture of experts where instead of using the whole model for every query you classify and you say well I\'m talking about chemistry it\'s like going on the college campus and saying \"Well we\'re going to talk about chemistry so let\'s go to the chemistry building.\" This is where models change from version to version number of parameters to number of parameters so you always think like in your brain the bigger brain is better than the smaller brain but a smaller brain can learn a very specific task very very well so this is basically your foundation your classifications these are going to be core topics that you\'re going to want to learn because you\'re going to run into them no matter what you\'re doing and knowing this background is really important when you run into issues or something new comes out and you\'re wondering why it works the way it does so this brings us to where we are today we have our basic foundation models like GPT and Claude and Gemini we have our multimodal which is including language video and a audio and then obviously we\'re just going to keep going back to this ethics and explanability thing but the the models right now are really based into two categories they\'re the foundation models which are textbased and then the multimodal and these are the models that can look at a picture see things tell you what\'s in the picture and draw pictures so uh I ran a little bit long i wanted this to be 15 minutes it\'s going to end up being a little over 20 but I think this this information is really important for you and then when we when we get into our reinforcement learning you\'re going to see these topics and you\'re going to see why why they\'re they\'re actually important'),
