(83, 34, 'First Live Call - 7/6/2025', 'Our first live call in the Free community have over 600 participants.', '', 'text', 60, 99, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/23RBl-v8vdQ', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-29 02:14:05', '2025-07-31 19:53:15', NULL, 'and you are live at all. Hey everybody, welcome. So, uh this is uh another introduction call, but we\'re going to do a lot more stuff that\'s actually technical today. I want to start going through things. Um but most importantly, if anybody hasn\'t uh wasn\'t on last week\'s call, I\'m at Honor. I I\'m the founder of the community. You probably found me through Instagram. Uh I\'m going to go through the introduction part really fast. I you know I\'ve been doing uh tech work since 1986. I graduated from the University of Illinois in 1986. Took me two years to get a job. So I very much understand what the technical environment is today. I\'m running into a lot of either uh computer science majors who are coming out of college and obviously with artificial intelligence being more uh recent than their freshman year, it\'s really hard for anybody to be keeping up with it. So my background is as a consultant and I do consulting today which is um one of the reasons I\'m really good at identifying what\'s going wrong in the artificial intelligence industry because anytime you have you\'re doing projects with large organizations and the project is going to span six months to a year but the technologies are going to change six times during the uh during the project you just run into all sorts of project management issues. Now, um, so the reasons that we created the community and we think that it\'s really important to have a community where we can share ideas is a we have the economic parallels to what I ran into in 1988 when I got out of college and couldn\'t find a job. And basically, the only place you could learn what you really needed to know was on the job. So, if the economy wasn\'t perfect and they weren\'t hiring graduates, um, there wasn\'t really any way to learn things. So, we have the economic parallels to what I was running into when I first got out of college. And then we have the technology parallels to what it was like, you know, between 1998 and 2001 when the web was taking off. It was changing really fast. Nobody knew what to do. Everybody was getting funded. Now, if you, you know, it\'s kind of the same thing is going on today when it comes to funding. You know, you had all these people, all these projects that were getting funded, but really nobody knew how to deliver them. And then when there was the big.com crash, you got this big consolidation into, you know, what a lot of what you\'re seeing today. And so, you know, colleges are great and you learn a lot of really good background information. And because when I was at the University of Illinois, we were part of the uh liberal arts department. Uh they were telling us that we\'re not teaching you to have a job, we\'re teaching you how to learn so that when you do have a job, they can teach you. That is actually really wise. At the time I\'m surpris you know at the time I was kind of annoyed because it was really making it hard to get a job. The fact that we were using technologies in co college that nobody was using themselves. But what we came up with is we came up with a different learning approach and our learning approach is ba based on really two sides. You know the first of all is everybody needs to know the basics. Now, you can go and you can learn how to do NADN, you can learn cursor, you can learn claude code, and you, you know, you can learn vibe coding, and you could probably build some really nice stuff, but when you go out to sell it, if you can\'t have a conversation with your client, or you\'re always going to run into these roadblocks. There\'s always a guy in the security department. There\'s always a guy in the infrastructure who wants to have an argument with you over the fact that you\'re using let\'s say you\'re using Claude and they think you should be using Grock and you know you just you need to know the basics. You need to be able to talk to talk in order to walk the walk when you go in. Whether you\'re going in as a client, you know, or you\'re whether you\'re talking to clients, whether you\'re talking to you\'re selling a product, you\'re going to run into somebody who\'s going to challenge you on your basic knowledge or if you\'re if you\'re the one in your organization bringing an artificial intelligence and you\'re going to your manager and they\'re they ask you random questions about um buzzword bingo. I mean, buzzword bingo in artificial intelligence is a game that everybody\'s playing. You go to I\'ve been to parties where I\'ve heard more buzzwords in a single sentence than anybody can actually believe. So, part of what we\'re doing is we\'re teaching you buzzword bingo. You know what what we did is we created an an an application and I\'ll jump over to it now. About 50 of you have definitely already played with it. Um, let me get this out of my way. Um, all right. I gotta play a little bit around here because the Zoom controls make it really hard for me to find my my page. Okay. So, we create a we created a learning um a reinforcement learning module where as you\'re going through the different parts of the course, you can do these practice sessions where let\'s say I\'m going to go in and I\'m going to pick um augmented AI agents. I go in and do start a practice. Hold on. My mouse. Let me do it this way. All right. So, basically, it just starts asking you questions and every time you get a question wrong, you lose a half a point. Every time you get one right, you get a point. And so the reason for this is basically and I\'m guessing which is really going to keep me off the leaderboard. Um the reason for this is just being reinforcement learning. The way reinforcement learning works for a large language model is you keep drilling it over and over again with information until it learns the information. And so because gamification is such a big deal with the way people are learning today, we have a leaderboard. Let\'s look at the leaderboard. Okay, Alexandra is winning so far. She\'s got 607 correct, 35 wrong. But what happens as you\'re doing this is it\'s kind of a flashc card thing and it allows you to know when somebody throws terms at you what those terms mean. And our reason for doing this is really there\'s just so many topics that there\'s so many topics in artificial intelligence and there\'s so many buzzwords and people just throw things out at you randomly. You know, I\'m using this SDK or whatever. I\'m doing this thing. Being able to just have that background information is just a way to um you know whether you\'re selling your product or you\'re selling to your client you\'re you\'re you\'re trying to convince somebody to do a project your way this is just the background information we want to do. So our learning approach is based on that. And then the other part is that live is always better than video. Um, you know, I don\'t know, we\'ve got um 221 participants right now who have identified that they could go to YouTube and probably get a lot of the same information that I\'m going to do uh here. But live really it forces you to think in real time and it\'s really better. It\'s I think it\'s just a better way of learning. And now I\'ve got a big X over here of the our MIT professional education certificate that Joe and I did back in 2021. This was a this was a threemonth MIT professional education thing that I think it took years off my life. That is when I learned how much uh I am a terrible student. And uh Joe was in college much more recently than me. So he did a he did a lot better. I think overall he was like number eight in the class and I was number 15 but there were 1,500 people in the class and it was a lot of what we\'re doing in these communities is from what we learned in those communities. We learned so much math of on and I\'ve never used it since. And so we\'re really focusing a lot more on making sure that you\'re learning stuff that\'s relevant because you don\'t have time to learn linear algebra, gradient descent. You don\'t you just don\'t have uh the time actually to learn all those things. So you know, our focus is everybody needs to know the basics. We want to come up with some tools and we\'re we\'re going to come up with more and more tools for how to learn, you know, how to get the reinforcement of this information. Um, and then live is always better. Now, we\'re also going to be doing, and this is more for the professional community, and I I caught nothing but grief last week for not having the URL to the pro community on uh on this screen. So, um, there it is. Uh now what we\'re doing is individual and group projects. We want people to be able to work independently because every project I\'m currently working on in artificial intelligence is technically a group project but it\'s actually Hold on. There we go. I muted him twice. All right. Um so so what we\'re doing and this is a lot of what we\'re doing over on the professional community is we have a bunch of projects that we\'re working on and we\'re having a bunch we have a bunch of projects that our um clients are bringing us our investment bankers are bringing us that um we just don\'t have time to do and so we\'re going to toss them into the community see if they can you know see who can do them give you the access to additional resources in the pro community if you need access to a virtual machine. We actually have our own data center where we have we can build VMs. We have access to GPUs and we\'re really doing a lot of stuff to build your resume. And that\'s really our learning approach is we only want to teach things or we really want to cover things that are going to help you in your career because this is a very very competitive space right now. I don\'t know. I\'ve made a couple of reels lately about the fact that Meta has been poaching um resources for $und00 million. So Meta went out and they offered 14 people $100 million to uh join Meta. Now um that is because there is a lack of resources in this space. Now it\'s also because those are all people who could get foundational models funded. If you know if any of those 14 people went to the investment bankers I know and said uh we\'ve got this idea and it could basically be an idea for anything they\'d get a hundred million $200 million in funding just by showing up. So you know it\'s a very very competitive market right now and you know it\'s there\'s a lot of fear uncertainty and doubt. So we\'re we\'re doing everything we can to take away that fear, uncertainty and doubt. So our road map is basically learning the background theory, learning what transformer models are, what large language models are, learning all that background information so that you can talk the talk and then from there the AI the the basic AI concepts which I don\'t like the list I have here. I have vibe coding, prompt engineering, context engineering and local models. That is really context engineering is going to be it looks like context engineering is going to be the title for software engineers going forward. They are going to be context engineers or software context engineers. And that is basically because vibe coding has gotten to the point where it is actually credible. And I\'m going to finish this presentation today talking about cloud claude code and how to get started with both claude code and Gemini CLI because when you start working with those tools that\'s when the epiphany kicks in that coding with AI is real. So you know at first I I was one of the first people to install GitHub C-Pilot and I absolutely hated it and I uninstalled it. Then I did a lot of coding where I would copy and paste my code into chat GPT and that was a horrible way to work. And then I brought got into cursor which is basically uh VS code wrapped around LLMs and that was pretty good. That was really good. But then when cursor then when claude came up or anthropic came up with claude code that just came to a whole new level because every complaint I had about cursor was fixed in claude code and it actually has completely changed the way I approach vibe coding when I\'m looking at um how I want to do a project. So that um reinforcement learning module that we had that was 100% vibecoded. Joe and I decided we were not going to write a single line of code in that ourselves. We were going to let cursor do the whole thing and most that was mostly done with the cursor free version. So one of the projects we want to do is we want to say all right let\'s start this project over let\'s rewrite it but let\'s plan it out properly. Let\'s use cloud code and you you\'ll see exactly how everything goes. Now once you\'ve explored the basic AI concepts you everybody should pick a focus but I there\'s a limited number of focuses at this point the it\'s really are you going to build things or are you going to manage the building of things so let me just continue when we\'re talking about the the projects that we\'re currently working on working on projects break can really be broken into two parts and hold on one Second, I have a ser I have a server behind me that uh decided it wanted to wake up. So, when we\'re talking about projects, they\'re really coming down into two categories. Building AI products and tools or building with AI. Now, mostly people come to me with tools that they want where they think AI is an integral part of the the final application, but really it\'s it\'s not it it\'s just in the building with AI series, it\'s more if you\'re if you\'re doing a SAS project and you want to build it out, build it out using AI. Uh we\'ve got projects that we\'ve been working on the healthcare and financial markets. Um a lot with healthcare. We\'ve been doing a lot with local large language models in health care because of the HIPPA requirements that you can\'t share patient data in the United States. We\'ve been doing stuff with financial markets because we have a good background in data science. So, predictive analytics in the financial markets and then in the health care markets um really doing a lot with retrieval augmented generation. We\'ve been working on longevity um and then also data and data warehousing projects where we\'re building things to access Oracle databases uh document databases and then a lot with the knowledge graphs and um nodes and edges and all of that stuff. So if we\'re talking about building AI project products to me the biggest area right now is building MCP tools. Now, if you\'ve ever built an MCP tool and that is model context protocol with model context protocol tools, what they are is they give large language models the ability to use tools. So, if you install claw desktop on one of your users machines and you build MCP tools that can access local data, can access their email, access their SharePoint, um use local APIs maybe to get to your Oracle database or your DB2 database. Um, MCP tools are what give large language models the power to do things. And why I think that is such a growing area is large organizations do not have their data on in the cloud because they never shared their data. Their data has always been proprietary. But it doesn\'t do them a lot of good. Let\'s say let\'s pick pharmaceutical for instance. Well, the large language model up in the cloud has a lot of background information about pharmaceuticals, but it knows nothing about the internal projects that are being worked on inside the organization. So you can build MCP tools using the um MCP Python SDK or they have a Java SDK, but you can build these tools that allow the large language model to go out and get that information in real time. And that is absolutely powerful. It\'s almost like a magic trick when you show that to clients when you you know I have one that I built where I just access the um the SEC Edgar database and you can ask real time it questions about filings that are filings that actually happen today because you\'re actually reaching out using the tool to the large to uh d to an external database. So MCP tools I think are the biggest opportunity for people in or large organizations or people who sell to large organizations to really be effective and efficient and and if if making money is your goal, MCP tools are a place to go. The second thing is automations and I talk about automations because there are 200 communities on school for building automations with NADN and automations are great. Again, it\'s like a magic trick. When you show somebody an automation, they are usually wowed, but there I I feel like automations right now are very premature. You can do things that are great, but what happens is um you\'re building them with tools that may or may not be around in a couple of years. And that is a Hold on a second. Something\'s beeping. Um that is a um that is an area that is great. We should all be looking at building automations. The question is the jury I think is still out on how. Now what we\'re doing a lot with claude code and cursor is we\'re building automations in those frameworks. So we\'re not using the visual frameworks to build automations. Now make and naden are very very powerful and and people really like them but I\'m really having trouble finding companies that are willing to deploy automations with NAN or with make. So that\'s a second area and then APIs and SDKs local AP. So when you run a model locally and you run a model um using lama or llama.cp CPP or VLLM. When you\'re running it locally, you have APIs that are basically clones of the APIs that were generated in version one of Chat GPT. So you basically just have the completions API to work with. Now the SDKs that the cloud models have are far superior to the SDKs that you have on the um Joe, can you do some muting? Um, got him. Sorry, he was off my screen. Okay, so it so the a the APIs for local models are really poor compared to the APIs for the cloud models. So there is a huge opportunity to build those APIs in the for the local model because I have clients right now who will not allow uh cloud LLMs to be used at their organizations but they have a lot of money so they are interested in having um local LLMs but like one example is named entity recognition. So if you\'re doing named entity recognition, the uh chat GPT latest version of the SDK, you can do named entity recognition in one line of code. If you\'re doing it manually through APIs, you\'re writing a lot of code. So those type of tools are really important projects and it\'s an opportunity whether you\'re building them to sell them or you\'re building them to be a consultant for an organization. building these kind of tools really get you get you grounded. So the next thing I want to talk about okay so now I want to talk about getting into you know getting into the meat and potatoes of this getting into the things that you need to have. Now, everybody needs to, you know, hardware selection is a really tough decision for a lot of people and you can basically do artificial intelligence projects working on a Chromebook all the way up to the most powerful MacBook Pro or you can also do it with Linux boxes. We\'ve got a Linux box in the other room that was making all that noise that\'s got eight GPUs on it. that\'s probably not most people\'s uh development environment when you go when you\'re looking at hardware selection. Uh my recommendation is if you can use Mac products and you want to run local LLMs, I\'m a big Apple I I have been called an Apple fanboy online very very often because I am a I am definitely somebody who uses a Mac. At the same time, I have a whole stack of Windows computers that my clients make me use because they won\'t allow a personal device on their network. So, your hardware selection, get as much RAM as you can. If you can get an Nvidia GPU, that\'s great. But for the most part, just have, you know, if you\'re on this Zoom, you probably have a pretty good a pretty good start. Now, we\'re talking about the cloud tools. Now, when you want cloud tools, you\'re going to want to have an account on HuggingFace, an account on GitHub, and if you\'re learning Python, get an account on Google Collab. I think Google Collab is just such an easy way to learn Python. And one of the things I\'m always going to recommend is to learn Python. Python is pretty easy. Most of the people that I know who are Python developers before the AI boom were data scientists. They were not developers. They were just pure data scientists who were calling who were calling Python and Google Collab is by far the easiest thing to uh learn Python on. Then we get into the desktop software which is non artificial intelligence and I have a pretty big list of things you want to get. So let\'s start with the IDE. So either cursor or VS code are the my ID my ids of IDE of choice. I am a big cursor fan. You can get away with the free version of cursor. And one of the reasons I recommend the free version of cursor is I\'m later on going to recommend claude code. And you should if you\'re going to pay for only one thing in the uh in the development space, pay for claw code. I I uh I sound like Anthropic is sponsoring me. They are not, but they are probably one of only two organizations that if they called me tomorrow to sponsor them, um them and Oracle. And the only reason Oracle\'s on the list is I\'ve been working with them since 1988. So, Anthropic is definitely um I I sometimes I feel like Anthropic reads my mind and everything that Anthropic has done I\'ve been pretty happy with. So on your desktop, you want to have everything that you need. Now, if you\'re going to be a Python developer or you\'re going to do a lot of um vibe coding or let\'s call it context engineering from now on, um having something like Anaconda or Minionda. Now, in the uh because I did not make slides with all of this list, they are all in the classroom area of the course. And after this, I\'ll do a post of all of my recommendations for the tools. So be instead of me going through all of them because I want to have as much time as possible to um talk about you to to actually do some code. Um I\'m just going to say look, you want to have everything you need on your desktop because debugging code using these tools requires you to be able to run the code on your desktop. That even includes having a LAMP stack. So I I run on Mac so I have X AMP. So I actually have an Apache, a MySQL and a PHP on my box so that when I write for the LAMP stack I\'m you know writing that uh things I can debug. Now in the last week uh Joe has not even heard this. I have suddenly become a fan of Nex.js. And the reason I became a fan of Nex.js JS is I asked Claude, \"If you\'re going to help me code, what programming languages and what frameworks should I use to get the best results in vibe coding using anthropic tools?\" And and Claude came back and said, \"ReactJS with Nex.js on the front end.\" And what Nex.js does is it supports serverside rendering of React code. And I have the vibe coding I have done with uh Nex.js has almost always worked on the first try and that I cannot say when I\'ve coded in Angular with um with vibe coding techniques. As a matter of fact using Angular I have never gotten even close to having something work on the first try and in several cases I\'ve actually had it get to the point where I\'ve had to give up. So, u we\'ve got our cloud tools, we\'ve got our nonAI desktop software, and then we\'ve got our LLM subscriptions and API keys. If you\'ve got if you\'re doing subscriptions to LLMs and you\'re getting and you\'re having paid API keys, just get them based on what it is you\'re planning to work on. So, then from here, oops. All right. All right. Now, let\'s get into a couple of uh another topic. The next topic I want to talk about now that I\'ve talked about your your hardware for your development environment is how large language models actually work. Now the important thing here is really you don\'t need to know about the transformer model. You don\'t need to know about lojits and softmax and the difference between relu and you know all these learning models. You don\'t need to know those things. What you do need to know is that there are differences from one large language model to the next. Now large language models are built on the transformer architecture which comes from the paper attention is all you need that came out I think in 2018. That paper is the model for all transformers and LLMs. Chat GPT is a generalized predictive transformer. That is their implementation of the transformer model. There are different implementations. Claude Grock, they all are implementing the transformer model, but they\'re doing it differently. So, GPT, when you call something a GPT, that is open AI\'s version of a transformer model. that is not necessarily everybody\'s um version of a transformer model. Now the first type of model is oneshot models or I\'m sorry dense models. Now with a dense model when you run inference all the parameters in the entire LLM are activating. And so that means it\'s sort of like I see something and my whole brain is going to work on solving that problem. That is not necessarily the most efficient way to go, but that was how neural networks started out. And so a lot of the LLMs that you see are dense models. And that is because they\'re they\'re basically transformer neural networks. When you ask it a question, it\'s going to use all of the parameters, whether it\'s 32 million parameters or 1.7 trillion parameters. All of the tr all of the parameters, which are the weights and the biases, are going to be used in coming up with the answer. Now, the third one down I have on this list is called mixture of experts. Mixture of experts was an innovative way to not have the models use all of the parameters. It\'s more like showing up at a college campus and the first place you go to is the reception desk who tells you what buildings you need to go to. So with the mixture of experts models, which mixtrol is one of those, it is going to use it. It takes the entire model and it breaks it up into call it departments. So it\'s like I\'m going to ask a question. It\'s going to go to something like a router and that router is going to say, \"Oh, you need chemistry and physics, go that way.\" Oh, you need uh uh human studies and something else, go that way. Now, what mixture of experts always does is it recommends two areas to go into so that they can fact check each other. Now, what makes Mixtixure of Experts models so powerful is they run much more efficiently and with much less power. So, when you\'re getting to models that have let\'s say 5 trillion parameters in them, a mixture of experts model is going to use far less electricity and you\'re going to be able to run it on far less hardware than you would if you\'re running a dense model. Because a dense model, the entire LLM has to fit into into GPU memory for you to run it. Whereas with a mixture of experts model, it\'s going to come back slower, but it\'s going to load the sections into the GPU as it needs it. Now, the second thing I have on this list are the thinking models or the reasoning models. and chat GPT40 came out to be the first and then Deep Seek came out and kind of said, \"Hey, we\'re here and you\'re you\'re we\'re here to stay.\" The thinking models are really taking over because the biggest thing about them is they eliminate hallucinations by attacking a problem from multiple steps and actually thinking through the reasoning of the answer. Now chat GPT03 is one of is their big reasoning model and then this the claude uh claude for opus is their big thinking model and the thinking models are incredibly powerful but they\'re also in they\'re slow and they are incredibly expensive to run. So that\'s why when we get to claude code I\'m going to say you do planning with one model and you do actually execution of the builds with another model. So my main reason for wanting to cover how LLMs work is not so that you understand how weights and biases work, but so that you know when you\'re doing different things, your use case is going to drive the model that you\'re going to choose. So if you\'ve got local models, meaning you\'re running them in your data center or you\'re running them on your Mac Mini, that\'s going to be a different selection process than if you\'re running a cloud model. If you\'re running a cloud model, you have to decide, do I need a thinking model or do I need a oneshot model? And those choices are going to drive your use case. Now, when you\'re making the decision between local models and cloud models, you have to realize that cloud models have a lot of extra bells and whistles built in that you probably thought were part of the model. So chat GPT40 the reason you can ask it relevant data about what\'s happening today or what happened this week is that it it has agents built into the cloud model so that these agents can actually um do things and go out and get data to include as part of the context window. So choosing the right model for the right use case actually becomes kind of an art form. So all right. So then the next thing that I want to get into is AI assisted coding. Now according to so many people the term is now context engineering. Now I remember when the term software engineer came out everybody wanted to have the title software engineer. I have a feeling prompt engineer did not go over very well because it was prompt engineering was something like I don\'t know going to trade school whereas context engineering is real engineering. Okay. So the there\'s different ways to do AI assisted coding. I started by using chat GPT and GitHub copilot. Initially, it\'s just copy your code into chat GPT, let it give you some ideas, paste that code back into your IDE, run it, and then paste the bugs back into ChatGpt. And it was just this iterative process, back and forth, back and forth, back and forth. And it was kind of a horrible way to code, but at the time, I loved it because I was writing code in areas that I had never written code before. and chat GPT was helping me. Well, then if you would have read my mind and made a list of all the complaints that I had about chat GPT for coding, cursor solved those. And what cursor basically is it is is VS Code which is open source and it\'s wrapped around large language models. And it is they do some things in there that are proprietary and you don\'t know what models they\'re calling. But it was a huge gamecher when we moved from just pasting code into Chad GPT into pasting code into ideides like cursor. Now once you got used to that you identified the next problem and the next problem was idees like cursor are very good at the task you\'re working on but they\'re terrible at seeing the big picture. If you want to write enterprise level software you have a lot of trouble when you\'re working with something like cursor because a lot of the modules have to interact with each other. you\'ve got a database design that\'s going to cross modules. If you\'re working if you\'re working with something like um if you\'re working with something like Angular where an Angular application has TypeScript, it has HTML and then it has whatever you\'re using on the back end to serve your APIs. C the ids like cursor really had trouble in you know seeing the big picture. So then all of a sudden out of nowhere cla appeared and when I learned about cla code I was watching a podcast with the developers of cla code and they were using it internally and you know it\'s kind of like when you go to any ethnic restaurant and you know that if the people of that ethnicity are eating there uh it\'s probably a good restaurant and that\'s what I saw with Claude Code. They started talking about how they were using claude code to do their internal projects and they won me over. So I I jumped into it. So the one area you want to be able to do in your career is this AI assisted coding. The next thing again is building MCP tools. These are building tools for your LLMs to use. They\'re not tools for you to use. They\'re tools that your large language model if as long as your large large language model can call tools. These are the tools you\'re going to build and you\'re going to build them using if you\'re doing Python the MCP Python SDK. Now, I used to be one of those people who I had a had an opinion on programming languages. I I hate Python, okay? When I\'m coding from scratch, I cannot stand Python. I\'m used to curly braces since the 1980s. And the fact that they replaced curly braces with indentation killed me. Okay. But when you start coding with AI assisted coding, it doesn\'t matter what language you\'re programming in. We\'re all at the same level. So the MCP Python SDK allows you to build MCP tools. They\'re very easy to build in in tools like Cursor and Claude Code. You can basically run them and test them using Claude Desktop. If what you wanted to bring to your client was a large language model that can talk to to your own private data, MCP tools are definitely the way to go. Then the next step again is automations. Now automations are automating some process. There\'s always a trigger that starts the automation. That may be you receiving an email. That might be you\'re receiving a chat. That may be those are the two I can think of. Or you might have a web hook. So something happened where there was a web hook that was fired and now you need to do something. So the automation is a stepbystep process of what you\'re going to do when let\'s say an email comes in. So an email comes in. I\'m going to open the email. I\'m going to send the email to a large language model along with a system prompt. It\'s going to come up with a response and then I\'m going to send a response or maybe I\'m going to forward the email to someone else in my department. But automations are basically these step-by-step processes. And if you\'ve never seen automations at work before, they definitely look like magic tricks. Most of my big corporate clients, if they saw automation, if I told them we what we could do with automations, they probably wouldn\'t even believe me. That\'s just how when you see automations, they\'re so surprising. And then automation, AI automations are built in two different ways. the code tools, which is what I recommend because anything you\'re ever going to put in a large organization is going to have to go through a design review. So, the idea that you\'re going to build something and make an N8N and bring it to something like McDonald\'s Corporation or JP Morgan Chase or, you know, any of these really large corporations, the noode tools are really going to be hard for you to make the argument that they should be implemented. So that\'s why I\'m always recommending learn vibe coding or context engineering in these these tools because you\'re going to need to provide so much more to your clients when you provide them automations. Now there\'s a difference between automations and agents. Now automations are like the process I just described where an email comes in, you read the email, you might look up the order information, and you send an email back. is always following the same bouncing ball. Agents on the other hand do different things depending on the input. So the LLM is what makes the decision where the agent goes. So maybe in the you\'re doing a sentiment analysis and the email comes in and you do a sentiment analysis and you determine is the writer of the email angry or is the writer of the email happy. And if the writer, if the email is angry, you\'re going to send it to a human or you\'re going to copy it to a human. But if it\'s happy, you\'re just going to write the email yourself and you\'re going to send it back. So that\'s where an agent is different than an automation because you\'ve got kind of a divergent of the process depending on um where what the LLM has determined. Now, AI automations and agents have their pros and cons. And the pros is they\'re really, really cool. The cons is getting an organization to accept that an AI agent is going to have access to its corporate databases and it is going to be able to take actions on its own. That is a really hard cell. Now, what I\'m hoping to do in this community is give everybody arguments for why you should why they should allow your automations in how you\'ve implemented security into your automations and how you\'ve created guard rails. Guardrails is one of the key topics in AI agents. Somebody had actually messaged me about some Oracle tools that they had developed that were using AI agents and the comments section talked about, yeah, we\'re not going to let you delete our Oracle database, but they were they had the vision to put in guard rails so that the guardrails were there to make sure you could never drop a table, you could never truncate a table. So, you\'re going to run into the cons of AI automations. Now, let\'s see how much time I\'ve got. Oh, perfect. All right, because this is where I really wanted to get to. I wanted to get to Claude Code because Claude Code is wild. That\'s all I can say. Let me go slideshow play from the start. Claude code fixes most of the things you run into with cursor. Now, uh hopefully some of you are old enough to remember the Matrix movies. Uh Cipher from the Matrix was the guy who could look at the screen and he could see all the green letters running down and actually see what was going on. One of the reasons you use cursor with clawed code is so that you can see what\'s going on in real time and you can see the files being changed because claude code and Gemini CLI are both command line interface tools. You can do the entire project without having an IDE, which means you could do the entire project at a Linux terminal if that\'s how you wanted to work. And on our pro community, we\'ll get you Linux terminals if that\'s how you want, if that\'s how you actually want to go about it. So, Claude Code is installed pretty easy. It\'s installed with node package manager. So, you\'re doing an npmi. If you want to install it globally, you do a minusg and it\'s just at anthropic AI cloud code. So what it is, it\'s a command line tool from Enthropic um that allows developers to delegate coding tasks to Claude directly from their terminal. Now I got that in I got that description from Claude, but the way I see it is one thing that Claude can do is it launches agents. Now this is great for anthropic because it burns tokens. It can really burn tokens. So, if you have a to-do list and you plan out your project and there\'s build 15 modules, it will it can simultaneously run off activate 15 agents and start building out the different parts of the project simultaneously. That\'s why I like using cursor because when that starts happening, you\'re just watching these files just change in real time. Now, what it does is it lets you plan your projects ahead of time. it isn\'t just about um working in real time the way it is in cursor. So the first thing you want to do is you want to make sure you have claude installed and then you create a a project directory and navigate to it. So let me go to cursor. So I have a directory here called claude crash course and let me do this. I\'m going to exit. Okay. So I am at my command line. You see that I am at claude crash course. To open up Claude code, you just type in the word Claude. Once you bring in Claude, now Claude is now connected with my API key to Enthropic. And there\'s all these things I can do. Now, the first thing you always want to do is do a forward slashinit. And what that does is it creates a new claude.md file for your codebase. Now you can take an existing project and do a claw to knit on it and if the directory you\'re working in your your current directory it will scan all of the files in your directory and build this claude MD file. So the first time I did it with a complex angular application it took five minutes for the init to run because it went off and it analyzed all this stuff. Now the claude MD file is like your rules file and cursor. I will post this in the community. It\'s important that you have this because this Claude MD the these seven steps are really important that you tell Claude you want to do it. So the first thing you want to do is you want it to think through the problem. Read the codebase for relevant files and write a plan to a directory called tasks todo.md. to-do.md is critical because it\'s your to-do list and as Claude is doing the different steps of your project, it\'s going to check it off that it\'s done. So, you\'re going to create a to-do list structure and the plan should have a to-do items that you check off as you complete it. Before you begin working, number three, before you begin working, check in with me and I will verify the plan because you don\'t want to just let it go off and run its own thing. Then with the task execution, begin working on them, marking them as complete as you go. And then we number five is communicate every step of the way. And number six is the simplicity principle. Make every change as simple as possible. Now this is where sonnet 3.7 used to fall down. If you if you had the experience of working with sonnet 3.7 before sonnet 40, it used to just build things that you didn\'t ask for. it just kind of went crazy and would go out there and say I\'m going to build a whole bunch of stuff. So this is where you needed to put this in here saying be do simple and then at the end add a review section. So this claude.md is the first thing that you want to create. Let me go back to here. So the first thing you want to do is initialize the project. Now the next file that\'s really important is requirements.md. This is your project plan. Now, it\'s an MD file, which means it\'s a markdown file, which means it\'s in this format. Now, if you\'re ever confused or you\'re ever not sure if your MD file is in the right format, just ask Claude. You could go in here right now and I could go into Claude and I could say, is my MD file in the right format? I I go off I run it and it is going to tell me that it is in the right format because I asked it to or I asked it to write it. So it is in an excellent format. It\'s well structured and comprehensive. So th in this case, I wanted to build a stock tracking application, something that could could track a stock that would use real- time APIs. So I had a pretty long conversation with Claude on Claude desktop to determine what functionality this stock application would have based on other stock tracking applications that are out there. So it is it is building this requirements.mmd for me. And then the next file is text stack. So in textstack.mmd I decided that I wanted this to be written in nex.js and I have an entire text stack of what dependencies I want to use, what I what API keys I\'m using, what environment variables I need to say. I create that in textstack.mmd. Now, if you notice, if you if you\'re familiar with cursor, we have not pressed go yet at all. And we\'ve done a lot. And really, Claude is about planning the project out ahead of time much more than actually executing it. Now, when it executes it, it\'s you\'re it\'s going to go it could go off for hours, but the more time you spend at the planning stage, the better off you are. Then this next file here is called design notes. Now I got this design notes using one of the coolest tools in um that Claude has which is the ability for me to go to a website I like. So I went to one of my Angular websites on my Mac. I went shift command4 drew a picture around the website and pasted it into claude and said please to please use this as your inspiration to create a CSS file for my application. So what it did is it developed this design notes.md based on an interface called absc which is a project I\'m working on. So in this case, it actually was able to look at my screen, see what my font hierarchy was, what fonts I was using, what colors I was using. And so now when I design this, when I let Claude run this stock trading application, it\'s going to use the design principles that I gave it by going into inspiration mode. So then we have our API keys.mmd which is basically do not remember to add it to get ignore if you put your API keys in your uh your if you create an API keys.mmd make sure you do not send it to get and that is security issues have been the the thing that is holding back vibe coding more than anything else. Okay, so this is the claw rules file that we already went through. Now there is a thing called plan mode and you get to plan mode by hitting shift tab twice uh from the command. Okay, it now says I am in plan mode. Now I want to also do model opus. Now I\'ve just set my model to the expensive thinking model. Okay, what you want to do is when you\'re planning, use Opus. When you\'re executing, use Sonnet. Now, Sonnet co sonnet and co Opus write code equally as well, but Sonnet is 12th the cost of Opus. But when you\'re planning, Opus plans way better than Sonnet. So, do your planning with Opus. Now that you\'re in plan mode, it is actually writing to all of these files. It is your planning. So I got, you know, basically plan mode is what got me my requirements MD. It got me my um it got me my text stack. This was all done in plan mode. Then all I have to do is shift tab back and now I\'m out of plan mode and I\'m ex in execution mode. So now I\'m going to type in model sonnet. And now I\'ve set the model to sonnet, which means now I can um do my builds, write my code, do it all in in sonnet. Now, um when you\'re in plan mode, do not be afraid to ask Claude what it thinks. If it is Claude is the LLM that if it if there was any LLM that I thought had a personality, it would be Claude. You Claude has no problem telling you if you did something stupid. Whereas Chat GPT is always going to tell you you\'re a genius. Claude has a tendency to not tell you you\'re a genius if you\'re doing something wrong. But it also follows instructions. So this is also a good time to talk about coding models. Chad GPT way too prone for hallucinations. Gemini doesn\'t have hallucinations, but it\'s very opinionated. And Gemini will and it also has a tendency to gaslight you. I\'ve had certain situations where Gemini Pro 2.5 has told me there\'s a calendar on the screen. I go to the screen, there\'s no calendar, and it basically tells me basically I\'m an idiot that there\'s a calendar on the screen. So, I have gotten into situations where I could not get any further with Gemini Pro. So, that is why as of today, Claude is what I\'m recommending now. But don\'t be afraid to ask Claude what it thinks. Now, I\'ve already talked about running Claude from cursor or VS Code. One of the reasons is you want to checkpoint often. Okay, it is not like cursor where you can checkpoint your code and roll back your code in the IDE. Cursor, what I do is every time something works, I get to a stage where something\'s working, I don\'t have bugs in it, push it to get. Okay. Um, now I\'ve also found that if you plan right, you may never need your checkpoints, but definitely push to get as often as you feel appropriate. Overuse Git that you\'re going to find um if you do go down a bad road, you\'re going to need it and and starting over is not not what you want to do. And I have had to start over several times anytime I\'ve used Gemini. And in my experiences with Gemini CLI, I\'ve also had to just abandon projects. So the the next thing you can do is um inspiration for UI design. So if I were to hit now you can paste images into claude which I find incredible. Okay. Now the two reasons to do it is inspiration for the UI design and debugging. If you get to the point where you\'re running out of the browser, you\'re running independently. Joe and I had run into a problem where something worked fine running locally, but when we pushed it to Apache, it didn\'t work anymore. Well, the easiest thing you can do is shift command 4 on a Mac, cut out your image, paste it into Claude, and say, \"What\'s going on here?\" And then if it\'s really complicated, that\'s actually when I switch back to Opus because Opus is going to reason through the bugs it sees pretty well. It\'s going to it\'s actually going to think it through. Now, the other thing you want to do often is when you\'re in here, do a forward slashcle. Anytime your context window starts to get full, you\'re going to be subject to hallucinations. And the reason you get the hallucinations, especially if you\'re coding in something like Angular, you\'re jumping back and forth between programming languages. It\'s writing TypeScript, then in my case, it\'s writing PHP, then it\'s jumping back and it\'s writing um HTML templates. And when you\'re doing that, as the context window gets bigger and you got more and more in the context and it because it\'s remembering all the prompts that I\'ve given it. So as you get further and further down into your project, you hit clear often. Anytime you\'ve got a feature to work, you\'re moving on to a next feature, just hit clear. That will clear your context window. It\'s going to eliminate the hallucinations and it\'s going to save you money because every time you do something it even if the tokens are cashed you\'re still paying for the tokens that are being used. Then the next thing you want to do is security checks. Now I don\'t know I have a prompt here and I\'ll post it in the school before you deploy anything or even take it off your computer. This is a prompt to give it that basically says please check through all the code and make sure it follows security best practices. Make sure there\'s no sensitive information in the front in the front end and that there are no vulnerabilities that can be exploited every time you before you push anything run that. Okay. And it will go through it will look at your code. It will make sure you can\'t do SQL injections. that will make sure that there\'s no APIs that are being exposed on the front end that somebody could use inspect element and see. Uh this is why vibe coding got a bad name to start off with because people were not running uh security checks. And Ed, I I would also add on to there um about uh removing all council log messages that were used for debugging. Um, a lot of times, um, I guess probably not as much in Claude, but in cursor, it\'ll put in a lot of console log debugging messages to help you figure out where your errors are at. And then you need to remind it to, hey, get rid of those to make your code production ready. Good, good point. And then my second to last thing to talk about is learn what claude code does. Okay, in my case, I\'m just now learning Nex.js. And when I say I\'m learning Nex.js, JS. I\'m just following the bouncing ball because I never plan to code it from scratch. But it is important. There is a prompt you can put in and I I\'ll post this also. Please explain the functionality in the code you just built. Walk me how you changed it and how it works. Act like you\'re a senior engineer teaching me to code. Now, what I recommend you do is you run that after every big after every big set of builds. you save that and you make that part of the project because at some point here you\'re going to want to turn this over to a junior dev. And what you\'re doing is you\'re turning over your claude code context your environment to a junior dev. So run this prompt summarize the results and save it for the next time you work on code. Now the one last thing I am going to add is do not doom scroll while you are waiting for Claude. So most of you found me on Instagram which means you probably all like to scroll through your Instagram. The one way to kill a day is to set off claude off on an adventure and the 30 minutes it takes you\'re sitting here doom scrolling on Instagram which is fine because that\'s how you found me. but it is going to kill your productivity. What I do, and this is a recommendation that was actually given to me by another creator, which is open up a new chat window while while Claude is working, and have a conversation with Claude about the project to get ideas about what you should do next, what other applications of the same type, also what kind of functionality you should add. Just don\'t pick up your phone. Don\'t start checking your emails. Have a conversation with with Claude about the project that you\'re working on because it will give you improvements. And this is um the hardest thing for me at first before I I found this was that I would start Claude, it would go off for an adventure and when it was done, it\'d be done 20 minutes later, but it would be two hours before I would look at the code again. So, this is just a pro tip that I totally recommend. Um so we actually in the pro community we have a uh a live event tomorrow for any of you who do actually want to join. Um we also have regional pricing on that because we did get we have a lot of people from all over the world and the US dollar um is not representative of the cost of living around the world. So DM me if you\'re uh in Southeast Asia or someplace like that. Um, but we\'re going to go through a lot more of the build process. This is stock application that I\'m actually wrote the requirements for. We\'re actually going to build it tomorrow. Um, the other thing is if you join the pro community, um, we\'ve got some projects. There\'s a there\'s a lot of I mean, we just have so many kids who are just getting out of college who are really having trouble. Um, this is a this is about building your resume also by building projects that actually have use. We have some applications that we handcoded that we want to rewrite in uh cursor and in cloud code. We\'ve also got some project managers who are in the pro community that are learning project management of um AI projects. Uh being a project manager, uh I got 4,000 followers on uh Instagram by trashing agile methodology. So we know that developers don\'t like project managers very much. And I\'m very curious to find out can I teach a developer to be a project manager by writing these requirements docs or a project manager as a developer. I\'m really curious. So we are going to we are going to explore that. So I want to thank you guys all. Um, I think this was one of the very few times I my time management was correct and the length of my uh and the length of my PowerPoint was the length of the um the presentation. I want to thank you all for attending. Next week, um, we haven\'t figured out what we\'re going to do next week, but is probably going to be MCP. I really want to get into building some MCP tools so people see exactly how we build MCPS. So, all right, everybody, have a great day. Does that um you have any um so things that came up are um from the chat that I saw were um specifically the claw.md file to post um I said all those texts will be posted in the school community and pinned so they should be in the top four or five posts on the board. um regional pricing um of the external community. Um I said that it was created but it\'s not uh it\'s not 100% fleshed out and set up ready to go to accept anybody in there yet. So we\'re still figuring that out. I\'ll I\'ll just change on that. Okay. So, it\'s currently manual, which means Joe and the rest of the team have not created the automations that if you if you sign up for the um the it\'s it\'s basically AI masters uh Southeast Asia is the name of the regional pricing community and if you join we have to move you manually but um eventually we\'ll we\'ll probably build a Zap year automation to do that. Um, and if uh if anybody is interested in learning Zapier, um, just post in the community that you are because that is uh that is something that Joe has been going down. He\'s been automating a lot of the stuff that we\'re doing with Zapier. Um, if you\'re interested in that, that is that is a real thing. Um, what else? Anything else on there, Joe? Um the I posted it a few times, but if you could flash it up on the screen again right now, the pro community link. And um a couple people asked about your thoughts on Windserve. You mentioned it before, but not at this meeting. So, okay. Honestly, I do not know enough about wind surf to be to give you an educated um view of wind surf.');
