INSERT INTO `lessons` (`id`, `course_id`, `title`, `description`, `content`, `lesson_type`, `duration_minutes`, `order_index`, `status`, `is_mandatory`, `video_url`, `video_duration`, `attachment_url`, `quiz_data`, `assignment_data`, `created_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `published_at`, `video_transcript`) VALUES
(119, 34, 'Live Event August 2, 2025', 'Live Event August 2, 2025', '', 'text', 60, 50, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/J6tMyyYzjJ4', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-08-05 02:03:55', '2025-08-05 02:04:26', NULL, 'All right, I\'ll uh I\'ll go for it. Okay, so what I what I wanted to cover today is uh prompt engineering because everything around artificial intelligence always comes back to prompting. So I spent a ton of time over the last couple of weeks vibe coding and vibe coding is prompting. Whatever you\'re doing, no matter how you get there, you\'re going to end up back at prompting. So the basically all a prompt is is it\'s the input that you give to a language model to tell it what you want it to do. Now that seems like it should be a really simple um straightforward. There should be no ambiguity about it and you should just be able to talk to the LLM and get what you want. But in reality that\'s not the case. there\'s certain things you need to be able to do um to be able to make sure that your prompt is um you know is understood by the large language model. So prompt engineering and I always do this to myself here got to get this thing out of the way. Um prompt engineering is the practice of crafting effective instructions to get optimal results from the AI language model. That\'s a long sentence to uh to basically say organize your prompts correctly so that the LLM isn\'t confused because one thing you do realize and the more you vibe code the more you realize that the the LLM is pretty stupid and the better you really have to lay things out in the prompt properly if you want the prompt or you want the LLM to return what you need. So, the difference between a prompt that\'s properly engineered and one that\'s kind of just thrown out there is the difference between getting a generic unhelpful responses and getting exactly what you need. So, good prompt engineering and this is something I definitely learned vibe coding and I had a little episode yesterday with Chad GPT giving me crazy crazy ideas of how to screw up my network. um the better your prompts, the better the results you\'re going to get. So there\'s a lot of different ways prompting happens and in the end prompting is how you\'re talking to the large language model. So whether you\'re in chat GPT using the chat interface, you\'re using the an any of the APIs, you\'re using a CLI like open AAI, Olama and uh GPT CLI, um you could be embedding it in tools or you could be using frameworks. Regardless of how you get that prompt into the context window, that prompt is it\'s a prompt. So, it\'s not like you\'re um prompting when you\'re talking to a chat interface and you\'re talking programmatically when you\'re talking through the API. The thing that\'s in the API is the prompt that you\'re that you\'re sending. So, prompting is how everything happens. Even if you it\'s all happening behind the scenes, it\'s happening as part of a workflow. And it\'s it\'s just always how well you put that prompt together is how well you\'re going to get a uh a response out of it. So prompt engineering as a name for something. Initially when LLM first came out, the the job of prompt engineer was what everybody was saying, okay, that\'s going to be my new career. Well, AI changes so fast. There\'s still prompting, but the idea of prompt engineering has turned into something totally new and but there are still some things that you can do through prompting that allow you to not have to do supervised fine-tuning. So if you have a big enough model and the model\'s got enough nuance, you can definitely guide the results of the output in such a way that in a lot of cases you can avoid supervised fine-tuning. It also enables behavior that is very taskspecific. It reduced cost and complexity. And most important, if you\'re going to build reliable systems using artificial intelligence, prompt engineering is the number one uh thing you\'re going to need to know. So, you\'re always going to be getting better at prompt engineering because prompting is always going to change. Prompting today is not what prompting was a year ago. and prompting a year from today is not going to be like it is right now. So the most important thing that you have to realize or one of the most important things is what is the context window. So when you hear about different size models you hear about differentiz context windows and initially the first sets of context windows with Chad GPT3 the context window was only about 3,000 tokens. Now, a token represents a word or a part of a word. So, there are some words that can be represented by one token, but there\'s also a lot of words that require two um tokens to represent it. The size of the context window is the number of tokens that are processed simultaneously through the prompt. So if we go back to the white paper uh attention is all you need. It\'s basically saying that every token that is in the context window is being analyzed against every other token that\'s in the context window. And that\'s how it\'s determining the meaning and that\'s how it\'s determining what it what you\'re asking for it. So everything that\'s in the context window gets processed with every prompt. Now, that\'s critical because when you start talking about context windows that are 10 million tokens in size, just because you have a 10 million token context window does not mean you want to use 10 million tokens. Because as far as computational power goes, the more that\'s in the context window, the more computational power that\'s going to be required by the LLM to do its job because everything is everything in the context window is processed. And now over here to the right on the orange part, one of the things and I I broke this out for a very specific reason. you the tools outside of the LLM are what is maintaining the history of the conversation. The LLM itself is stateless. It\'s like a website and it\'s like an old a very old website that when you go to it, it is taking everything that is in the context window and it\'s analyzing it. So, it is looking at your current prompt and then it\'s also looking at the history of the conversation. That\'s why over time as your your conversations get longer and longer, sometimes they get crazier and crazier. So, for example, yesterday when I was using chat GPT to try to destroy my Proxmox network, the longer the conversation got, the worse the ideas that GPT was coming up with to fix the problem. And that\'s also something that you run into with um when you\'re doing a a large project in vibe coding. It\'s a thing called a distractor. And basically, if there\'s something in your context window that isn\'t relevant to the current thing you\'re acting on, that\'s called a distractor. And so as yesterday as I was going uh through network commands trying to figure out how to do things, the fact that all of my previous conversation was still being passed into the context window was actually confusing chat GPT because it was giving it ideas about things that hadn\'t worked in the past. So it would try something that thing wouldn\'t work but it was still in the context window as one of the recommendations. So you do need to manage everything that\'s in the context window with the prompt. Now if you\'re doing it programmatically that\'s a lot easier than if you\'re using a chat window like chat GPT because in some cases with chat GPT you just need to start over. Now one of the things I don\'t like about chat GPT 03 which is their reason their big reasoning model is it reads all of your conversations. So it it tries to know everything it can about you. So it knows about conversations that aren\'t necessarily relevant to what you\'re current doing currently doing. So sometimes the context window is um more of a distraction than it\'s a benefit. So the larger context windows aren\'t always the best thing. Now what we get out of the LLM is not always going to be what we want out 