 after the prompting styles, let\'s look at fshot program prompting. So, fshot prompting is probably one of the most popular and it\'s probably um one of the easiest ways to do so. What you\'re doing is you\'re providing a short series of input outputs and then you keep giving the model new inputs. So, you pro you say here\'s some examples. Okay, now let\'s uh let\'s change it a little bit. Okay, I don\'t want to use this kind of flower. I want to use this other kind of flower. Few shot prompting is it\'s a method where the prompt includes a few examples, but it also helps generalize with new inputs. So, as you\'re going through the prompting, you\'re refining the output. So you\'re starting with a few examples, you\'re giving it more information, and you\'re just completely iterating until you get to a certain output. Now, one of the things you can do with Fshot programming is reducing the need for fine-tuning. So if I can give you examples of let\'s say JSON arrays or JSON objects of this is how I want the output to look the better the model the more likely it is to be able to do that without supervised fine-tuning. So in the case of like um let\'s say I want my model to constantly I want it to output a J JSON objects. If I can give examples of the JSON objects in my prompt then I may not need to fine-tune the model to give me decent JSON objects. Now, this is much more of a real thing when we\'re talking about the big models like the GPTs, the clauds because those models have so many parameters. They can get they can gain the nuance and they can figure things out just from the prompting. Whereas with um a small model, let\'s say it\'s a 2 and a half billion parameter model, that\'s where just giving it the prompting probably isn\'t going to work for you and you\'re going to need to do something more intense like a um supervised fine-tuning. So in chain of but in chain of thought prompting we\'re going to guide the tools through the process and um you\'re going to