ame in. Maybe we\'re using a vector database. Maybe we\'re using a relational database. Maybe we\'re using a knowledge graph. But we\'re going and gathering some additional data. Maybe they\'re examples. Maybe it\'s some relevant information. but we\'re putting that into the prompt. And so this is really important for factual accuracy. And it\'s one of the things that will stop hallucinations is if you\'re putting in relevant articles or relevant information that you retrieved ahead of time to make sure that the large language model can\'t go down a path that you don\'t want it to go. My favorite one, although it\'s not an an issue anymore for the most part, is how many planets are in the solar system. So, when I was a kid, we had nine. Now, we have eight and an exoplanet. So, if I want to make sure that asking about the planets of the solar system gets it right, I might put a document in there that references the eight planets. So there\'s just different things that you can do um with the retrieval augmented generation to make sure that the prompt actually performs better. So another thing and the reason I put another reason I put this here is retrieval augmented generation is going to be a career path. You know prompting and putting information into the prompt and as prompt engineering this is a critical offshoot of it. Now, its baby brother is context augmented generation. And this is when you\'re going to be adding the same documents and the same context every time. So, for instance, let\'s say I\'ve got my little medical chatbot and it this chatbot is only going to ask questions about one specific thing. Maybe there\'s a a certain uh ompic. So let\'s just say we created a chatbot that asks you about GLP-1 in or GLP-1 inhibitor. So we have OMIC, we have all of those and that\'s the only thing it is going to answer questions about and we know we have a decent size context window that gives us the opportunity to pass in the same information every time. So that\'s 