(516, 148, 'What are Large Language Models', 'I realized my introduction to LLMs was a little intense, so I added this one as a warm up.  This is a great presentation from Google explaining Large Language Models (LLMs)! It covers the key concepts really well.', 'What LLMs are:\r\n\r\n    Machine learning models that understand and generate human language\r\n    Based on transformer architecture (invented by Google)\r\n\r\nCalled \"large\" because of both the neural network size and massive training datasets (trillions of tokens)\r\n\r\nHow they work:\r\n\r\nLike sophisticated autocomplete - they predict what text is likely to follow given input\r\nLearn patterns from massive text datasets\r\nCan handle multiple tasks with one model (chat, translation, summarization, code generation, etc.)\r\n\r\nHow to use them:\r\n\r\nThrough prompts (the input text you provide)\r\nZero-shot learning: Single instruction (\"Write me a poem about Ada Lovelace\")\r\nFew-shot learning: Providing examples to establish patterns (like showing English-French translation pairs)\r\n\r\nKey insights:\r\n\r\nYou don\'t need to be a machine learning expert to use LLMs\r\nPrompt design is an art - small changes in wording can significantly affect outputs\r\nResults are highly dependent on the specific model\r\nThere\'s no \"optimal\" way to write prompts - experimentation is key', 'text', 6, 3, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/iR2O2GPbB0E', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-27 12:36:19', '2025-07-29 00:19:55', NULL, 'Automatic transcript retrieval requires OAuth authentication. Please use OAuth flow or manually add transcript. Video title: What are Large Language Models (LLMs)?'),
(517, 148, 'What is Prompting', 'This Google Cloud video explains prompt engineering as the foundation for effective interaction with generative AI tools like ChatGPT.', '', 'text', 8, 7, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/S-n3hwpMap4?si=b9NDIPxKE5HUfUMP', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-27 12:38:50', '2025-07-29 00:17:14', NULL, 'Automatic transcript retrieval requires OAuth authentication. Please use OAuth flow or manually add transcript. Video title: LEARN AI: Module 3: Prompt Engineering - Listen'),
(518, 148, 'What is the Context Window?', 'This IBM Technology video explains context windows as the \"working memory\" of large language models, determining how much conversation history an LLM can maintain and reference.', '', 'text', 11, 8, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/-QVoIxEpFkM?si=cBch2fYZQP0TwYEG', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-27 12:40:16', '2025-07-29 00:17:03', NULL, 'Automatic transcript retrieval requires OAuth authentication. Please use OAuth flow or manually add transcript. Video title: What is a Context Window? Unlocking LLM Secrets'),
(519, 148, 'What are AI Agents?', 'This Google Cloud video provides a foundational overview of AI agents, exploring definitions, use cases, and building considerations for these increasingly popular AI systems.', '', 'text', 7, 9, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/ZZ2QUCePgYw?si=Lrm-qV_2P3z5umpP', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-27 12:41:42', '2025-07-29 00:16:52', NULL, 'Automatic transcript retrieval requires OAuth authentication. Please use OAuth flow or manually add transcript. Video title: Intro to AI agents'),
(520, 148, 'What is MCP?', 'This ByteByteGo video explains the Model Context Protocol (MCP) as one of the most significant advancements in LLM integration, released by Anthropic in late 2024.', '', 'text', 9, 10, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/_d0duu3dED4?si=hKTICIeSQsGDMw0p', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-27 12:42:49', '2025-07-29 00:16:19', NULL, 'Automatic transcript retrieval requires OAuth authentication. Please use OAuth flow or manually add transcript. Video title: Why Everyone’s Talking About MCP?'),
(521, 148, 'What is Ollama?', 'This video provides a quick overview of Ollama, an open-source platform that simplifies running large language models locally.', '', 'text', 3, 11, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/_6SlPLNEpzQ?si=VnQLMs4GL3X0KqCI', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-27 12:43:50', '2025-07-29 00:16:06', NULL, 'Automatic transcript retrieval requires OAuth authentication. Please use OAuth flow or manually add transcript. Video title: Ollama In 120 Seconds'),
(522, 148, 'Brief History of AI', 'In this presentation I discuss a brief history of how we got to where we are in AI.', '', 'text', 15, 1, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/-ks0yIIuMnc', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-29 00:12:04', '2025-07-29 00:12:05', NULL, 'hey everybody welcome so this section is called the brief history of an artificial intelligence you\'re probably wondering yourself why is Ed focusing on history i\'m not here to learn history i want to learn how to do AI i want to learn how to do machine learning i want to build things i want to build agents i want to build tools what does history matter well the reality is artificial intelligence is all about context and if we I\'m going to I want to go through the history of artificial intelligence to tell you how it was so important that about 5 years ago there was a white paper written I guess actually I guess it was longer than it was about 2018 but it was more like 5 years ago that it became really important and people realized what to do with it and there was a paper called attention is all you need and attention is basically context and what it was is it was a way to determine what something means and then that is where this whole AI I thing kicks off of so what we really want to talk about is what is artificial intelligence and then why does the history matter and the history matters because when you\'re working on projects and you run into problems or there\'s something that you want to do and you don\'t know how to do it it\'s the context of what you\'re trying to do through the whole scope of artificial intelligence that\'s really important okay so you\'re going to run into problems there\'s going to be issues and there might be something that\'s in this history that says \"Oh well now I understand why this was important.\" So let me talk a little bit about why it\'s important as far as context goes so let\'s have a sentence and the sentence is uh I swam across the river to get to the other bank now let\'s look at the word bank okay now if you just take that word specifically it could mean a financial institution and in English it\'s most likely going to mean a financial institution if you say the word bank 90% of the time it means financial institution but when you put the words in front of it I swam across the river those other words tell you what the meaning of the word bank means so I swam across the river to get to the other bank bank alone means nothing but bank with swam and river pretty much tells you what the word bank means and that\'s why it\'s so important to know as much as you can about the foundations of artificial intelligence how we got there and then that way when you run into a problem that problem you\'re going to be able to see it in more wider context it\'s really hard to just dive in the middle of something and then think you\'re going to understand why something is the case because a lot of cases it\'s not just um what you do it\'s why you\'re doing it so the ancient uh roots of artificial intelligence really come back to understanding how the world works okay now if you were in it for any time in the last 40 years everything was binary there was a certain way we represented things inside of computers and it was with ones and zeros it\'s ons and offs is the voltage high or is the voltage low now that is a way to represent things and you can represent a lot of the world by representing it in ones and zeros but in reality the world is represented as a as neural networks and the interesting thing about math is that the entire world can be described with math now not by me not by most people and more and more people are learning that even how your brain works there\'s mathematical formulas that represent how your brain works math is a representative uh it represents how something works so going back all the way to Greek mythology everybody was trying to figure out how things worked and because of the technologies at the time they had a little trouble figuring out how things work and one thing I need to remind everybody is you know 5,000 years 10,000 years ago the people were just as smart as we are today they had as much ability to do intellect they can as much ability to figure things out but they didn\'t have the history and they didn\'t have the context and they didn\'t have the fundamental building blocks to know how to do these things so if we go back all the way to 1936 there was a thing called the Turing machine okay from Alan Touring and I\'m not going to go too far into detail with it but basically it was the first time we tried to model uh the way things work around something other than binary ons and offs yes and nos so in 1950 there was a thing called the Turing test and that was where there was this idea that machines could think now again we are 75 years later and machines still aren\'t thinking now they\'re looking like they\'re thinking but they\'re really not thinking so in 1956 at at a Dartmouth con conference AI was born so these three guys these McCarthy Minsky Simon and Newell they came up with this idea for artificial intelligence now the problem was back in the 1950s we didn\'t have computers and we sure didn\'t have GPUs so the idea was there the math was there they were starting to see how the math pulled together but it really didn\'t you know there was nothing for them to do because you didn\'t even have computers okay back then so um from from the 1950s to the 1960s there was all this interest in what\'s now artificial intelligence but the technology wasn\'t there so when you got to the 1970s and the 1980s you got to this thing called uh the AI winter and that\'s just when mathematicians were off doing their things but they were doing basic research nobody was really worrying about artificial intelligence and for about 20 years nothing really happened there was no funding for it u there was huge limitations in the way they were going about things like with everything if you take the wrong approach to something you you might end up with a problem so there was a lot of issues from 1970s to 1980s which meant artificial intelligence kind of went away you didn\'t hear about it but then we got into the 1990s and the there was this move from rules-based to statistical approaches now a rules-based approach is if this then this or uh a switch statement this or this or this or this it\'s a very binary it\'s a rules you just basically you programmed a computer by giving it a series of rules and you teached it a a bunch of rules and if it you know and it just was a decision it just said if it\'s this then we\'re going to go this if it\'s this and then you had math and so u in the 1990s people started to say well you know what statistics are are really interesting and now we\'re starting to get the computers that can do the kind of math that is with statistics now when I was in college computers couldn\'t really do anything with statistics and it was all done by hand so a lot of the math I learned in college was like you either did it by hand or it didn\'t get done so you got to the 90s and the 2000s all of a sudden you could start to rep um represent the statistics and the math behind things so uh in 1997 and I remember this IBM\'s uh Deep Blue bait Casper off in a game of chess doesn\'t seem like much now because computers beat people in chess all the time but back then it was a huge deal okay and so and I was actually working on a project with IBM at the time and it was the big it was the big news okay everybody wanted the computer that could play chess so over the 1990s and the 2000s you had this growth of data and computational power and so you got into things like decision trees SVMs and ensemble learning so you basically were getting to the point where you were starting to use statistical approaches in computers now in 2012 there was a thing called AlexNet and it was image recognition and it was the first time a computer could look at a picture and figure out what was in the picture now the way it did it now we would call that ancient history we wouldn\'t do it today but the fact that it could be done told everybody hey we we we\'re on to something here if this works there\'s a lot of other things we can do so in the two in the 2010s all of a sudden GPUs became a big thing and we\'re gonna I\'m gonna give a little credit to cryptocurrency there because you you got into this whole mining and GPUs the idea of using a GPU for for something other than graphics power on you know displaying graphics on your screen suddenly became a thing so in the 2010s all of a sudden there\'s GPUs and then in 2016 there was something called Alph Go and if you\'re not familiar with the the game go it is way it is an order of magnitude more complicated than chess when it comes to the number of possible moves cuz up until this point you could play chess through brute force you could basically say this is what the this is what the board looks like right now let\'s play through every possible combination and come up with what\'s the best next move based on just playing the game millions of times alph Go with Go you can\'t do that so the fact that Alph Go beat um the Go the Go champion was a huge deal because you couldn\'t just do that through brute force so throughout the two the 2010s AI was powering speech recognition vision and we started getting into the self-driving tech now the biggest thing was Transformers in 2017 now it\'s funny because I was taking courses in data science in 2021 and nobody had heard of the paper uh that was the big seismic shift like we were talking about convolutional neural networks decision trees all these other things but we were not talking about transformers so in all of a sudden there was a GPT2 which was a hallucination central but it proved that things could be done and it moved all the way in the 2020s from GPT2 to GPT4 now you have claude you have gemini and it all goes back to this paper called attention is all you need and it\'s where the transformer model uh came about and so at one point you would hear people say everything now is a transformer former so what transformers can do text images code generative AI it\'s all because of this transformer model and this one paper so consider yourself lucky to be starting artificial intelligence now because if you were doing it in the late 201s you were learning about a whole bunch of stuff that\'s not all that important right now or it\'s kind of nice to know in the history but I\'m not really going into any detail in it because it\'s just kind of reference material it\'s not stuff that you absolutely need to know so now we\'re talk now I\'m going to just talk about some of the fluffy stuff that I got to throw in here because you know there\'s a lot of people watching this you know there are things with artificial intelligence that we have to take into account okay bias and fairness is your large language model bias against certain people is it fair now one of the things I\'m starting to see is that as the model gets bigger it gets pulled towards fairness it can\'t the more it knows the less it has bias and even when you\'re trying to put bias in larger models it\'s hard to do because the models are trained on so much data it just kind of pulls it into fairness and then explainability and hallucinations now I ran into explanability in the early 2020s where I had models that were doing things and then people were saying \"Well we\'re making million-dollar decisions based on the what this model is saying explain to me how it works.\" And you say \"I can\'t explain it because it\'s a black box it\'s AI it\'s doing it inside of the model how am I possibly going to know how this works?\" And so you couldn\'t explain the answer well simultaneously AI is generating these wild hallucinations and and the way the probability of the models work you could get these crazy hallucinations coming out of it where the model was just making stuff up so the combination of explanability and hallucinations were a real problem you had you couldn\'t explain what the model was doing and it was doing stuff blatantly wrong sometime then you have things called alignment and control you know every society wants the models to be aligned to their social values and uh so you see different things you ask different models different questions and I\'m not going to say what\'s right or what\'s wrong but if you ask some international models different things than US models you get different answers or there\'s a heck of a lot of history on some subjects in one part of the world and there\'s other history about these subjects in the other parts of the world so alignment in control is going to kind of be a big issue and then we get into the regulatory and the ethical debates and that\'s this is going to be hugely problematic in the future because the more AI can do things and the more AI becomes accessible to the public you\'re really going to you\'re going to need some regulations but who\'s doing the regulating and I know in the United States Congress knows nothing about computers so you you start out with the fact that hey I got to tell you what a computer is and then and I got to teach you about it so that you can tell me what we\'re allowed to do or not so there\'s going to be some huge issues with that in the future now again I wanted to go through this history and you\'ll start to understand in the future when I start talking about other things why this is important so just take all this in if you need to watch it a couple times if you can go through it a couple times maybe a 2x speed uh just to remember that there is a history that started artificial intelligence and then we\'ll move on to the the next topic in the next section'),
(523, 148, 'Types of Artificial Intelligence', 'There are many different type of AI.  In this video I discuss the different types and categories of Artificial Intelligence.', '', 'text', 12, 2, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/6kOmiNcjiBU', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-29 00:15:03', '2025-07-29 00:17:47', NULL, 'hey everybody welcome so in this section we\'re going to go through uh AI foundations and uh and a lot of background in artificial intelligence and what I\'m basically doing in the next 15 minutes and trying to distill everything I\'ve learned since I graduated college related to AI into 15 minutes so we can refer to it later now this is where a lot of the reinforcement learning is going to come in for the technique I\'m trying to experiment with for on how to teach you AI faster than any college can ever do it so this is going to be an interesting uh this is going to be an interesting section but what we\'re talking about here is we\'re going to talk about the different types and techniques of artificial intelligence and kind of how they evolved over time so I\'m going to introduce a couple of things that were really big a while ago um and when I say a while I mean like five years ago and that are obsolete today but you still may run into them one of the things you have to understand is there are a lot of people in artificial intelligence who\'ve dedicated their lives going down a certain path only to find out in 2021 2022 that that path was the wrong path so you do run into a lot of people especially if you work in a science community of people who are grasping old technologies so I want to introduce those old technologies so you\'ll have context and hey if I had to spend a ton of time learning this crap you might as well have to sit through some of it so we are going to talk about um what is artificial intelligence and why we category categorize AI models so there have been different models that have evolved over time think of AI as as constantly moving and with anything in technology in general you come up with an idea somebody releases it the masses jump on on board with it and then they point out everything that\'s wrong with it okay so you do this great work you release this AI model you release chat GPT 3.5 the masses get a hold of it and within six months every problem it has has been defined and then people start fixing those problems and then you evolve and sometimes the fixes of those problems introduce new problems and then you fix those new problems and you\'re kind of playing whack-a-ole where you\'re just always searching for the solution so we\'re going to go through how we got to where we are and so you can understand because a lot of times people look at things and go why the heck are you doing it that way and it\'s a historical reason for doing it that way now the other thing is is I am not going to bring up the math now when I took my one graduate level uh and I won\'t go with the three-digit uh college that offered it um in Massachusetts um it was all math okay it was crazy math it was It was math to intimidate you and math to punish you it was basically we had 1500 people in a program and you better know your math okay so my chief technology officer Joe and myself we took this thing and I will say I think he was in the top 10 and I was in the top 15 but it was brutal we\'ll never do that again and I\'m here to spare you from that so let\'s just look at the highlevel artificial intelligence model categories so the first one is basically everything that we had up until like 2020 which is um rulebased and and I got that a little bit wrong because convolutional neural networks and the the timing of things we you\'ll you\'ll understand where I\'m going with this rule-based reasoning systems it\'s a set of rules and it\'s really based on data science and you create these rules and you follow the rules and they basically have rule-based reasoning if I did this and I did this then I did this it\'s kind of that stuff that they they teach you like in grade school if so and so is so and so\'s mother who was so and so it\'s like basic logic okay that\'s symbolic artificial intelligence it\'s rules-based reasoning and these are called expert systems now we go to something called machine learning and machine learning is where it\'s really getting fun now think of your brain and your brain has neurons and synapses okay the neuron let\'s just say your neuron has got a piece of electricity between zero and one and you have 100 trillion of those and they\'re interconnected with each other with synapses and think of synapses as little resistors okay so the amount of electricity that can go from one neuron to the next neuron is regulated by this thing called a syninnapse and there is a mathematical formula that rep represents that and you you\'re it it\'s in layers okay so all of these synapses and neurons are all interconnected and you model that in a computer and then you start showing the computer things and what it is and that\'s called machine learning so what you do is you take a big set of data you take like 10% of it you set it off to the side and then you just keep teaching and teaching and teaching and then you take that 10% back and you go well how well did I do i didn\'t do that good let\'s do some more and that\'s called machine learning and it\'s called and you\'re doing basically you\'re learning how the neurons in the synapses and in this case they\'re called weights and biases how they\'re interrelated to each other by just feeding it data feeding it data and you keep trying new things and it\'s a lot of trial and error with some mathematics from calculus and linear algebra that lets you adjust the weights and the biases until it learns things and that is machine learning now there\'s a lot of intricacies to machine learning and it\'s you\'re going to be really happy that you did not have to go through uh all the math and all the different trial and errors that happened with machine learning and pattern-based matching to get to where you are and so that is basic machine learning and then we get into neural networks and represent representation learning now machine learning is still neural networks okay um but it\'s learning the patterns based on just data deep learning is much it\'s deeper i guess that\'s the right word to use and you\'re basically this is everything today that you see is really based on deep learning so if we look at these things called neural networks and and that\'s what I started to explain it\'s kind of a it\'s kind of a computer representation of basically how your brain works i mean we\'re finding out more and more that it\'s not exactly this way but you have an input so let\'s say your eyes are your input okay then but you see something it goes into your brain and now there\'s layers okay and there\'s an there\'s an input there\'s layers and then your final result let\'s say moving my hand or moving to the left so I get an input we have layers of these neurons and synapses that determine something and then I have an output so you have uh input layer hidden layers that are doing the the math and then output layers and so that\'s the basic structure of a neural network and there\'s a lot of different kinds of neural network what I just described is called a feed forward neural network it comes in and it moves forward and then it goes to the output and that\'s uh in the history it used to be called a multi-layer perceptron because it\'s you\'re perceiving something and then it\'s going through multi-layers okay so that\'s the structure the basic structure of a neural network now to train the neural network you have this thing called gradient descent and it\'s basically it\'s back propagation so what you do is let\'s say I\'m teaching you to recognize a number so I show you a one and I just guess that it\'s a six and then it comes back and says \"Nope that\'s not a one that\'s not a six.\" So it readjusts all my weights and my biases and then it tries with another number and it will just keep doing that over and over and over again and part of this to in the math are these things called activation functions because in computers things are ones and zeros but in your brain it\'s 0ero to one okay it\'s the one being the most amount of electricity in your neuron zero being the least amount of electricity in your neuron so there\'s something called activation functions and different activation functions are better at training different types of models and that\'s as far as I\'m going to go into it i mean I will tell you we spend a whole lot of time on Rayu Sigma and Tanh as to when you would use each one this is not the this is not the place for that so then what you get with the use case of this is basic classification and regression so let\'s say classification is this a number one or is this a number six i\'m basically taking something in and I\'m determining what it is and that\'s what we\'re going to do basically with a neural network now one of the first really popular really important neural networks was called the convolutional neural network and if you\'ve gone into a hospital any time in the last 15 years and you\'ve had either an MRI or a CT scan there used to be a time when they would take this picture they would send it off to a room full of doctors and they would actually look for things well a convolutional neural network is image recognition and it uses something called the convolution which is it basically scans and goes through it and it does it at different levels and it\'s it\'s a really cool technique but what it got good at is training models to learn things okay training models that there\'s a carcinoma in there and now when you use a convolutional neural network and this you can actually determine a whole bunch of stuff you weren\'t even looking for so within seconds you can put an MRI or a CT scan in and it\'ll determine everything that you need to know so the use cases for this are object detection and image classification is there a cat in this picture yes and the convolutional neural network is what is good at doing that so convolutional neural networks are good at taking something at a point in time and determining what\'s in it well now what one what happens when we want to predict the future predict the next word or predict is the stock price going to go up or is the stock price going to go down that\'s called an RNN that\'s a recurrent neural network that is related to processing data sequentially we have a one we have a two we have a three okay the next number is going to be a four and now recurrent neural networks because they\'re timebased there\'s some real limitations to them because the as things get more complicated you need to be able to look further back in the past to help detect what the future\'s going to be and as you\'re doing more and more complicated things with rec recurrent neural networks you ran into this thing called a a vanishing gradient problem which basically would mean the recurrent neural network would get confused and it couldn\'t predict the next number so in order to fix things now let\'s keep going back to our process the way AI has always worked is we come up with something it\'s really cool we see a bunch of limitations then everybody jumps through hoops to try to fix those limitations well the because of the vanishing gradient problem they came up with something called called long shortterm memory which is basically enhancing the RNN to have more memory so it can see further back okay and then the other thing was GRU and of course I\'m not remembering what GRU is right now it GRU was a was an enhancement it was a simpler method of long shortterm memory it related to long short-term memory but it was just a simpler way of doing it so what we were doing here is RNN\'s had a problem let\'s fix it with LSTMs and GRUs then we get to the next thing which is natural language processing and that\'s really the precursor to large language models we\'re talking about text now we\'re talking about figuring out what something means now if you go back to the original translation uh programs they were great because all they basically did is word for word you translated the one word the next word the next word well the problem was the sentence structure from one language to another is different we have a very weird uh sentence structure in English compared to like the Slavic languages or the Asian languages so you can\'t just take word for word and say I\'m going to the the word bank for instance uh it it could mean a the shore of a river it could mean a financial institution so natural language processing was using um artificial intelligence to basically turn words into numbers and then have mathematical ways to determine what something means based on the sentence structure and that\'s where things like word embeddings called word devec and glove came about and then you had these traditional versus uh neural NLP models so natural language project uh processing was again that progression we had RNN\'s now we\'re going into NLP now everything changed in 2017 when this paper came out of course it changed in 2017 but we didn\'t really know about it until chat GPT35 came out or ChatGPT3 came out because they\'re all working off working on things and this is from a paper called attention is all you need and that is where you created they created this thing called an attention mechanism and that\'s where you look at each word and you look at the words around it and based on not just the word itself but what the other words around it mean is what does that word mean and that\'s how you get the context or the meaning of something and when we talk about the context window that\'s the number of words or tokens that the attention mechanism is looking at so when you hear the early uh the early GPTs or the transformer models had like 2,000 tokens that would be the number of tokens that it looked at for each token now we\'re talking about uh LLMs that have a 10 million token context window that means each word is being compared to the other 10 million tokens in the context window that\'s why it\'s not always best to make your to give the context window a ton of stuff in your prompting because if you have a 10 million token context window every token is going to get analyzed against every other token so you know if you\'re going against two that\'s one thing if you\'re going against four that\'s another if you\'re going against 10 million that\'s a lot of math so this led into everything we\'re doing today which is the encoder decoder architecture where you\'re taking a sentence and then you\'re encoding it into these vectors into these um basically that\'s what it is it\'s a vector is a list of numbers so the transformer model is what most large language models and most artificial intelligence that we\'re talking about in 2025 you know is based on so that is your um your model that you\'re off off of so convolutional neural networks it\'s still got a specific use case rnn\'s not so much nlp people are still trying to use it but for the most part the transformer model is the model that you need to know now how do you train this and this is where reinforcement learning comes in so if we look at the way we build a model so they what they do is they go out to they do this thing called pre-training and you just take as much stuff as you can get and you encode it and you do the you put the attention mechanisms in and what you\'re basically doing is you\'re teaching the model the basics the this is a cat this is a dog a dog\'s kind of like a cat you\'re just you\'re you\'re training it in all of the basics but what happens is that it doesn\'t mean that the model can do anything it just knows a lot of stuff now where reinforcement learning comes in is that\'s where it\'s training the model to do stuff it\'s training the model of the interactions between this thing so it\'s basically it you\'ve got an agent okay and this agent is teaching the model something and then it\'s giving it a reward based on how good of a job it did so initially for chat GPT35 it was reinforcement learning through human feedback they had rooms full of people they would ask GPT a question gpt would give it an answer and it would say no that\'s not a good answer try again okay that\'s a better answer and you would just keep doing it until it got better and it was basically a reward structure and so over time the model would get better because you would keep asking it questions and then telling it was it good or bad and it would use that to learn and improve itself now that\'s done through agents okay that\'s done through technology so it\'s basically playing a game and that\'s kind of how I\'m teaching in this course is that we\'re playing a game the if you get it right you get a point if you don\'t get it right you lose a point so it is a reward state thing so you\'re basically teaching the model to get better at things by teaching it by making it try more and more and more basically the more reinforcement learning the better the model is and that\'s where it comes into that\'s where models get really expensive because you\'re spending so much time doing this reinforcement learning so that it\'s good at everything okay so now let\'s just talk about this all in um in a in a review so AI starts with neural networks and that is basically layers and weights and biases so when you talk about training you\'re talking about determining what the right weights and biases are between basically in your brain it would be this the neurons in the synapses so when you\'re training you\'re using this technique called back propagation where you say this is what we did was it good was it not if it wasn\'t good you adjust the weights and biases a little bit and then you try it again and you just keep doing that over and over and over excuse me so a neural network is feed forward so the the perception comes in it goes through the different layers and then at the end you have an output and that\'s usually a classification so is this a number one or is this a number two based on what it sees it your neurons and your synapses the neural network basically classifies cnn\'s are convolutional neural networks and those are called convolutional layers and that\'s where it may look at a 3x3 then a 6x6 and then it looks at things and that\'s really good for vision tasks next we have RNNs so we talked about detecting things now we talk now we\'re talking about predicting things so what\'s the next word so that is where recurrent neural networks and long short-term memory came in so you may still hear about that but they\'re sequence models but that\'s really the the precursor to natural language processing which is the basics of transformers where you\'re talking about embedding transformers chat bots and translations and then we get to reinforcement learning which is where we are today so every time a new model comes out it goes through some base training tasks and then it goes through reinforcement learning and the difference between models is how the reinforcement learning is done so a reasoning model has reinforcement learning done completely different than a non-reasoning model because in this case you\'re telling the model that it you want it to stop and think and you want to stop and evaluate then you get into things like mixture of experts where instead of using the whole model for every query you classify and you say well I\'m talking about chemistry it\'s like going on the college campus and saying \"Well we\'re going to talk about chemistry so let\'s go to the chemistry building.\" This is where models change from version to version number of parameters to number of parameters so you always think like in your brain the bigger brain is better than the smaller brain but a smaller brain can learn a very specific task very very well so this is basically your foundation your classifications these are going to be core topics that you\'re going to want to learn because you\'re going to run into them no matter what you\'re doing and knowing this background is really important when you run into issues or something new comes out and you\'re wondering why it works the way it does so this brings us to where we are today we have our basic foundation models like GPT and Claude and Gemini we have our multimodal which is including language video and a audio and then obviously we\'re just going to keep going back to this ethics and explanability thing but the the models right now are really based into two categories they\'re the foundation models which are textbased and then the multimodal and these are the models that can look at a picture see things tell you what\'s in the picture and draw pictures so uh I ran a little bit long i wanted this to be 15 minutes it\'s going to end up being a little over 20 but I think this this information is really important for you and then when we when we get into our reinforcement learning you\'re going to see these topics and you\'re going to see why why they\'re they\'re actually important'),
(524, 148, 'Large Language Models', 'This is my introduction to Large Language Models.', '', 'text', 0, 4, 'published', 1, '', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-29 00:20:50', '2025-07-29 00:20:50', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `lessons` (`id`, `course_id`, `title`, `description`, `content`, `lesson_type`, `duration_minutes`, `order_index`, `status`, `is_mandatory`, `video_url`, `video_duration`, `attachment_url`, `quiz_data`, `assignment_data`, `created_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `published_at`, `video_transcript`) VALUES
(525, 149, 'Overview Presentation', 'This is the 20 minute overview of our learning approach.', 'This is the 20 minute overview of our learning approach.', 'text', 20, 1, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/ZlllVIBK1I8', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-24 17:05:01', '2025-07-24 18:30:21', NULL, 'Hey everybody, welcome. So this is a PowerPoint presentation that I gave in one of the live sessions that unfortunately I deleted the recording by accident right after it was created. So I\'m going to go through it again and I\'m going to use it in the about page for the pro community and I\'m also going to use it here because what I\'m going to cover is our process for learning artificial intelligence. So over the last couple of weeks as we\'ve been working with the community, we have seen uh what works and what doesn\'t work. And really the uh it\'s good for us to be able to set targets, set goals, and set metrics. We want to we want to as humans learn AI the way we teach machines how to be more like humans. So, our goals when we\'re learning AI is the first thing we want to be able to do, especially if we\'re talking to clients, we\'re talking to uh potential consulting clients or clients for our products, we want to be able to learn to identify when artificial intelligence should be used. Now, a lot of people think artificial intelligence is the fit for everything that you want to do. Well, that\'s really not the case. Artificial intelligence has certain use cases when it makes a lot of sense, and then it has other use cases when it doesn\'t make any sense at all. So we also want to learn what AI techniques should be used for different use cases. Things like retrieval augmented generation has a use case. Supervised fine-tuning has a use case. There\'s times you want to build MCP tools. There\'s times you want to build automations. So what we need to learn is what AI techniques should be used at the different use cases to solve different problems. And then when we\'ve decided what techniques we need to use, we want to learn what tools we are going to use to implement each technique. So what tools do you use for retrieval augmented generation? What tools do you use for supervised fine-tuning? This is an important thing that is part of our goals, things that we want to learn. So next thing we want to do is we want to be able to implement the AI techniques. So, we want to be able to learn what we\'re going to do and then we\'re going to learn how we\'re going to do it. And then most importantly, we need to be able to communicate the AI strategies and techniques effectively. It\'s not enough just to know about it to know how to do it, but you also have to be able to communicate it. So, these are our overall goals when we\'re talking about learning artificial intelligence. So these key areas is what is artificial intelligence and what can it do. So these are things that we need to know. So we need to know what artificial intelligence is, how it works and what it can do. And then you also we want to get the basic skills related to what you want AI to do for you. And we\'re going to learn by doing projects. So let\'s look at our road map. Now this is a custom road map that is different for every learner. So the first thing we\'re going to do is we\'re going to identify your interests by technology and project. You might be interested in building automations. You might be interested in building tools. You might be interested in nothing that actually has to do with building AI, but you want to be able to use AI to do things. So once we have identified your interests by technology and pro project, we\'re going to identify your current skill level. So where are you in the learner process? Now almost everybody\'s a beginner at something and everybody\'s an expert or not a beginner at other things. So what we need to do is based on technology and project type, what is your current skill level? Then we want to identify your target skill level. You don\'t necessarily need to become an expert in everything. You\'re going to based on what your career goals are or your current career objectives um identify what your target skill level is going to be. And then we\'re going to take those three things and we\'re going to consolidate these three things into a custom learning plan. And then we\'re going to execute the learning plan. We\'re going to evaluate your process. And then we\'re going to repeat. This is an iterative approach because pretty much by the time you get through from identifying your interests all the way to the end of evaluating your progress, AI is going to change. AI is going to change pretty much every week, every month, every couple of months. Uh everything that you\'re doing in artificial intelligence is subject to change. Uh if you are one of those people who want to learn something once and then just use it forever, artificial intelligence is not for you. This is going to be changing often. Now this is actually a benefit to you in your career because when things change, if you got behind on the technologies, this is giving you the opportunity to catch up because you\'re just going to skip what you didn\'t know before and you\'re going to go to the new stuff. So when we\'re talking about identifying your interest which is the first step what type of projects interest you. Now projects come into three different categories. There is using AI to build traditional products. There is taking traditional products and adding AI to them. So you\'re basically expanding the product or building things that are artificial intelligence for artificial intelligence. So you identify what type of projects you want to do. Then types of technologies and skills you want to learn. Do you want to become a Python programmer or do you just want to skip the whole thing and vibe code everything? Do you want to learn about supervised fine-tuning or is that just something that you\'re not interested in? So you\'re going to do this based on what is best for your career and your personal goals. Now when we\'re talking about the type of projects, I covered this a little bit. So, one is a traditional web-based or mobile app that you\'re going to build using artificial intelligence. And right now, this is kind of my favorite application because I\'m really into vibe coding. I\'m I\'m really trying to master the techniques of vibe coding. And we\'re not talking about vibe coding little applications. I\'m talking about vibe coding giant enterprise level applications. So, your first type is your traditional web-based or mobile app using AI. The second type is doing a foundational project using AI. So are you going to implement a rag project which is retrieval augmented generation? Are you going to in uh implement something like a supervised fine-tuning project or are you going to do something like a knowledge graph? So those are foundational artificial intelligence projects that are based on artificial intelligence and use different tools to implement them. Then the next type of tool is adding artificial intelligence to a traditional application. Now, I\'ve had a lot of a lot of good luck with processing documents and processing PDFs using large language models. So, if you\'re trying to read something like a medical record and before artificial intelligence, being able to parse that PDF and extract the document that the data that you need out of it was really a challenge. But with uh artificial intelligence, certain things that were really hard in traditional web-based or mobile apps suddenly gets a lot easier. And then another thing is building AI tools, building MCP tools. Now, I think this is going to be the home run in the short term. If you can build MCP tools and you\'ve got the vision as to what you should build, that is going to be way more profitable than uh the next one down, which is AI automations or AI agents. Because I think that when you\'re talking about selling to a large corporation or an enterprise, what you\'re looking at is an an organization that\'s willing to spend a lot of money to integrate their own data into artificial intelligence. So building AI tools with MCP is great. And then AI automations, I think I like those because they\'re kind of the magic tricks. You you do them right away and people just happen to love it. So now let\'s talk about the types of technologies and skills. So the first thing is building with AI. That\'s vibe coding. That is a very very popular. This is really where the future is going to be. A couple of months ago, if you would have asked me, I would have said, \"Yeah, I don\'t think this vibe coding is ready for prime time.\" Now I\'m like, well, there\'s challenges to it, but there\'s challenges with every programming language. So I treat vibe coding tools like my IDE. So those are the tools that I\'m going to use. Now, uh, the next thing is building AI tools. Now building your MCP servers and building background agents. Now you can do that in many different ways. So if you\'re building uh automations and you\'re doing using a visual tool, that\'s one way. But what we really want to be able to do is we want to be able to use the foundational programming languages and the software development kits to build MCP servers and agents that run in the background. And then the next type of skill is building AI projects, building workflows and automations and agents. Now on the school platform, there\'s over 200 communities based on building uh workflows and automations using N8N. We are not one of those. The reason we are not one of those is there\'s if that\'s what you want to do, there\'s plenty of places for you to learn that. And fundamentally, I do not see that as being a technology that has a long lifespan. It\'s going to be kind of like Dreamweaver. It was great for a couple years, then it was gone. Okay. And the next type of skills in technology is project architecture. Project architecture is really a big deal. It\'s you\'re talking about running uh large language models in the cloud. You\'re talking about run running large language models locally. So project architecture how you actually are going to implement and deploy applications. That\'s an important skill. That\'s an important uh area of focus that we\'re going to focus on. And then the last one is project management. project management in the artificial intelligence space is way different than traditional applications because if you let\'s say you use something like agile methodology where you do sprints normally sprints can be anywhere from uh two weeks to six weeks although I\'ve seen uh organizations that have three month sprints those are more like marathons but um you\'re talking about defining what you\'re going to do for the next build sprinting getting it done sprints go from pretty much a minimum of two weeks Now they\'re really two hours. So how do you manage a project where a sprint can be done within just a couple of hours? Something that might have taken a month to do would take a day to do. So those are those are going to be project management talent or u challenges and those are going to be things that we\'re going to want to make sure we cover and that\'s a focus area that is very important to to a lot of people\'s careers. Now, we always are going to start with fundamental knowledge that everybody should have. There\'s certain things you should know what a large language model is. You need to know what a context is. Even if you are never going to do context engineering and you\'re never going to do prompt engineering, you need to know what those things are. So, these are fundamental foundational knowledge that everybody should have. You should you need to know how the internet works. You need to know how websites work. You need to know how these things are deployed. those that is knowledge that regardless of what your area of focus is going to be, you\'re going to need to have that foundational knowledge. Now, in a lot of cases, you can start projects without having that, but for your career sake, it\'s something that you need to know. You\'re not going to be able to communicate projects without having this foundational knowledge. Now, this is going to start our beginner series. Now, our beginner series is really it\'s beginners for everybody because we\'re going to cover everything thoroughly and even if you\'re not a beginner, they may be things that you\'ve missed. You know, you might have been in tech for years and really not known how an IP address works or how DNS works. So, we\'re talking about learning things like your internet technologies, your basics of AI and machine learning. When somebody talks about training, it\'s always good to know what training actually is. We\'re going to talk about development technologies and tech stacks. You know, there\'s a lot of a lot of people are very opinionated about tech stacks and it\'s important to know the pros and the cons of different technologies that you use when you\'re either developing a website or you\'re developing AI technologies. Then we want to talk about the common web tools. The tools that everybody in AI is going to use. You need to use Git or GitHub. you need to use something like Google Collab if you\'re going to be learning Python or you\'re going to be doing supervised fine-tuning on the side without building the infrastructure. Hugging face is a place that you can go to get models and if you\'ve got there\'s a lot of pre-trained models out there. There\'s a lot of really good work out there and a lot of times that\'s where you\'re going to go to download your um models that you\'re going to run or you\'re going to run locally. Then we want to talk about using common desktop tools like how you use the development features in Google Chrome. How you use so that\'s dev tools how you use VS code how you use the terminal being able to navigate the terminal and then common networking tools how to to transfer files using FTP SSH and RDP. Now after the beginning series we get into the AI concept series. This is more things like how large language models work how these different architectures work. So not all large language models are the same. They\'re all built on the transformer architecture, but a GPT is way different than the way Gemini does it. So a dense model is way different than a mixture of experts model. So we want to learn about large language models. We want to learn how to select the right model for the use case. And then we want to be able to evaluate if a large large language model is right for whatever we\'re doing. So we\'re also going to talk about prompt engineering and context engineering. Prompt engineering is basically talking to the large language model. Context engineering is managing everything that the LLM has access to when it\'s solving a problem for you. So those are they\'re similar but different um technologies or concepts. Then we want to talk about AI process flow. Retrieval augmented generation and context augmented generation. They\'re not the same thing. Retrieval augmented generation uses querying a database. Context augmented generation means you know the documents that you\'re going to need to pass into the LLM ahead of time. Then we want to talk about AI process refinement. Uh supervised fine-tuning is really one of the tools techniques you use there. So in your process if you\'re not getting the output you want and you can\'t get that output be through prompt engineering or context engineering that\'s when you\'re going to do supervised fine-tuning. And then we\'re going to talk about workflows and agents. what\'s the difference between a workflow and an agent and what is a multi- aent system and then we\'re going to talk about setting goal AI goals and strategy because that\'s always really important. You don\'t want to bite off something that\'s too big for you. So next we\'re going to do the project learning roadmap. So how are we going to go about doing this? So the first thing we\'ve talked about is we\'ve talked about identifying your interest. So we\'re going to explore the technologies that are available. So you get a little bit of a background. to see what the different things are. Everybody should try a little bit of vibe coding. Maybe you\'re going to like it. Then after you\'ve explored the technologies, you\'re going to decide what you like. So, we\'re going to start simple and we\'re going to work up to more more complex. Now, if you can\'t decide, you want to explore different categories more in depth. So, what you need to do is you need to balance your interests with your opportunities. So, let\'s say you really love supervised fine-tuning, but there is no supervised fine-tuning work anywhere around you. You got to balance it. You know, you might be interested in something. Maybe you\'re really interested in game development. But you know what? Maybe there\'s no opportunities for you in game development. So, we\'re going to identify your interest and then balance your interest with your opportunities. Next thing we need to do is after you\'ve identified your interest, well, what skills are necessary for your interest? So if you\'ve got certain types of projects, we are going to be tying projects to skills. That is something that is very important. So when you say I want to work on a on a project and I want to work on let\'s say I\'m going to work on building a mobile app. Well, there\'s certain things you need to know about building a mobile app. So we want to make sure that we have the courses available for you that are your prerequisites for doing the projects that you\'re interested in. Then the next thing we need to say is identify what your skill level is at at each of these skills. So maybe you need a lot of you need to use a lot of Python. Maybe you already know enough or maybe you need to take some classes or some courses to get up to speed in the Python if that\'s what is tied to the project that you want to do. So then we got to look at what is the right path to reach each skill level. So is it doing YouTube videos? Is it doing projects? Is it doing our courses? Is it doing uh our quizzes and our feedback learning? So, if you need to reorder your interests, you probably should do that. So, if if you\'re interested in five different things, but one is a lot easier to get to, maybe that\'s the one you need to focus on. And then you want to look at projects that are the closest to your quickly achievable level. So, if maybe I only have to take one little course and study a few things to be able to do this type of project, that\'s what I should do first. Now the project learning process. Now we are launching a new platform called Skilix and we are really happy with this platform. It is actually taken a lot of the things that we\'ve learned by teaching artificial intelligence and realize that learning artificial intelligence is project-based. You will learn 10 times as much trying to do a project than you will actually reading about how to do that project. So you\'ll see what all the pitfalls are and you\'ll see what goes wrong in the project. So first thing you\'re going to do in the project learning process is you\'re going to choose a project that interests you. Then you\'re going to perform the lessons that at that apply to the project to achieve the necessary skills. Now you could just jump right into the project and then you know learn by fire. That is actually a valid approach. I\'ve taken that approach many times. But sometimes you need to have a lot of experience in tech if you\'re just going to jump in and try to do something without learning some background information yet. So I think our more experienced learners might want to just dive into the projects. Our less experienced learners want to perform some of the lessons first to achieve the necessary skills. Then you\'re going to do the project. Now none of the projects that are in our platform, you\'ll be the first to do. So everything with the exception of the super advanced crazy projects that I\'ll talk about later, these are all things that we\'ve done. So, I\'m not putting a project out there that either myself or Joe or one of our other people at our organization haven\'t already um performed. And this is something because we are a community. If you get to a project and you and there\'s a project that you\'re really interested in, you do it first, create the knowledge base and then um people will follow you and hopefully they\'ll do it better or at least do it as well as you did. So then what we\'re going to do is we\'re going to evaluate what you performed. So, if you built a project, um we\'re going to evaluate the project and determine what you needed and uh if it if there\'s any way that you could have done that project better. And then from there, we\'re going to perform a similar but more complex project. So, if you built a little mobile app, now we\'re going to build a medium-siz mobile app. If you built a mediumsiz mobile app, now we\'re going to build a big mobile app. So, we\'re going to perform a similar but a more complex project. Now, what is a learning project? There\'s different things you could do. You could take an existing project and just add a feature to it. Maybe we we have a website and we say, \"Hey, we want to add a page that does this.\" Just adding a simple feature. Another project is actually designing new features and projects. Because a lot of times if you\'re a project manager or a subject ma matter expert, you\'re not going to actually add the feature yourself, but you\'re going to define it and you\'re going to design it. You\'re going to be able to communicate to others what the feature needs to be. Another type of learning project is evaluating and reviewing a project. taking a look at a project and and identifying what can be done better in that project. That is actually a really important skill. That\'s actually the favorite skill I have because I love going in and throwing uh cold water on other people\'s projects. It\'s kind of fun sometimes. So, evaluating and reviewing an existing project is actually a um is a skill that you want to learn in a learning project. Another type of learning project is taking a project that somebody did and take doing it a completely different way. You know, let\'s say you did it with a traditional LAMP stack, which is a Linux, which is a traditional stack that\'s been used probably for the last 15 years. Maybe you want to do it using Next.js or React. Or maybe somebody did it in Nex.js or React because they\'re under the age of 30, and you just decided that, you know, I\'m I\'m old, so I\'m going to redo it, but I\'m going to redo it with old technologies. You know, doing an existing project with a different way is definitely a learning project. And then defining and completing an entire project. just sit down and say, \"I\'m building this SAS, my software as a service. I\'m going to build the entire thing from end to end.\" A lot of people like to start there. Um, you learn a lot if you try to do a an entire project, but I wouldn\'t say you use your best idea on your first project because you\'re going to have you\'re going to make a lot of mistakes and I can guarantee you that the first project you do is not something that you\'re going to want to deploy to production. Okay. Now the way our platform works and the way our new uh our new learning platform works is that everything is project collaboration. So you can start projects at any time. So anytime you join the school there\'s projects ready for you to go and you can always start a project. You don\'t have to be the first to do a project. If anything we recommend that you don\'t be the first to do a project because when other people do projects we expect them because this is a collaborative community. We expect them to make comments and posts about what they ran into and how they resolved it. Now, in our knowledge base, we actually have a retrieval augmented generation AI bot that is going to take the learnings that everybody has from all the projects they work on and allow you to ask questions. This is using something like the techniques behind claw desktop where you actually have an MCP server that goes out goes into our knowledge base and answers questions. So, um, seeing the comments and the post from those that came before you is really important and project collaboration. Now, if you\'re working on a project and there\'s other people are working on the same project, you could actually collaborate with them in real time or you can just pass ideas back and forth and, you know, get on Zoom calls and discuss how to get the project done better. Now, you want to repeat projects as your skills grow. So, if you do a project and I and I\'ve seen this throughout my entire career, I build a project and then a couple of months or years later, I look at it and go, \"Oh my god, what was I thinking? Uh, I didn\'t know what I was doing back then.\" And then I\'m going to go back and I\'m going to redo the project. And this time, I\'m going to redo the project with all the new things I\'ve learned. And I\'m going to do a better version of the projects I\'ve already done. And then anytime you do something in our community, we expect you to add to the knowledge base for those who are going to follow you. So if you run into a bunch of problems and maybe it takes you two days to solve some problem that you run into, make sure that the person behind you doesn\'t have to run into that same problem and that when they do, there is something in the knowledge base that you added that was good for the project. So the first thing you\'ve got to do is you\'ve got to determine what is achievable. Now what is achievable in artificial intelligence is basically anything. The skills that you that are that you need that are achievable are ways faster than you think. So if you think something is going to take you months to learn, you might learn it in a week. Everything about artificial intelligence moves faster than you think it\'s going to move. People who never thought they\'d be writing code are now vibe coding. And it\'s it\'s really crazy. People who think Python is so difficult. I\'m never going to be able to learn Python. Couple weeks later, they\'re doing Python. So all the projects that we put out there, we\'re going to do first. So that way we can identify what skills were necessary because I got to tell you, we\'re always surprised. There\'s never a project that we run into that we go, okay, we we know what\'s avail. Now projects usually follow a predictable pattern. So the required skills do not vary very much. So, you know, if we\'ve identified the required skills, they\'re probably going to be pretty close. It\'s it\'s not going to vary much from project to project. Okay. Do what you\'ve learned while you level up the next skill. So, if you\'re actually working in AI or you\'re working as a software engineer and you learn a bunch of skills because you\'ve done a project, keep using those skills and while you\'re leveling up to the next skill. So, you\'re going to operate in parallel. You\'re gonna you\'re going to do a project. You\'re going to start you\'re going to keep working on that technology while you\'re learning the next technology. Now, we\'re also in the pro community doing something we call crazy advanced group projects. These are the projects that push the limits. These are the projects that are I would consider hard, Joe would consider hard. Everybody I work with and AI would consider hard. These are not going to be easy projects because you know what? The harder the project you attempt, the more you\'re going to learn. So, we are going to push the limits with advanced projects. So, uh, one of the things we\'re going to do is we are going to repeat groundbreaking projects and we\'re going to do them better. So, if you\'ve heard of our Gemini Master CLI that is a fork of Gemini CLI from Google that actually runs local models. It\'s something we\'ve already worked on and now we\'re actually going to create our own um, coding tool from the ground up that uses local models. So this is going to be interesting and but it\'s what we\'re doing is somebody else did a groundbreaking project and now we\'re repeating it. Now the next thing we want to do is we want to do things that nobody has done before. So any on one of our calls recently somebody mentioned an MIT white paper. We discussed it a little bit. It was a a white paper on large language models that can learn. We tossed around some ideas and maybe that\'s a project that we\'re going to do that we\'re just going to do things that nobody has done before. And then we\'re also going to try things that they say can\'t be done because that\'s also fun. Sometimes when somebody says, \"Hey, you there\'s no way you\'re going to do this.\" I have several projects that I\'ve implemented that were the result of somebody saying it couldn\'t be done. And then it\'s just more fun to do with it that that way when uh somebody tells you you can\'t do it. And then we\'re going to define best practices for others to use. So when we\'re vibe coding super large projects, there\'s a whole lot of things we\'re going to run into and we\'re just going to define those best practices. So, the next thing I want to do is the educational platform demo, but I\'m going to put that in another video because that has actually changed a couple times even since I demoed this on a live on our live call. We\'ve added some features. It\'s actually really kind of cool. So, this is actually a pro um a presentation I did on diffusion models. So, I\'m basically going to end this part here, but uh I\'m really excited to have you in the community. You\'re going to learn a lot.'),
(526, 150, 'HTML in 10 Minutes', 'Learn the basics of HTML in 10 minutes.', 'This video from SkillShare presents an introduction to HTML in 10 minutes. As an interesting side note, they were the first training company to reach out to me on instagram to partner, so I probably should respond.', 'text', 10, 1, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/MDLn5-zSQQI', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-27 01:59:08', '2025-07-27 01:59:09', NULL, 'Automatic transcript retrieval requires OAuth authentication. Please use OAuth flow or manually add transcript. Video title: HTML Tutorial For Beginners |HTML In 10 Minutes | HTML Basics For Beginners | Simplilearn'),
