(546, 161, 'Free Community Live Event - 7/5/25', 'This was our first free live event.', '', 'text', 65, 19, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/23RBl-v8vdQ', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-28 20:39:51', '2025-07-28 20:39:52', NULL, 'and you are live at all. Hey everybody, welcome. So, uh this is uh another introduction call, but we\'re going to do a lot more stuff that\'s actually technical today. I want to start going through things. Um but most importantly, if anybody hasn\'t uh wasn\'t on last week\'s call, I\'m at Honor. I I\'m the founder of the community. You probably found me through Instagram. Uh I\'m going to go through the introduction part really fast. I you know I\'ve been doing uh tech work since 1986. I graduated from the University of Illinois in 1986. Took me two years to get a job. So I very much understand what the technical environment is today. I\'m running into a lot of either uh computer science majors who are coming out of college and obviously with artificial intelligence being more uh recent than their freshman year, it\'s really hard for anybody to be keeping up with it. So my background is as a consultant and I do consulting today which is um one of the reasons I\'m really good at identifying what\'s going wrong in the artificial intelligence industry because anytime you have you\'re doing projects with large organizations and the project is going to span six months to a year but the technologies are going to change six times during the uh during the project you just run into all sorts of project management issues. Now, um, so the reasons that we created the community and we think that it\'s really important to have a community where we can share ideas is a we have the economic parallels to what I ran into in 1988 when I got out of college and couldn\'t find a job. And basically, the only place you could learn what you really needed to know was on the job. So, if the economy wasn\'t perfect and they weren\'t hiring graduates, um, there wasn\'t really any way to learn things. So, we have the economic parallels to what I was running into when I first got out of college. And then we have the technology parallels to what it was like, you know, between 1998 and 2001 when the web was taking off. It was changing really fast. Nobody knew what to do. Everybody was getting funded. Now, if you, you know, it\'s kind of the same thing is going on today when it comes to funding. You know, you had all these people, all these projects that were getting funded, but really nobody knew how to deliver them. And then when there was the big.com crash, you got this big consolidation into, you know, what a lot of what you\'re seeing today. And so, you know, colleges are great and you learn a lot of really good background information. And because when I was at the University of Illinois, we were part of the uh liberal arts department. Uh they were telling us that we\'re not teaching you to have a job, we\'re teaching you how to learn so that when you do have a job, they can teach you. That is actually really wise. At the time I\'m surpris you know at the time I was kind of annoyed because it was really making it hard to get a job. The fact that we were using technologies in co college that nobody was using themselves. But what we came up with is we came up with a different learning approach and our learning approach is ba based on really two sides. You know the first of all is everybody needs to know the basics. Now, you can go and you can learn how to do NADN, you can learn cursor, you can learn claude code, and you, you know, you can learn vibe coding, and you could probably build some really nice stuff, but when you go out to sell it, if you can\'t have a conversation with your client, or you\'re always going to run into these roadblocks. There\'s always a guy in the security department. There\'s always a guy in the infrastructure who wants to have an argument with you over the fact that you\'re using let\'s say you\'re using Claude and they think you should be using Grock and you know you just you need to know the basics. You need to be able to talk to talk in order to walk the walk when you go in. Whether you\'re going in as a client, you know, or you\'re whether you\'re talking to clients, whether you\'re talking to you\'re selling a product, you\'re going to run into somebody who\'s going to challenge you on your basic knowledge or if you\'re if you\'re the one in your organization bringing an artificial intelligence and you\'re going to your manager and they\'re they ask you random questions about um buzzword bingo. I mean, buzzword bingo in artificial intelligence is a game that everybody\'s playing. You go to I\'ve been to parties where I\'ve heard more buzzwords in a single sentence than anybody can actually believe. So, part of what we\'re doing is we\'re teaching you buzzword bingo. You know what what we did is we created an an an application and I\'ll jump over to it now. About 50 of you have definitely already played with it. Um, let me get this out of my way. Um, all right. I gotta play a little bit around here because the Zoom controls make it really hard for me to find my my page. Okay. So, we create a we created a learning um a reinforcement learning module where as you\'re going through the different parts of the course, you can do these practice sessions where let\'s say I\'m going to go in and I\'m going to pick um augmented AI agents. I go in and do start a practice. Hold on. My mouse. Let me do it this way. All right. So, basically, it just starts asking you questions and every time you get a question wrong, you lose a half a point. Every time you get one right, you get a point. And so the reason for this is basically and I\'m guessing which is really going to keep me off the leaderboard. Um the reason for this is just being reinforcement learning. The way reinforcement learning works for a large language model is you keep drilling it over and over again with information until it learns the information. And so because gamification is such a big deal with the way people are learning today, we have a leaderboard. Let\'s look at the leaderboard. Okay, Alexandra is winning so far. She\'s got 607 correct, 35 wrong. But what happens as you\'re doing this is it\'s kind of a flashc card thing and it allows you to know when somebody throws terms at you what those terms mean. And our reason for doing this is really there\'s just so many topics that there\'s so many topics in artificial intelligence and there\'s so many buzzwords and people just throw things out at you randomly. You know, I\'m using this SDK or whatever. I\'m doing this thing. Being able to just have that background information is just a way to um you know whether you\'re selling your product or you\'re selling to your client you\'re you\'re you\'re trying to convince somebody to do a project your way this is just the background information we want to do. So our learning approach is based on that. And then the other part is that live is always better than video. Um, you know, I don\'t know, we\'ve got um 221 participants right now who have identified that they could go to YouTube and probably get a lot of the same information that I\'m going to do uh here. But live really it forces you to think in real time and it\'s really better. It\'s I think it\'s just a better way of learning. And now I\'ve got a big X over here of the our MIT professional education certificate that Joe and I did back in 2021. This was a this was a threemonth MIT professional education thing that I think it took years off my life. That is when I learned how much uh I am a terrible student. And uh Joe was in college much more recently than me. So he did a he did a lot better. I think overall he was like number eight in the class and I was number 15 but there were 1,500 people in the class and it was a lot of what we\'re doing in these communities is from what we learned in those communities. We learned so much math of on and I\'ve never used it since. And so we\'re really focusing a lot more on making sure that you\'re learning stuff that\'s relevant because you don\'t have time to learn linear algebra, gradient descent. You don\'t you just don\'t have uh the time actually to learn all those things. So you know, our focus is everybody needs to know the basics. We want to come up with some tools and we\'re we\'re going to come up with more and more tools for how to learn, you know, how to get the reinforcement of this information. Um, and then live is always better. Now, we\'re also going to be doing, and this is more for the professional community, and I I caught nothing but grief last week for not having the URL to the pro community on uh on this screen. So, um, there it is. Uh now what we\'re doing is individual and group projects. We want people to be able to work independently because every project I\'m currently working on in artificial intelligence is technically a group project but it\'s actually Hold on. There we go. I muted him twice. All right. Um so so what we\'re doing and this is a lot of what we\'re doing over on the professional community is we have a bunch of projects that we\'re working on and we\'re having a bunch we have a bunch of projects that our um clients are bringing us our investment bankers are bringing us that um we just don\'t have time to do and so we\'re going to toss them into the community see if they can you know see who can do them give you the access to additional resources in the pro community if you need access to a virtual machine. We actually have our own data center where we have we can build VMs. We have access to GPUs and we\'re really doing a lot of stuff to build your resume. And that\'s really our learning approach is we only want to teach things or we really want to cover things that are going to help you in your career because this is a very very competitive space right now. I don\'t know. I\'ve made a couple of reels lately about the fact that Meta has been poaching um resources for $und00 million. So Meta went out and they offered 14 people $100 million to uh join Meta. Now um that is because there is a lack of resources in this space. Now it\'s also because those are all people who could get foundational models funded. If you know if any of those 14 people went to the investment bankers I know and said uh we\'ve got this idea and it could basically be an idea for anything they\'d get a hundred million $200 million in funding just by showing up. So you know it\'s a very very competitive market right now and you know it\'s there\'s a lot of fear uncertainty and doubt. So we\'re we\'re doing everything we can to take away that fear, uncertainty and doubt. So our road map is basically learning the background theory, learning what transformer models are, what large language models are, learning all that background information so that you can talk the talk and then from there the AI the the basic AI concepts which I don\'t like the list I have here. I have vibe coding, prompt engineering, context engineering and local models. That is really context engineering is going to be it looks like context engineering is going to be the title for software engineers going forward. They are going to be context engineers or software context engineers. And that is basically because vibe coding has gotten to the point where it is actually credible. And I\'m going to finish this presentation today talking about cloud claude code and how to get started with both claude code and Gemini CLI because when you start working with those tools that\'s when the epiphany kicks in that coding with AI is real. So you know at first I I was one of the first people to install GitHub C-Pilot and I absolutely hated it and I uninstalled it. Then I did a lot of coding where I would copy and paste my code into chat GPT and that was a horrible way to work. And then I brought got into cursor which is basically uh VS code wrapped around LLMs and that was pretty good. That was really good. But then when cursor then when claude came up or anthropic came up with claude code that just came to a whole new level because every complaint I had about cursor was fixed in claude code and it actually has completely changed the way I approach vibe coding when I\'m looking at um how I want to do a project. So that um reinforcement learning module that we had that was 100% vibecoded. Joe and I decided we were not going to write a single line of code in that ourselves. We were going to let cursor do the whole thing and most that was mostly done with the cursor free version. So one of the projects we want to do is we want to say all right let\'s start this project over let\'s rewrite it but let\'s plan it out properly. Let\'s use cloud code and you you\'ll see exactly how everything goes. Now once you\'ve explored the basic AI concepts you everybody should pick a focus but I there\'s a limited number of focuses at this point the it\'s really are you going to build things or are you going to manage the building of things so let me just continue when we\'re talking about the the projects that we\'re currently working on working on projects break can really be broken into two parts and hold on one Second, I have a ser I have a server behind me that uh decided it wanted to wake up. So, when we\'re talking about projects, they\'re really coming down into two categories. Building AI products and tools or building with AI. Now, mostly people come to me with tools that they want where they think AI is an integral part of the the final application, but really it\'s it\'s not it it\'s just in the building with AI series, it\'s more if you\'re if you\'re doing a SAS project and you want to build it out, build it out using AI. Uh we\'ve got projects that we\'ve been working on the healthcare and financial markets. Um a lot with healthcare. We\'ve been doing a lot with local large language models in health care because of the HIPPA requirements that you can\'t share patient data in the United States. We\'ve been doing stuff with financial markets because we have a good background in data science. So, predictive analytics in the financial markets and then in the health care markets um really doing a lot with retrieval augmented generation. We\'ve been working on longevity um and then also data and data warehousing projects where we\'re building things to access Oracle databases uh document databases and then a lot with the knowledge graphs and um nodes and edges and all of that stuff. So if we\'re talking about building AI project products to me the biggest area right now is building MCP tools. Now, if you\'ve ever built an MCP tool and that is model context protocol with model context protocol tools, what they are is they give large language models the ability to use tools. So, if you install claw desktop on one of your users machines and you build MCP tools that can access local data, can access their email, access their SharePoint, um use local APIs maybe to get to your Oracle database or your DB2 database. Um, MCP tools are what give large language models the power to do things. And why I think that is such a growing area is large organizations do not have their data on in the cloud because they never shared their data. Their data has always been proprietary. But it doesn\'t do them a lot of good. Let\'s say let\'s pick pharmaceutical for instance. Well, the large language model up in the cloud has a lot of background information about pharmaceuticals, but it knows nothing about the internal projects that are being worked on inside the organization. So you can build MCP tools using the um MCP Python SDK or they have a Java SDK, but you can build these tools that allow the large language model to go out and get that information in real time. And that is absolutely powerful. It\'s almost like a magic trick when you show that to clients when you you know I have one that I built where I just access the um the SEC Edgar database and you can ask real time it questions about filings that are filings that actually happen today because you\'re actually reaching out using the tool to the large to uh d to an external database. So MCP tools I think are the biggest opportunity for people in or large organizations or people who sell to large organizations to really be effective and efficient and and if if making money is your goal, MCP tools are a place to go. The second thing is automations and I talk about automations because there are 200 communities on school for building automations with NADN and automations are great. Again, it\'s like a magic trick. When you show somebody an automation, they are usually wowed, but there I I feel like automations right now are very premature. You can do things that are great, but what happens is um you\'re building them with tools that may or may not be around in a couple of years. And that is a Hold on a second. Something\'s beeping. Um that is a um that is an area that is great. We should all be looking at building automations. The question is the jury I think is still out on how. Now what we\'re doing a lot with claude code and cursor is we\'re building automations in those frameworks. So we\'re not using the visual frameworks to build automations. Now make and naden are very very powerful and and people really like them but I\'m really having trouble finding companies that are willing to deploy automations with NAN or with make. So that\'s a second area and then APIs and SDKs local AP. So when you run a model locally and you run a model um using lama or llama.cp CPP or VLLM. When you\'re running it locally, you have APIs that are basically clones of the APIs that were generated in version one of Chat GPT. So you basically just have the completions API to work with. Now the SDKs that the cloud models have are far superior to the SDKs that you have on the um Joe, can you do some muting? Um, got him. Sorry, he was off my screen. Okay, so it so the a the APIs for local models are really poor compared to the APIs for the cloud models. So there is a huge opportunity to build those APIs in the for the local model because I have clients right now who will not allow uh cloud LLMs to be used at their organizations but they have a lot of money so they are interested in having um local LLMs but like one example is named entity recognition. So if you\'re doing named entity recognition, the uh chat GPT latest version of the SDK, you can do named entity recognition in one line of code. If you\'re doing it manually through APIs, you\'re writing a lot of code. So those type of tools are really important projects and it\'s an opportunity whether you\'re building them to sell them or you\'re building them to be a consultant for an organization. building these kind of tools really get you get you grounded. So the next thing I want to talk about okay so now I want to talk about getting into you know getting into the meat and potatoes of this getting into the things that you need to have. Now, everybody needs to, you know, hardware selection is a really tough decision for a lot of people and you can basically do artificial intelligence projects working on a Chromebook all the way up to the most powerful MacBook Pro or you can also do it with Linux boxes. We\'ve got a Linux box in the other room that was making all that noise that\'s got eight GPUs on it. that\'s probably not most people\'s uh development environment when you go when you\'re looking at hardware selection. Uh my recommendation is if you can use Mac products and you want to run local LLMs, I\'m a big Apple I I have been called an Apple fanboy online very very often because I am a I am definitely somebody who uses a Mac. At the same time, I have a whole stack of Windows computers that my clients make me use because they won\'t allow a personal device on their network. So, your hardware selection, get as much RAM