(552, 162, 'Beginners Live Event 7/16/26', 'An introduction to Internet and Web Topics.', '', 'text', 0, 1, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/m9IG19J6Hcs', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-28 20:56:40', '2025-07-28 20:56:41', NULL, 'Yeah, the AI companion is running. Um, and the recording will be available for future reference as well. There is a new um there\'s a new uh course for those. I don\'t know if Ed, if you want to I\'ll tab and show that new course where you\'re posting the live ones. [Music] Yes, I got all this stuff in my way. Um, I\'ll just go to this one. And while you\'re there, I\'ll tell you about the other one that I added at the bottom as well. Right next to yours down here. Yeah. See, at the bottom we have the live event replays. Um, that\'s where all live event recordings will go. And then I added uh community links. These were a couple links that people were asking for. school has the issue of only being able to pin three posts. So, um I had a lot of uh direct uh DMs about asking for these links and also in various chat post. So, I just put them in there. It has the the link to the git, the link to the reinforcement learning and a link to the um uh Stripe payment portal for those that did not subscribe through school. And for the record, um I\'m not that thrilled about school as a platform. So what I\'m vibe coding over here in my free time is the ability to actually link courses to projects. So when you create a project, we can then say these are the prerequisite courses you should take if you\'re going to work on that project. So if you want to build out let\'s say the the Gemini CLI, these are the four or five courses you should master the skills it before you try to do that. But let me get started since uh it is 7:01. So I\'m going to play from start. Okay. So this is our beginner background series. And the idea here is that if you\'re going to do artificial intelligence, most of it is communicating with the with the AI. So, you\'re going to interface with your artificial intelligence through prompting, but without proper vocabulary and knowing what you\'re talking about, you\'re not going to be able to communicate effectively with the AI. And that really becomes very obvious when you\'re vibe coding with something like cursor or with claude code or Gemini CLI because as you\'re debugging and you get to the point where you you\'re trying to get the point across to the LLM that it needs to move this card over to the right. If you\'re using the proper terminology, you know, if you\'re saying move this div tag or move this, move any div with a class of this, if you\'re using the proper terminology, then you\'re going to be able to communicate with the AI a lot better. You\'re going to be able to get your point across and you\'re going to spend a lot less time debugging. And so there\'s certain basics that where you just kind of need to know both to use the tools because some of these tools are actually really more complicated. They\'re they\'re tools that really help you out, but when you use them, there\'s a lot of background information about both traditional tech and artificial intelligence that you um you really need to know in order to be that. So the goal of this today and or this the beginner series in general is to learn enough to start doing simple projects and then work your way up. So the more you learn, the more um the more projects you can do. And we really want this to be a project driven education. You\'re going to learn by doing versus learn by listening to me ramble for an hour at a time. So the idea at the background is is we want you to understand what the AI is doing when it writes code. We want you to understand what it\'s doing when it\'s doing an automation. If it\'s building an MCP tool, whatever it\'s doing, we want you to have that understanding of what the AI is doing. So it\'s not just off running wild without you having some idea of what it needs to do. This is also to give you a basic understanding of the ecosystem that supports it because when we talk about local models versus cloud models, we really should know the difference between those and so it\'s a lot of this is getting the knowledge that is necessary to understand the tools. So the first place I am going to start is with traditional computing and I am going back a ways and I apologize to everybody who already knows all this stuff but I think it\'s something that we really need to just make sure that we have the foundation and that we\'re all at the at the same place. So the way computers work is it goes back to a transistor. So transistors were uh invented in like the 1940s and it basically allows you to have ones and zeros. So if electricity is going through the transistor, it\'s a one. If electricity is not going through the transistor, it\'s a zero. So with putting together a bunch of transistors, you can then represent letters and numbers in binary code, which is ones and zeros. So in this case, everything in the computer is represented as bin binary. So a character A is 0 1 0 0 0 1 and the number five is represented by 4 0 1 0 1. So you can represent data, you can represent characters, numbers, anything you want to represent. You can represent them as big long strings of ones and zeros. Now in addition to representing things as ones and zeros, there\'s a thing called a logic gate. And those those say and or or not. So if you if you look at boolean logic, you know, A and B, A or B, not A or not B, you\'ve got the ability to basically create um process flow. So between by having the ability to represent everything in ones and zeros and basically represent it in your computer and then having logic gates where you have and ors or knots that allows the creation of traditional programming languages. Now there\'s different kinds of programming languages and the first programming languages that came out were procedural programming languages and for most web development you can kind of get away with using procedural programming languages. PHP which is my language of choice whenever I want to do a lot of code and I want to do it fast is a procedural programming language. The things you need to do are organized as procedures, functions, and subutines. So I might write a procedure that says delete record or get this or do this and those would be procedures. And procedural programming languages follow a linear flow. They just it goes through the code. Now you have you could have loops and conditionals, but it really is focusing on how a task is performed. So procedural languages are pretty much all we had up until like the early 198 or probably the late 1980s when we got object-oriented languages. Now object without objectoriented languages today there would be no artificial intelligence and object-oriented languages better represent how the world is structured. So you have these things called classes and classes are basically a blueprint. So let\'s say you have a class called a car. Okay. And objects. So you have a class called the car. And the class is the blueprint. Everything that is a car has these attributes. It has these things about it that make it a car. Objects are instances of a class. So if I have a 100 cars, we have a hundred objects of the car class. Now this is this gets really interesting when you look at these things called inheritance and polymorphism. So uh an electric car is also a car. So an electric car let\'s say a Tesla that is inheriting all of the attributes of a car which means it has four tires. It has uh a motor of some sort that makes it go. It can go forward and in reverse. It has all the things that a car has. but because it inherited what a car is, but now it\'s overridden some of the the the things that make a car a car. So instead of having a gas engine, you\'re replacing the gas engine with the electric motor. And that is where the idea of classes and um objects, that\'s how the whole thing works. So what it allows us to do is build on each other\'s code. And what makes that super important in artificial intelligence is the fact that now you don\'t need to know complex math. You don\'t need to know uh linear algebra. You don\'t need to know gradient descent. You don\'t need to know all these things these that these mathematicians put together. Now I personally am somebody who doesn\'t really not really good at math. Okay. I I got a degree in math. I I learn it well enough to get through it, but I don\'t do it very well. Okay. But I don\'t have to because I know I know what I\'m doing. I don\'t necessarily know how to actually do it. So when you have these functions and you have functions that can build upon each other. So for instance, PyTorch is a library that was created by Google for artificial intelligence and it has a bunch of classes in it for doing different things. So if I want to do a ukitian diff distance between two vectors, all I need to know is that I want to do a ukitian distance between two vectors. I happen to know that shows how similar those two vectors are or but I don\'t actually have to know how to do the math and that\'s how over the period of let\'s say the last 10 years artificial intelligence has gotten really really powerful because there are people who write these classes and they write these class libraries and then there\'s other people who don\'t necessarily know how the classes work but they know how to include them in their code to make things better and better and better. So that\'s how objectoriented languages are making artificial intelligence move so fast because you don\'t have to know everything. You just have to know what to grab and what to use. Now once you get to this point this is where data science comes in. Now in traditional programming you are talking it\'s mostly if then logic. So, if I type the word um if I type a word into the terminal and it\'s and I have a save button, it it knows through ifs and thens and else\'s what to do. Data science is the art of turning raw data into useful information. What you\'re doing with data science and machine learning is you\'re working in the opposite direction. You\'re taking a big pile of data and you\'re looking for the patterns in the data and then you\'re training the model in machine learning to do to to replicate basically it\'s writing the code from the data instead of you writing the code that generates the data. So data science is basically the foundation of artificial intelligence. So, we got to this point where we had computers, we had object-oriented programming, and now we\'ve got data science where you\'re talking about all these different mathematicians who came up with these ideas of decision trees and random forest and convolutional neural networks. And that was the basics of artificial intelligence, which is where you have these things called neural networks. Now, neural networks have been around for a while, but it wasn\'t until maybe the last 15 years or so that that computing power was really good enough to do what you wanted to do with neural networks. So, this picture I have is a picture of a neural network. And each of the circles is called is called well, it\'s a neuron, but it\'s actually called a node. Okay? So, you have weights and biases. Okay? So each of these um layers are the weights and that is a a number between zero and one and then each of these lines is a resistor. Now Frank Rosenblat back in 1958 realized that if you this is what\'s actually called a feed forward neural network. And if it kind of like works like your eyes do. If you put in impul inputs into the input layers, it goes through all this mathematics and then figures out something on the other end. So when you hear the term we\'re training the model, training the model means determining the weights and the biases for all these lines and all these dots to to make it know things and to make it do things. So the reason when you talk about training a model, it might take two or three million dollar worth of electricity to train a model, the number of these circles plus the number of these lines is the number of parameters. So when somebody says I have a 1.7 trillion parameter model, that means there\'s a combination of these dots and these lines that adds up to 1.7 trillion of them. So you\'ve got some really um some really complex math. There\'s uh linear algebra and some calculus related to gradient descent that makes this possible. Now this was not possible for the most part until GPUs came into existence. And GPUs are the graphics processing unit. and they are much better at doing a certain type of mathematics in wildly parallel form. So a CPU if you think so a CPU versus a GPU think of a a CPU as an expressway with eight lines or eight lanes uh an 8 core CPU would have eight lanes of traffic. A GP but and it it can handle anything. It could take trucks. It could take uh tractors, cars, cars of any size. Anything can be on a CPU. It\'s a very generalpurpose highway. A GPU on the other hand does only one certain type of calculation, but it does millions of them at the same time. So when you are calculating the all these weights and the biases in the neural network, you need this massive parallel processing. And one of the big barriers to entry of being a somebody who creates foundational models is having the GPU resources to be able to train the model and calculate all these weights and biases. Now in 2018 everything changed. Now, I wish that we would have Joe and I would have known about this paper in 2018 because in 2021, we were taking a threemonth class at MIT that was completely made obsolete by this 2018 paper. We had absolutely no idea that everything that we were learning about data science was completely obsolete already. uh but nobody had released Chad GBT. So the transformer model is basically the model that all large language models are based on. Now it doesn\'t matter whether it\'s open AIS with Chad GBT or anthropic with Claude or uh X with Grock. They\'re all based on the transformer model, but they\'re different implementations of the transformer model. So when you hear a GPT, which is a generalized pre-trained transformer, is not the same as what Claude does or what Gemini does. They\'re they\'re different implementations of the same type of transformer model. Now, the whole discussion on how models are trained and all that, I\'m going to save that for another lesson because what I want to get into now is the basics that you need to know to be able to be to do decent vibe coding and to be able to write to use AI to generate whatever it is you want to generate. Whether you want to create tools for uh model context protocol, if you want to build automations, if you want to build applications like traditional applications, this is the kind of information that you\'re going to need in order to just be able to talk, walk the walk and talk the talk with the large language model. So, I\'m starting with what is the internet? Now the internet is basically a giant network that connects everything. Okay? So it connects your smartphones, it connects your laptops, it connects your your PCs, all of these devices that were up here is part of the internet. And a very big part of the internet is something called the internet of things. Okay? And that is traffic lights and cameras, sensors, electric meters, your home devices like your smart TVs, they\'re all on the internet, your mobile tech like all the cars, the drones, the wearable devices, all of this stuff is allworked together and theoretically they can all talk to each other. Now, every one of these devices that\'s listed up here has an address. It was called an IP address. Now, at one point in history, they got to the point where they were about to run out of IP addresses. So, they moved from IPv4 to IPv6, which gives it pretty much an unlimited number of IP addresses. Now, any device like the laptop that I\'m on right now doing this presentation, it has more than one address. It has a public address that\'s visible to the world, which that address is basically the address of the router that\'s in the other room. And then it has a private address that\'s visible inside my local network in the office. And then it has an internal address that when I have one program that wants to talk to another program, it can communicate on that address. Now we\'ve got our addresses and now we have something called ports. Think of ports like doors and windows to the location. So if the addresses you\'re building, the ports are the doors and the windows. And you\'re going to use ports a lot, especially when you\'re using a lot of the tools that come with artificial intelligence. And each door or each port has a special purpose. Now, for web traffic, it\'s port 80 and port 443, which for the most part, it\'s just port 443 because if you do HTTP, it\'s port 80. If you do HTTPS as port 443 and then so each port has a special use. So, when you\'re developing software, sometimes you\'re integrating with an API, which is an application programming interface. It\'s not necessarily going to be listening on port 80 or port 443. It might be listening on port 8080 or some other port that was specifically designed for use for that for that information. So whatever port you\'re connected to is the basically determines the language that the communication is going to be done over that port. And then port 25 is email. So now domain names. Okay. So now now what we have is we have a network of all these things. All these things have addresses. All these addresses have doors and windows. Now I\'m old enough to remember a thing called the phone book where if you wanted to call if you wanted to call your grandmother, you and you didn\'t know her number, you had this book and it had all the names and it would give you the phone number. Well, domains are like an easy to remember name that\'s related to an address. So, google.com is a name, but somewhere there\'s an IP address with a web server that is hosting google.com. And the domain name service, the DNS is basically the phone book that the web browser goes to to say, \"Hey, I want to go to google.com.\" and it says, \"Oh, that\'s at 1717 1717 1717 17 and returns the address of where the traffic needs to be routed.\" So, anytime you enter a URL, which is the address that you type into the browser bar, the domain name resolves to a physical address behind the scenes. So the combination of whether you put HTTP or HTTPS in the browser and then the domain name tells it what port to go through and then it it it establishes the conversation between the browser and the web server. Now one thing that\'s really important especially when you\'re doing development is your computer is always called localhost. So when you are writing your own code or when you\'re vibe coding and you have a little web server that\'s running on your computer so that the code that you\'re writing is talking to the web server that you have it\'s talking on something called localhost and that\'s also the IP address of uh 127.0.0.1 I think. So, um, yeah, space there. My brain\'s fried. Okay, so you\'re going to very often hear the terms cloud versus local. Now, when we talk about cloud models, we\'re talking about models that are not located on our premises. They\'re located in the cloud. Now, cloud has another connotation which basically means you don\'t really know where it is. So the cloud actually means using the internet to store and access data apps and service uh instead of keeping them only on your own device. So a cloud model is a model where the inference which is the running of the model is happening somewhere other than where you are. So when you hear about organizations that don\'t want to send their prompts to the cloud, that\'s mostly because they don\'t want to send their prompts and their proprietary data to some place that they have no control over. Because once that prompt leaves your organization and leaves your no local network, you don\'t know if it\'s being intercepted. You don\'t know how many hops it\'s taking to the large language model. We know that Chat GPT is turning over all of their prompts and all of their responses to the Southern District of New York because of a lawsuit with um with the New York Times. So, if you do if you have some embarrassing prompts and some embarrassing response, well, the Southern District of New York now has your prompts and your responses. Now, if you\'re using something like retrieve augmented generation where you\'re sending not only the prompt, but you\'re sending your own corporate data to the cloud, that\'s something else that a lot of companies don\'t want to leave their organization. So, when you when you hear the term that you\'re running something locally, it means that you\'re running it on either your own computer or your own private network. So when I say um McDonald\'s Corporation wants to run their LLM locally, it doesn\'t necessarily mean on the individual laptops. It could just mean at a data center that only they have access to. And so the difference between your cloud models and your local models or something being in the cloud or something being locally, it\'s a very important distinction because you\'re going to hear that it running local or it running on the cloud is a prerequisite sometimes as for the requirements for what it is you\'re trying to to access. So, what is a website? Now, originally, back when I learned what a website was back in like 1994, they\'re basically billboards. So, you would go to an address through the uniform resource locator or the URL, and it would display information about whatever it was you\'re looking for. And there\'s a thing that\'s in HTML called an anchor tag or an A tag. and that is links to other pages. So it when it was called the worldwide web, which I don\'t know if that name is still around or it\'s gone, the reason it got the name worldwide web is every website would have links to other websites and you could basically just navigate around the internet by clicking on links. And usually at the time you would go back to Yahoo and then at the bottom of Yahoo was Google and it was how you would find what you were looking for to go to. So when you have a website, the websites are sitting at a on a a server somewhere on the internet that has an address and it is running specialized software that when it gets a request going to it based on the URL that you gave it, it\'s going to return data. Now, the web server is the software that\'s sitting at the address. And for the most part, it\'s usually listening on port 443 or port 80. And in the case I have here where I say httpacample.com, it means it\'s going to port 80. If it\'s httpsacample.com, it means go to port 4443. Now you have the opportunity to put a colon at the end of it and special and specify the port. And when you\'re working on AI projects and you\'re working locally, you\'re going to do that a lot. You\'re going to use colon port number because that basically is allowing you to do multiple things on the same computer running the same protocols. Now the other thing that makes an address unique is the prefix. So www.agample.com could be a completely different website in a completely different part of the world than api.agample.com. So the prefixes, so that entire address all the way up to the colon or the next forward slash is the address that you\'re going to and the domain name server or domain name server. The DNS is going to resolve that to a specific address. Now so what happens when you go to a website? So the first thing that happens is the browser looks up the real address from the URL. Then it connects to the web server and it delivers its payload. So when you\'re when you\'re writing an application, the get is what everything that is on the URL. So if you have a URL that says example.comward a= or uh example.comx.php question mark a= 7 b= 6. Those are what\'s called the get request. Those are the variables that are being passed in using that method. Now there\'s a second type of way uh the payload is delivered to the web page which is a post and that\'s usually form data and that\'s data that\'s being sent behind the scenes. Now, when you\'re using URLs, it\'s always preferable to use a post over a get because it\'s much harder to intercept. The post is actually handled different because you you don\'t actually see it in the URL. Now, another thing that gets delivered to the web server every time you go to a website is something called a cookie. Now, the cookies, you\'ve probably heard the term tracking cookies. Cookies are the ways that companies keep track of you because a cookie is persistent after you leave the browser. So if you go to Google is the worst at this or let\'s go let\'s say Amazon. You go to Amazon and you go you start looking at things. Okay. And then you say wow I\'m sure getting a lot of advertisements for that thing I was just looking at. Uh, for instance, right now I go into Facebook or I go into Instagram and I\'m getting a ton of advertisements for school.com because I just couple weeks ago bought something from school.com. They don\'t know that I actually bought, but they do know that I went to the website because of these cookies. And this is what\'s stored on my computer to let them track me. So, but again, you\'re going to want to use these cookies in your application. So they\'re not all bad. And then the other way that the browser delivers its p payload is something called JSON. Now JSON stands for JavaScript object notation. And it\'s a way to send unstructured text. And when you\'re working with APIs or application programming interfaces, JSON is the format that is pretty much agreed upon throughout the universe of this is how you\'re going to send data. So you deliver your payroll to payload to the web server and then the web server returns HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Excuse me. Now it sends that back because that\'s what the browser knows how to understand or what to understand. It only understands HTML, CSS and JavaScript. So it doesn\'t matter if you wrote your application in Ruby or Rust or PHP or Angular or TypeScript. When it gets back to the browser, it\'s coming back as HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. And that\'s an important distinction because we have a lot of people and I have a lot of people who definitely uh say so in uh comments on Instagram that they have a tool of choice. And if you\'re starting out with artificial intelligence and you\'re starting out with vibe coding where you\'re having AI write the code for you, the simpler the programming languages you choose, the better off you\'re going to be. You don\'t want to be coding in React or Nex.js or Angular if you\'re just starting out. Those are programming languages that take humans um a year to learn. So the idea that you\'re just going to start out, you\'re going to be learning all these artificial intelligence topics and at the same time you just happen to be deciding that you\'re going to pick up Nex.js or Angular or React is probably not good for your sanity. So you want to work when you\'re vibe coding with the simplest languages possible. Now when you\'re building a web application, there\'s two things you could be doing. One is if you\'re just creating a website for um that is basically a billboard for your company. All it is is there\'s a directory on your web server that has HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files in it. And by default, when you go to the website, it\'s going to find index.html. It\'s going to present index.html on the page. It\'s going to load the JavaScript and the CSS. And that\'s all you\'re doing. You\'re just you\'ve just got a very static web page, which is a web page for your business. That\'s option number one. Almost nobody does that option anymore. Now, we\'re on option number two. you have a programming language that creates HTML, CSS and JavaScript files and it creates them on the fly. Now in a LAMP stack and now a stack is basically a set of programs that work together. So a LAMPstack stands for Linux, Apache, MySQL and PHP. So in this case, Linux is the operating system, Apache is the web server, MySQL is the database, and a database is where data is stored. And then PHP is the programming language. So there are a bunch of different stacks, and they all have cute little names. And the LAMP stack is the one that\'s probably been around the longest of ones that are still in use. And it is a very easy stack to understand and it is a very easy stack for claude code and cursor and wind surf and any other large language models to program in. Now the extension of the file name tells the web server how to process the file. So if it\'s a PHP file, it ends inph. The web server knows that it uses the PHP interpreter to process the request, run the program, and send it back. If it\'s got ajs, it knows that it\'s a JavaScript file. If it\'s a CSS, it knows that it is a cascading stylesheet. So, the extension of the file, which is also called the mime type, tells the web server how to process the file. So what when you think about what is a web server, a web server is the operating system. In this case, it\'s usually Linux or Microsoft Windows server. Most of the most of the time it\'s Linux. Then you have your web server software. This is the second part of the stack. So when a LAMP stack, it\'s Apache. There\'s another stack that uses something called EngineX, which is considerably faster than Apache unless you\'re doing PHP. So which of these web server softwares you\'re doing is determined very much by what um applications you\'re running. So another web server software is NodeJS with express and then of course Microsoft has internet information services which has been around forever and just won\'t go away. Okay. Then you have your application. Your application is the thing that is going to generate the HTML, the CSS and the JavaScript. And now on top of that, you might be using something that is a design framework like Bootstrap or Tailwind. Now, Bootstrap was developed by Twitter and actually I think it\'s my my next slide. Um, Bootstrap was actually developed by Twitter and I\'ll get to that in a moment be after I get to what is HTML. HTML stands for hypertext markup language. It is the basic framework of the page. Now when I learned HTML, it was the only thing you had and it\'s basically built around tags. So over here to the right is a very basic HTML page. At the top it says dock type HTML which is telling the browser that what came through is HTML. Now you notice that there are pairs of tags. So the page begins with an HTML tag and ends with a forward slashhtml and then the header begins with a head ends with a forward slash head and then the title begins with a title and ends with a forward slashtitle. This is the basic formatting that that creates the structure of the page. Now when the web first came out, this was all you had. you had to figure out how to get it to look right with a series of maybe 30 different tags. Those times have changed and they invented this thing called CSS which is the cascading stylesheets. Now without CSS websites became really too hard to maintain and pretty much everything we see today with CSS3 started in 2009. But learning CSS is a was a terrible process and it\'s kind of like hazing for college students or football players. You learning CSS either in web school or in uh in your computer science major. It was it\'s a terrible thing to learn and it\'s it\'s miserable to program in. So all of a sudden, Twitter said, \"Yeah, we don\'t like coding in CSS, so we\'re going to create a framework called Bootstrap.\" And Bootstrap is probably the most used framework in all of web development. And it makes the website responsive, which means you can run it on your phone or you can run it on a tablet or you can run it on your PC, but it also makes it very easy to work with and maintain. So, one of the things that we really want to make sure we do in this course is make sure you understand the terminology of the framework. And I\'m just going to go over to this page right now. This is a page that was developed in Bootstrap. It\'s served using Linux, PHP, and MySQL. So, this is a lampstack. Everything that is on here is in Bootstrap. And Bootstrap is what lets me look at the the color schemes. Um the you\'ve got these cards. Uh you have this navigation bar over to the right. This is all very very easy to maintain and very very easy to work with because it\'s written in Bootstrap. Now, one of the tricks that you do in HTML, so let\'s say I wanted to change the color of this purple thing up to the top. What I would do is I would rightclick it. I would hit inspect element and then I would see this is the actual code and you see that there is a class on here called alert heading. That class comes from bootstrap. So I would then go to claude code and say uh please change all the alert heading um elements to red and then claude code is going to go out. Now, the only reason I can do that on the first try is I know that it\'s called alert heading because I know enough about Bootstrap to know that this class here is a Bootstrap class and that it got created and it\'s what is making this look the way it looks. So by me knowing how to do an inspect element and then know that it\'s the the alert heading class, I can now go back and ask Claude code to make all those changes for me. So you and in the uh claude.md file, I am also telling it to post all the changes to GitHub. Now, if you\'re not familiar with Git and GitHub, it\'s a version control system that allows you, among other things, to go back after you\'ve made mistakes. It\'s perfect for working in collaboration with other developers, and you can basically take a project, clone it, work on it, make your own branch of it, and then push it up. Now, let\'s see. I\'m almost terrified to see what\'s going to happen here. I hit reload and it didn\'t work. So, um, now let\'s see. I can do an inspect element and I can see that Claude decided not to do what I told it to do. So, this is kind of the the part of the debugging process. So I see here it said alert heading is the color and it\'s it updated custom.css CSS. And uh now another thing though, this is one of my favorite features of Clawude Code is I can I\'m on a Mac so I can go shift command 4 and I can just grab it and then it\'s now created a file that I can go into Finder, copy it, and I can just say it did not make it red. And then I can paste in the image. And now it\'s going to look at the image and see that it did not make it red. I see the issue. The welcome banner has its own specific CSS rule that is overriding the general alert heading style. Let me update the CSS to ensure all alert heading elements are read, including the one in the welcome banner. Now, the problem is is I really didn\'t want to do this, so I\'m going to have to see if uh Claude is actually writing the uh the changes. So, it\'s now going out and Claude is making those changes. Now the only reason this is working and I can do this pretty easily is that I know what is going on in the code. Now you can learn how to vibe code without ever learning how to code from scratch. It\'s kind of like understanding a language sometimes is easier than um speaking it. And so you can actually vi you can let um the large language model actually do the coding, but you just have to understand how it works. So now I come back over to here and it still didn\'t work. All right. Well, we\'re going to we\'re going to punt on that because this is actually one of the frustrating things about working with um AI to do your coding. But just so you just so you see what we\'re working on here. So, I have not been terribly happy with the school platform because we\'ve gotten a lot of complaints about um finding the courses and then we\'ll we\'ll post something. And what Joe had just said where we had to create another course in order to put our posts because the posts were getting lost. Um, what I decided we should do is vibe code an application where we have our own courses and all of the courses are here, but we also have projects and when you do a view project, you can say, \"Hey, I want to recommend specific courses that you take before the project.\" So, we\'re building our own school.com, but we\'re doing it 100% vibe coding. So these frameworks like the like CSS, Tailwind and Foundation, they make it possible to make CSS manageable because otherwise that conversation you would be having with the large language model to get it to write the code you want is it it could be a ridiculous conversation. Now there is actually I think I know why it\'s not working. So I\'m just going to go back real fast. I ran out of context. I\'ve been on this page probably for five hours. So, one of the the pro tips you need to know in Clawed Code and in any of them, you need to exit. You need to go back in and then you need to give it a prompt that says we are continuing the project. You can mention where it says the context left edge. Oh, let them know that it\'s 39%. Yeah. So, we were at 39% but it compressed once. So, I\'d actually gone all the way through, used up all the context. It compressed. So, we\'re continuing the process. Please uh re reread the requirements and uh uh scan the codebase. I have something I usually type in And what this is going to do is it\'s going to go out for like the next five minutes and reanalyze the entire codebase to understand the current state of the project because what ends up happening is over time it starts to get confused. So it has done so much work. You can only fit so much into the context. It got to the point where it couldn\'t figure out what it wanted to do anymore and was messing up. So, it it should have been able to change those um that color of that uh alert. It should have been able to change that on the first try, but it wasn\'t able to. So, that\'s these are the the things you learn about coding with AI that you only really learn by doing it. So, that\'s why we\'re trying to make everything we do, especially in the pro community, we\'re trying to make everything we do projectbased. So, we have got a bunch of projects to work on. We\'re going to do them and everybody is individually going to run into all of the same uh problems. And then one of the things we\'re Joe and I are trying to figure out how to set up is we\'re going to set up office hours in the pro community there when you\'re working on, you know, we get a bunch of people who are working on all the same project, get everybody on a Zoom call together and walk through all the bugs because it\'s a lot easier to do peer programming where we work on certain things together than it is to go off and do it on your own. So then after we got CSS and HTML, we got JavaScript. Now JavaScript is an actual programming language. A lot of software engineers don\'t want to admit that it\'s an actual programming language, but it\'s an actual programming language and it\'s the programming language that brings your website to life. Without the JavaScript, you would have no animation. You\'d have no ability to do popups. your buttons that when you click on them, they would only have one thing that they could do, which is submit the entire page. You\'d have no animations. But most important, every time you would do something, you would have to refresh the entire page. So, one of the things that happens on a page like this is when you\'re working on the page, notice you\'re not seeing a lot of flickering of the pages because it is using this thing called Ajax to use JavaScript to take portions of the script instead of repainting the entire page. So, JavaScript is the programming language that makes web pages powerful. Now, there was a time between when JavaScript came out and today that there were things in the middle that you would use. There was a a tool we use called jQuery. And jQuery made JavaScript a lot easier. Now, I was recommending that uh vibe coders use jQuery, but then I realized that if you\'re letting a large language model write code for you, you might as well write it in JavaScript because when you write an application vibe coding, you have to commit to vibe coding it forever. So, if you\'re going to let AI write the application, you\'ve got to let AI support the application. Because one thing I can show you by going into the code is that it writes some ugly ugly code. Like this code, I know you probably have not seen these programming languages before, but this one little bit of code is 357 lines of code. Okay, it vibe coding writes some just it repeats itself. It\'s it\'s definitely not code that you would want to manage uh manually. So you\'ve got your JavaScript and now we want to talk about Bootstrap. So I\'m going to talk about Bootstrap a little bit in detail. So Bootstrap is a design framework that Twitter came up with. Now, a lot of these companies do really cool things. And when Twitter first came out, it realized that it had solved a problem when it developed Twitter that everybody in the web community was having. So, they just kind of donated it and they said, uh, Bootstrap, it\'s your, you know, universe, it\'s yours. You guys just got to maintain it. And so, that was Bootstrap one that came out in 2011. Bootstrap 5, I think, came out probably five years ago and hasn\'t changed much since. There really isn\'t much left to do to Bootstrap it. It can do everything it needs to do. It makes your designing your web web pages really easy. And there\'s an entire library of templates in Bootstrap 5. You can go to this one website called uh monster one or template monster and you can find a bunch of pre-developed bootstrap code that other people have done for you and the combination of working with a template plus cursor and claude and all the other LLMs or tools. It makes it makes it really easy for um that to be done. Now, uh, let me, uh, go to the next page. All right. So, now that I finished the slides, let\'s go back and see. Okay. So, now Claude has gone out. It\'s updated the to-do list. It\'s read the current project status. It sees everything that it used to do. And now I can say, now it does not remember the last conversation we had. So, I now can go back to it. and let\'s see if it gets it right on the first try. I\'m going to do an inspect element. I\'m just going to say I\'m going to copy this and I\'m going to say I want this to be read and I\'m going to paste it in. So now now it\'s it\'s now forgot the conversation that we add. So it doesn\'t actually remember that we\'ve it\'s tried twice. So this time we\'ll see if it gets it. And then if it doesn\'t get it, the other thing that we would do is we would change the models. So right now I\'m actually using Claude Sonnet 4. If I go to Claude Opus, a lot of times you have a lot better results with Opus than you do with sonnet. It\'s just so much more expensive to work with it. Okay, so I found it. The welcome page is line 146 in dashboard.php. I\'ll check to see if there\'s already a CSS rule for this in custom.css. It found it. Now it found three lines of code to change, which is interesting. Now it\'s going to ask me again, do does do I want it to be allow it to make changes to the code. So the first time it does everything, it does that. Okay. So now it says the welcome heading will now be read. The CSS has been updated to force all alert headings. So now I\'m going to come through and it still does not work. So uh Joe, any ideas on this one? Um so it\'s t um I don\'t know if you want to talk about hierarchy in CSS because that\'s what I\'m seeing there. Talking about using a parent class and it not overriding the private one. Um, the key thing I see in there is in line 1286, it\'s using the dollar sign, uh, uh, bang important, which basically says in CSS, you want to override what the parent one is. Um, but what it\'s actually typing in there, Ed, if you go in inspect element on the page, what color does it actually say? because the color on there will um in the styles section over there instead of computed you can inspect element and then click on the thing and you\'ll see um the the code will actually tell you which class that color is actually coming from and actually yes I\'m not going to go into detail on this just because this is not supposed to be this class but this is a class we need to do because when you start debugging what so What I did here is I\'ve now changed to opus and opus failed. Okay, so um and a lot of times with uh vibe coding you need to pick your battles like at a certain point you just decide, hey, is this really worth changing to red? Because I could do a lot of other things like I could go into courses here. I could go into available courses and I could say in any every place it is remove these um these badges. I mean this whole thing like this canband board that I can do drag and drops on where it says task move it successfully all of that was written by this code. Now as the project gets more and more complicated the AI has to do more and more work to be able to do it. But you notice that the the combination of the LLM and the tools lets it do a lot of other things. So, let me go here to the dashboard. I\'m going to go, what do I want to do here? I got team members. Oh, this is actually a funny one. One thing that I was fighting with today is I actually have a profile picture. So, if I go up here to profile, I uploaded a random picture, but in the team members display, it\'s not showing the picture. So, at a certain point here, you do have to pick your battles because I actually spent a good amount of time on this today trying to figure out why the profile picture wasn\'t actually printing. So, um All right. So, couple other things that I wanted to cover is go ahead. Right there. um in your styles there you see the dot welcome banner and dot alert heading is still showing color white important and it\'s also telling you that that is in custom.css CSS on line 1045. So if you click on it, you can see exactly where that is. And if you wanted to, you could then go into the CSS and not Vibe Code and actually go through and change that to red in that file, right? Although but that\'s only if you have it open in like uh an IDE like cursor or whatever. Um and that\'s that\'s that\'s not background color. That\'s that color. color is equal to text color again in in font but there is no BG color there. Okay, I see that. Okay, so but these are these are the types of things that so vibe coding is gives you superpowers. Oh, I just changed I changed this to blue. Uh vibe coding gives you superpowers. But the reason that you want to do the courses and you want to work with this is to just start to get the skills and start figuring out how you debug things and how you write the requirements. And there\'s a lot to this. So in the and I think in our next beginners course I\'m going to cover um setting up your environment and then just starting a project from scratch because I want to get people especially in the pro community. I want to get people started working on some projects. Uh because that\'s just really going to be the fastest way to learn is uh you know I\'ve learned I\'ve learned clawed code the most by fighting with it. You know trying to make it do things and then seeing all the things it can\'t do. And uh there\'s I\'ve made a lot of changes to the way I go about doing projects because I\'ve tried to do projects. Now, if you are one of the people who\'s who\'s learning divide code and you\'re learning AI because you want to develop and sell products, the LAMP stack, which is this Linux, Apache, MySQL, and PHP, is one of the most accepted stacks if 