INSERT INTO `lessons` (`id`, `course_id`, `title`, `description`, `content`, `lesson_type`, `duration_minutes`, `order_index`, `status`, `is_mandatory`, `video_url`, `video_duration`, `attachment_url`, `quiz_data`, `assignment_data`, `created_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `published_at`, `video_transcript`) VALUES
(583, 169, 'First Live Call - 7/6/2025', 'Our first live call in the Free community have over 600 participants.', '', 'text', 60, 99, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/23RBl-v8vdQ', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-29 02:14:05', '2025-07-31 19:53:15', NULL, 'and you are live at all. Hey everybody, welcome. So, uh this is uh another introduction call, but we\'re going to do a lot more stuff that\'s actually technical today. I want to start going through things. Um but most importantly, if anybody hasn\'t uh wasn\'t on last week\'s call, I\'m at Honor. I I\'m the founder of the community. You probably found me through Instagram. Uh I\'m going to go through the introduction part really fast. I you know I\'ve been doing uh tech work since 1986. I graduated from the University of Illinois in 1986. Took me two years to get a job. So I very much understand what the technical environment is today. I\'m running into a lot of either uh computer science majors who are coming out of college and obviously with artificial intelligence being more uh recent than their freshman year, it\'s really hard for anybody to be keeping up with it. So my background is as a consultant and I do consulting today which is um one of the reasons I\'m really good at identifying what\'s going wrong in the artificial intelligence industry because anytime you have you\'re doing projects with large organizations and the project is going to span six months to a year but the technologies are going to change six times during the uh during the project you just run into all sorts of project management issues. Now, um, so the reasons that we created the community and we think that it\'s really important to have a community where we can share ideas is a we have the economic parallels to what I ran into in 1988 when I got out of college and couldn\'t find a job. And basically, the only place you could learn what you really needed to know was on the job. So, if the economy wasn\'t perfect and they weren\'t hiring graduates, um, there wasn\'t really any way to learn things. So, we have the economic parallels to what I was running into when I first got out of college. And then we have the technology parallels to what it was like, you know, between 1998 and 2001 when the web was taking off. It was changing really fast. Nobody knew what to do. Everybody was getting funded. Now, if you, you know, it\'s kind of the same thing is going on today when it comes to funding. You know, you had all these people, all these projects that were getting funded, but really nobody knew how to deliver them. And then when there was the big.com crash, you got this big consolidation into, you know, what a lot of what you\'re seeing today. And so, you know, colleges are great and you learn a lot of really good background information. And because when I was at the University of Illinois, we were part of the uh liberal arts department. Uh they were telling us that we\'re not teaching you to have a job, we\'re teaching you how to learn so that when you do have a job, they can teach you. That is actually really wise. At the time I\'m surpris you know at the time I was kind of annoyed because it was really making it hard to get a job. The fact that we were using technologies in co college that nobody was using themselves. But what we came up with is we came up with a different learning approach and our learning approach is ba based on really two sides. You know the first of all is everybody needs to know the basics. Now, you can go and you can learn how to do NADN, you can learn cursor, you can learn claude code, and you, you know, you can learn vibe coding, and you could probably build some really nice stuff, but when you go out to sell it, if you can\'t have a conversation with your client, or you\'re always going to run into these roadblocks. There\'s always a guy in the security department. There\'s always a guy in the infrastructure who wants to have an argument with you over the fact that you\'re using let\'s say you\'re using Claude and they think you should be using Grock and you know you just you need to know the basics. You need to be able to talk to talk in order to walk the walk when you go in. Whether you\'re going in as a client, you know, or you\'re whether you\'re talking to clients, whether you\'re talking to you\'re selling a product, you\'re going to run into somebody who\'s going to challenge you on your basic knowledge or if you\'re if you\'re the one in your organization bringing an artificial intelligence and you\'re going to your manager and they\'re they ask you random questions about um buzzword bingo. I mean, buzzword bingo in artificial intelligence is a game that everybody\'s playing. You go to I\'ve been to parties where I\'ve heard more buzzwords in a single sentence than anybody can actually believe. So, part of what we\'re doing is we\'re teaching you buzzword bingo. You know what what we did is we created an an an application and I\'ll jump over to it now. About 50 of you have definitely already played with it. Um, let me get this out of my way. Um, all right. I gotta play a little bit around here because the Zoom controls make it really hard for me to find my my page. Okay. So, we create a we created a learning um a reinforcement learning module where as you\'re going through the different parts of the course, you can do these practice sessions where let\'s say I\'m going to go in and I\'m going to pick um augmented AI agents. I go in and do start a practice. Hold on. My mouse. Let me do it this way. All right. So, basically, it just starts asking you questions and every time you get a question wrong, you lose a half a point. Every time you get one right, you get a point. And so the reason for this is basically and I\'m guessing which is really going to keep me off the leaderboard. Um the reason for this is just being reinforcement learning. The way reinforcement learning works for a large language model is you keep drilling it over and over again with information until it learns the information. And so because gamification is such a big deal with the way people are learning today, we have a leaderboard. Let\'s look at the leaderboard. Okay, Alexandra is winning so far. She\'s got 607 correct, 35 wrong. But what happens as you\'re doing this is it\'s kind of a flashc card thing and it allows you to know when somebody throws terms at you what those terms mean. And our reason for doing this is really there\'s just so many topics that there\'s so many topics in artificial intelligence and there\'s so many buzzwords and people just throw things out at you randomly. You know, I\'m using this SDK or whatever. I\'m doing this thing. Being able to just have that background information is just a way to um you know whether you\'re selling your product or you\'re selling to your client you\'re you\'re you\'re trying to convince somebody to do a project your way this is just the background information we want to do. So our learning approach is based on that. And then the other part is that live is always better than video. Um, you know, I don\'t know, we\'ve got um 221 participants right now who have identified that they could go to YouTube and probably get a lot of the same information that I\'m going to do uh here. But live really it forces you to think in real time and it\'s really better. It\'s I think it\'s just a better way of learning. And now I\'ve got a big X over here of the our MIT professional education certificate that Joe and I did back in 2021. This was a this was a threemonth MIT professional education thing that I think it took years off my life. That is when I learned how much uh I am a terrible student. And uh Joe was in college much more recently than me. So he did a he did a lot better. I think overall he was like number eight in the class and I was number 15 but there were 1,500 people in the class and it was a lot of what we\'re doing in these communities is from what we learned 