py it to a human. But if it\'s happy, you\'re just going to write the email yourself and you\'re going to send it back. So that\'s where an agent is different than an automation because you\'ve got kind of a divergent of the process depending on um where what the LLM has determined. Now, AI automations and agents have their pros and cons. And the pros is they\'re really, really cool. The cons is getting an organization to accept that an AI agent is going to have access to its corporate databases and it is going to be able to take actions on its own. That is a really hard cell. Now, what I\'m hoping to do in this community is give everybody arguments for why you should why they should allow your automations in how you\'ve implemented security into your automations and how you\'ve created guard rails. Guardrails is one of the key topics in AI agents. Somebody had actually messaged me about some Oracle tools that they had developed that were using AI agents and the comments section talked about, yeah, we\'re not going to let you delete our Oracle database, but they were they had the vision to put in guard rails so that the guardrails were there to make sure you could never drop a table, you could never truncate a table. So, you\'re going to run into the cons of AI automations. Now, let\'s see how much time I\'ve got. Oh, perfect. All right, because this is where I really wanted to get to. I wanted to get to Claude Code because Claude Code is wild. That\'s all I can say. Let me go slideshow play from the start. Claude code fixes most of the things you run into with cursor. Now, uh hopefully some of you are old enough to remember the Matrix movies. Uh Cipher from the Matrix was the guy who could look at the screen and he could see all the green letters running down and actually see what was going on. One of the reasons you use cursor with clawed code is so that you can see what\'s going on in real time and you can see the files being changed because claude code and Gemini CLI are both command line interface tools. You can do the entire project without having an IDE, which means you could do the entire project at a Linux terminal if that\'s how you wanted to work. And on our pro community, we\'ll get you Linux terminals if that\'s how you want, if that\'s how you actually want to go about it. So, Claude Code is installed pretty easy. It\'s installed with node package manager. So, you\'re doing an npmi. If you want to install it globally, you do a minusg and it\'s just at anthropic AI cloud code. So what it is, it\'s a command line tool from Enthropic um that allows developers to delegate coding tasks to Claude directly from their terminal. Now I got that in I got that description from Claude, but the way I see it is one thing that Claude can do is it launches agents. Now this is great for anthropic because it burns tokens. It can really burn tokens. So, if you have a to-do list and you plan out your project and there\'s build 15 modules, it will it can simultaneously run off activate 15 agents and start building out the different parts of the project simultaneously. That\'s why I like using cursor because when that starts happening, you\'re just watching these files just change in real time. Now, what it does is it lets you plan your projects ahead of time. it isn\'t just about um working in real time the way it is in cursor. So the first thing you want to do is you want to make sure you have claude installed and then you create a a project directory and navigate to it. So let me go to cursor. So I have a directory here called claude crash course and let me do this. I\'m going to exit. Okay. So I am at my command line. You see that I am at claude crash course. To open up Claude code, you just type in the word Claude. Once you bring in Claude, now Claude is now connected with my API key to Enthropic. And there\'s all these things I can do. Now, the first thing you always want to do is do a forward slashinit. And what that does is it creates a new claude.md file for your codebase. Now you can take an existing project and do a claw to knit on it and if the directory you\'re working in your your current directory it will scan all of the files in your directory and build this claude MD file. So the first time I did it with a complex angular application it took five minutes for the init to run because it went off and it analyzed all this stuff. Now the claude MD file is like your rules file and cursor. I will post this in the community. It\'s important that you have this because this Claude MD the these seven steps are really important that you tell Claude you want to do it. So the first thing you want to do is you want it to think through the problem. Read the codebase for relevant files and write a plan to a directory called tasks todo.md. to-do.md is critical because it\'s your to-do list and as Claude is doing the different steps of your project, it\'s going to check it off that it\'s done. So, you\'re going to create a to-do list structure and the plan should have a to-do items that you check off as you complete it. Before you begin working, number three, before you begin working, check in with me and I will verify the plan because you don\'t want to just let it go off and run its own thing. Then with the task execution, begin working on them, marking them as complete as you go. And then we number five is communicate every step of the way. And number six is the simplicity principle. Make every change as simple as possible. Now this is where sonnet 3.7 used to fall down. If you if you had the experience of working with sonnet 3.7 before sonnet 40, it used to just build things that you didn\'t ask for. it just kind of went crazy and would go out there and say I\'m going to build a whole bunch of stuff. So this is where you needed to put this in here saying be do simple and then at the end add a review section. So this claude.md is the first thing that you want to create. Let me go back to here. So the first thing you want to do is initialize the project. Now the next file that\'s really important is requirements.md. This is your project plan. Now, it\'s an MD file, which means it\'s a markdown file, which means it\'s in this format. Now, if you\'re ever confused or you\'re ever not sure if your MD file is in the right format, just ask Claude. You could go in here right now and I could go into Claude and I could say, is my MD file in the right format? I I go off I run it and it is going to tell me that it is in the right format because I asked it to or I asked it to write it. So it is in an excellent format. It\'s well structured and comprehensive. So th in this case, I wanted to build a stock tracking application, something that could could track a stock that would use real- time APIs. So I had a pretty long conversation with Claude on Claude desktop to determine what functionality this stock application would have based on other stock tracking applications that are out there. So it is it is building this requirements.mmd for me. And then the next file is text stack. So in textstack.mmd I decided that I wanted this to be written in nex.js and I have an entire text stack of what dependencies I want to use, what I what API keys I\'m using, what environment variables I need to say. I create that in textstack.mmd. Now, if you notice, if you if you\'re familiar with cursor, we have not pressed go yet at all. And we\'ve done a lot. And really, Claude is about planning the project out ahead of time much more than actually executing it. Now, when it executes it, it\'s you\'re it\'s going to go it could go off for hours, but the more time you spend at the planning stage, the better off you are. Then this next file here is called design notes. Now I got this design notes using one of the coolest tools in um that Claude has which is the ability for me to go to a website I like. So I went to one of my Angular websites on my Mac. I went shift command4 drew a picture around the website and pasted it into claude and said please to please use this as your inspiration to create a CSS file for my application. So what it did is it developed this design notes.md based on an interface called absc which is a project I\'m working on. So in this case, it actually was able to look at my screen, see what my font hierarchy was, what fonts I was using, what colors I was using. And so now when I design this, when I let Claude run this stock trading application, it\'s going to use the design principles that I gave it by going into inspiration mode. So then we have our API keys.mmd which is basically do not remember to add it to get ignore if you put your API keys in your uh your if you create an API keys.mmd make sure you do not send it to get and that is security issues have been the the thing that is holding back vibe coding more than anything else. Okay, so this is the claw rules file that we already went through. Now there is a thing called plan mode and you get to plan mode by hitting shift tab twice uh from the command. Okay, it now says I am in plan mode. Now I want to also do model opus. Now I\'ve just set my model to the expensive thinking model. Okay, what you want to do is when you\'re planning, use Opus. When you\'re executing, use Sonnet. Now, Sonnet co sonnet and co Opus write code equally as well, but Sonnet is 12th the cost of Opus. But when you\'re planning, Opus plans way better than Sonnet. So, do your planning with Opus. Now that you\'re in plan mode, it is actually writing to all of these files. It is your planning. So I got, you know, basically plan mode is what got me my requirements MD. It got me my um it got me my text stack. This was all done in plan mode. Then all I have to do is shift tab back and now I\'m out of plan mode and I\'m ex in execution mode. So now I\'m going to type in model sonnet. And now I\'ve set the model to sonnet, which means now I can um do my builds, write my code, do it all in in sonnet. Now, um when you\'re in plan mode, do not be afraid to ask Claude what it thinks. If it is Claude is the LLM that if it if there was any LLM that I thought had a personality, it would be Claude. You Claude has no problem telling you if you did something stupid. Whereas Chat GPT is always going to tell you you\'re a genius. Claude has a tendency to not tell you you\'re a genius if you\'re doing something wrong. But it also follows instructions. So this is also a good time to talk about coding models. Chad GPT way too prone for hallucinations. Gemini doesn\'t have hallucinations, but it\'s very opinionated. And Gemini will and it also has a tendency to gaslight you. I\'ve had certain situations where Gemini Pro 2.5 has told me there\'s a calendar on the screen. I go to the screen, there\'s no calendar, and it basically tells me basically I\'m an idiot that there\'s a calendar on the screen. So, I have gotten into situations where I could not get any further with Gemini Pro. So, that is why as of today, Claude is what I\'m recommending now. But don\'t be afraid to ask Claude what it thinks. Now, I\'ve already talked about running Claude from cursor or VS Code. One of the reasons is you want to checkpoint often. Okay, it is not like cursor where you can checkpoint your code and roll back your code in the IDE. Cursor, what I do is every time something works, I get to a stage where something\'s working, I don\'t have bugs in it, push it to get. Okay. Um, now I\'ve also found that if you plan right, you may never need your checkpoints, but definitely push to get as often as you feel appropriate. Overuse Git that you\'re going to find um if you do go down a bad road, you\'re going to need it and and starting over is not not what you want to do. And I have had to start over several times anytime I\'ve used Gemini. And in my experiences with Gemini CLI, I\'ve also had to just abandon projects. So the the next thing you can do is um inspiration for UI design. So if I were to hit now you can paste images into claude which I find incredible. Okay. Now the two reasons to do it is inspiration for the UI design and debugging. If you get to the point where you\'re running out of the browser, you\'re running independently. Joe and I had run into a problem where something worked fine running locally, but when we pushed it to Apache, it didn\'t work anymore. Well, the easiest thing you can do is shift command 4 on a Mac, cut out your image, paste it into Claude, and say, \"What\'s going on here?\" And then if it\'s really complicated, that\'s actually when I switch back to Opus because Opus is going to reason through the bugs it sees pretty well. It\'s going to it\'s actually going to think it through. Now, the other thing you want to do often is when you\'re in here, do a forward slashcle. Anytime your context window starts to get full, you\'re going to be subject to hallucinations. And the reason you get the hallucinations, especially if you\'re coding in something like Angular, you\'re jumping back and forth between programming languages. It\'s writing TypeScript, then in my case, it\'s writing PHP, then it\'s jumping back and it\'s writing um HTML templates. And when you\'re doing that, as the context window gets bigger and you got more and more in the context and it because it\'s remembering all the prompts that I\'ve given it. So as you get further and further down into your project, you hit clear often. Anytime you\'ve got a feature to work, you\'re moving on to a next feature, just hit clear. That will clear your context window. It\'s going to eliminate the hallucinations and it\'s going to save you money because every time you do something it even if the tokens are cashed you\'re still paying for the tokens that are being used. Then the next thing you want to do is security checks. Now I don\'t know I have a prompt here and I\'ll post it in the school before you deploy anything or even take it off your computer. This is a prompt to give it that basically says please check through all the code and make sure it follows security best practices. Make sure there\'s no sensitive information in the front in the front end and that there are no vulnerabilities that can be exploited every time you before you push anything run that. Okay. And it will go through it will look at your code. It will make sure you can\'t do SQL injections. that will make sure that there\'s no APIs that are being exposed on the front end that somebody could use inspect element and see. Uh this is why vibe coding got a bad name to start off with because people were not running uh security checks. And Ed, I I would also add on to there um about uh removing all council log messages that were used for debugging. Um, a lot of times, um, I guess probably not as much in Claude, but in cursor, it\'ll put in a lot of console log debugging messages to help you figure out where your errors are at. And then you need to remind it to, hey, get rid of those to make your code production ready. Good, good point. And then my second to last thing to talk about is learn what claude code does. Okay, in my case, I\'m just now learning Nex.js. And when I say I\'m learning Nex.js, JS. I\'m just following the bouncing ball because I never plan to code it from scratch. But it is important. There is a prompt you can put in and I I\'ll post this also. Please explain the functionality in the code you just built. Walk me how you changed it and how it works. Act like you\'re a senior engineer teaching me to code. Now, what I recommend you do is you run that after every big after every big set of builds. you save that and you make that part of the project because at some point here you\'re going to want to turn this over to a junior dev. And what you\'re doing is you\'re turning over your claude code context your environment to a junior dev. So run this prompt summarize the results and save it for the next time you work on code. Now the one last thing I am going to add is do not doom scroll while you are waiting for Claude. So most of you found me on Instagram which means you probably all like to scroll through your Instagram. The one way to kill a day is to set off claude off on an adventure and the 30 minutes it takes you\'re sitting here doom scrolling on Instagram which is fine because that\'s how you found me. but it is going to kill your productivity. What I do, and this is a recommendation that was actually given to me by another creator, which is open up a new chat window while while Claude is working, and have a conversation with Claude about the project to get ideas about what you should do next, what other applications of the same type, also what kind of functionality you should add. Just don\'t pick up your phone. Don\'t start checking your emails. Have a conversation with with Claude about the project that you\'re working on because it will give you improvements. And this is um the hardest thing for me at first before I I found this was that I would start Claude, it would go off for an adventure and when it was done, it\'d be done 20 minutes later, but it would be two hours before I would look at the code again. So, this is just a pro tip that I totally recommend. Um so we actually in the pro community we have a uh a live event tomorrow for any of you who do actually want to join. Um we also have regional pricing on that because we did get we have a lot of people from all over the world and the US dollar um is not representative of the cost of living around the world. So DM me if you\'re uh in Southeast Asia or someplace like that. Um, but we\'re going to go through a lot more of the build process. This is stock application that I\'m actually wrote the requirements for. We\'re actually going to build it tomorrow. Um, the other thing is if you join the pro community, um, we\'ve got some projects. There\'s a there\'s a lot of I mean, we just have so many kids who are just getting out of college who are really having trouble. Um, this is a this is about building your resume also by building projects that actually have use. We have some applications that we handcoded that we want to rewrite in uh cursor and in cloud code. We\'ve also got some project managers who are in the pro community that are learning project management of um AI projects. Uh being a project manager, uh I got 4,000 followers on uh Instagram by trashing agile methodology. So we know that developers don\'t like project managers very much. And I\'m very curious to find out can I teach a developer to be a project manager by writing these requirements docs or a project manager as a developer. I\'m really curious. So we are going to we are going to explore that. So I want to thank you guys all. Um, I think this was one of the very few times I my time management was correct and the length of my uh and the length of my PowerPoint was the length of the um the presentation. I want to thank you all for attending. Next week, um, we haven\'t figured out what we\'re going to do next week, but is probably going to be MCP. I really want to get into building some MCP tools so people see exactly how we build MCPS. So, all right, everybody, have a great day. Does that um you have any um so things that came up are um from the chat that I saw were um specifically the claw.md file to post um I said all those texts will be posted in the school community and pinned so they should be in the top four or five posts on the board. um regional pricing um of the external community. Um I said that it was created but it\'s not uh it\'s not 100% fleshed out and set up ready to go to accept anybody in there yet. So we\'re still figuring that out. I\'ll I\'ll just change on that. Okay. So, it\'s currently manual, which means Joe and the rest of the team have not created the automations that if you if you sign up for the um the it\'s it\'s basically AI masters uh Southeast Asia is the name of the regional pricing community and if you join we have to move you manually but um eventually we\'ll we\'ll probably build a Zap year automation to do that. Um, and if uh if anybody is interested in learning Zapier, um, just post in the community that you are because that is uh that is something that Joe has been going down. He\'s been automating a lot of the stuff that we\'re doing with Zapier. Um, if you\'re interested in that, that is that is a real thing. Um, what else? Anything else on there, Joe? Um the I posted it a few times, but if you could flash it up on the screen again right now, the pro community link. And um a couple people asked about your thoughts on Windserve. You mentioned it before, but not at this meeting. So, okay. Honestly, I do not know enough about wind surf to be to give you an educated um view of wind surf.');
INSERT INTO `lessons` (`id`, `course_id`, `title`, `description`, `content`, `lesson_type`, `duration_minutes`, `order_index`, `status`, `is_mandatory`, `video_url`, `video_duration`, `attachment_url`, `quiz_data`, `assignment_data`, `created_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `published_at`, `video_transcript`) VALUES
(584, 169, 'Live Call 7/12/25', 'This was our second live call in the free community.', '', 'text', 60, 80, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/fgtr5-Z0kKs', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-07-29 02:16:07', '2025-07-29 02:22:16', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `lessons` (`id`, `course_id`, `title`, `description`, `content`, `lesson_type`, `duration_minutes`, `order_index`, `status`, `is_mandatory`, `video_url`, `video_duration`, `attachment_url`, `quiz_data`, `assignment_data`, `created_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `published_at`, `video_transcript`) VALUES
(585, 169, 'Live Event August 2, 2025', 'Live Event August 2, 2025', '', 'text', 60, 50, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/J6tMyyYzjJ4', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-08-05 02:03:55', '2025-08-05 02:04:26', NULL, 'All right, I\'ll uh I\'ll go for it. Okay, so what I what I wanted to cover today is uh prompt engineering because everything around artificial intelligence always comes back to prompting. So I spent a ton of time over the last couple of weeks vibe coding and vibe coding is prompting. Whatever you\'re doing, no matter how you get there, you\'re going to end up back at prompting. So the basically all a prompt is is it\'s the input that you give to a language model to tell it what you want it to do. Now that seems like it should be a really simple um straightforward. There should be no ambiguity about it and you should just be able to talk to the LLM and get what you want. But in reality that\'s not the case. there\'s certain things you need to be able to do um to be able to make sure that your prompt is um you know is understood by the large language model. So prompt engineering and I always do this to myself here got to get this thing out of the way. Um prompt engineering is the practice of crafting effective instructions to get optimal results from the AI language model. That\'s a long sentence to uh to basically say organize your prompts correctly so that the LLM isn\'t confused because one thing you do realize and the more you vibe code the more you realize that the the LLM is pretty stupid and the better you really have to lay things out in the prompt properly if you want the prompt or you want the LLM to return what you need. So, the difference between a prompt that\'s properly engineered and one that\'s kind of just thrown out there is the difference between getting a generic unhelpful responses and getting exactly what you need. So, good prompt engineering and this is something I definitely learned vibe coding and I had a little episode yesterday with Chad GPT giving me crazy crazy ideas of how to screw up my network. um the better your prompts, the better the results you\'re going to get. So there\'s a lot of different ways prompting happens and in the end prompting is how you\'re talking to the large language model. So whether you\'re in chat GPT using the chat interface, you\'re using the an any of the APIs, you\'re using a CLI like open AAI, Olama and uh GPT CLI, um you could be embedding it in tools or you could be using frameworks. Regardless of how you get that prompt into the context window, that prompt is it\'s a prompt. So, it\'s not like you\'re um prompting when you\'re talking to a chat interface and you\'re talking programmatically when you\'re talking through the API. The thing that\'s in the API is the prompt that you\'re that you\'re sending. So, prompting is how everything happens. Even if you it\'s all happening behind the scenes, it\'s happening as part of a workflow. And it\'s it\'s just always how well you put that prompt together is how well you\'re going to get a uh a response out of it. So prompt engineering as a name for something. Initially when LLM first came out, the the job of prompt engineer was what everybody was saying, okay, that\'s going to be my new career. Well, AI changes so fast. There\'s still prompting, but the idea of prompt engineering has turned into something totally new and but there are still some things that you can do through prompting that allow you to not have to do supervised fine-tuning. So if you have a big enough model and the model\'s got enough nuance, you can definitely guide the results of the output in such a way that in a lot of cases you can avoid supervised fine-tuning. It also enables behavior that is very taskspecific. It reduced cost and complexity. And most important, if you\'re going to build reliable systems using artificial intelligence, prompt engineering is the number one uh thing you\'re going to need to know. So, you\'re always going to be getting better at prompt engineering because prompting is always going to change. Prompting today is not what prompting was a year ago. and prompting a year from today is not going to be like it is right now. So the most important thing that you have to realize or one of the most important things is what is the context window. So when you hear about different size models you hear about differentiz context windows and initially the first sets of context windows with Chad GPT3 the context window was only about 3,000 tokens. Now, a token represents a word or a part of a word. So, there are some words that can be represented by one token, but there\'s also a lot of words that require two um tokens to represent it. The size of the context window is the number of tokens that are processed simultaneously through the prompt. So if we go back to the white paper uh attention is all you need. It\'s basically saying that every token that is in the context window is being analyzed against every other token that\'s in the context window. And that\'s how it\'s determining the meaning and that\'s how it\'s determining what it what you\'re asking for it. So everything that\'s in the context window gets processed with every prompt. Now, that\'s critical because when you start talking about context windows that are 10 million tokens in size, just because you have a 10 million token context window does not mean you want to use 10 million tokens. Because as far as computational power goes, the more that\'s in the context window, the more computational power that\'s going to be required by the LLM to do its job because everything is everything in the context window is processed. And now over here to the right on the orange part, one of the things and I I broke this out for a very specific reason. you the tools outside of the LLM are what is maintaining the history of the conversation. The LLM itself is stateless. It\'s like a website and it\'s like an old a very old website that when you go to it, it is taking everything that is in the context window and it\'s analyzing it. So, it is looking at your current prompt and then it\'s also looking at the history of the conversation. That\'s why over time as your your conversations get longer and longer, sometimes they get crazier and crazier. So, for example, yesterday when I was using chat GPT to try to destroy my Proxmox network, the longer the conversation got, the worse the ideas that GPT was coming up with to fix the problem. And that\'s also something that you run into with um when you\'re doing a a large project in vibe coding. It\'s a thing called a distractor. And basically, if there\'s something in your context window that isn\'t relevant to the current thing you\'re acting on, that\'s called a distractor. And so as yesterday as I was going uh through network commands trying to figure out how to do things, the fact that all of my previous conversation was still being passed into the context window was actually confusing chat GPT because it was giving it ideas about things that hadn\'t worked in the past. So it would try something that thing wouldn\'t work but it was still in the context window as one of the recommendations. So you do need to manage everything that\'s in the context window with the prompt. Now if you\'re doing it programmatically that\'s a lot easier than if you\'re using a chat window like chat GPT because in some cases with chat GPT you just need to start over. Now one of the things I don\'t like about chat GPT 03 which is their reason their big reasoning model is it reads all of your conversations. So it it tries to know everything it can about you. So it knows about conversations that aren\'t necessarily relevant to what you\'re current doing currently doing. So sometimes the context window is um more of a distraction than it\'s a benefit. So the larger context windows aren\'t always the best thing. Now what we get out of the LLM is not always going to be what we want out of the LLM. Now, LLMs are only going to work with the information we explicitly provide. So, it\'s got it\'s got the model, it\'s got its learnings in the model, but when you\'re asking it for something, it\'s not intuitive to say, \"Oh, you should be also asking about this other thing.\" So, LLMs are only going to work with whatever informations you\'re explicitly going to provide it. And most people underestimate how much context and specivivity AI needs to pro produce truly useful outfits. And I and I put that one in there because people underestimate I\'ve seen people do prompting that are they just assume that the LLM is going to figure out what they mean based on what they put in. Now, if you\'re just having a chat trying to get a recipe, that\'s perfectly fine. But if you\'re doing this as part of a business application, you need to make sure that it\'s your prompts are very specific and they\'re very repeatable because if you\'re writing a program, you want it to work the same way every time. You don\'t want it to act differently because your prompts aren\'t specific enough. Now, the same AI model can produce mediocre result results if you don\'t prompt it right. But at the same time, when you do get the prompting right, it can do amazing things. And I\'m bashing Chad GPT40 today because it wasted half my day. Uh Chad, but Chad GPT40 is a perfect example of a model where the better you prompt it, the better off you\'re going to be. So, one of the things I had to do to Chad GPT yesterday was tell it stop giving me multiple answers at the same time. Because if you use Chad GPT and I say, \"Okay, I want to I want to add a firewall route.\" It will give me 10 steps to adding the firewall route, but then the first step won\'t work and then by the time the first step doesn\'t work, the last nine steps are obsolete and you get confused. So, in the prompt, I had to keep telling Chad GPT over and over again, only give me one uh one instruction at a time. And after about three or four instructions, it would forget and then it would give me six or seven instructions all at once. So, the whole thing with the prompting is you\'re negotiating with the LLM to get what you want. Now, with Gemini, you\'re negotiating a lot harder because it\'s going to give you what it wants. Uh Claude, I still think, you know, in my in my endeavors yesterday. Um Chad GPT got me in trouble, Claude helped dig me out. But the thing that I like the most about Claude was the way it told me when a command was dangerous. because in a lot of cases the chat is not going to tell you um that that command that you\'re doing is going to blow up your uh blow up the network. Now, so let\'s start with the whole concept of prompting. There is a minimum viable prompt. If your prompt does not have these two things, you\'re not going to get you don\'t stand any chance of getting useful output for it. So the first thing is the task to perform. So you\'ve got to explicitly tell chat GPT or whatever model what you want. What do you want from it? So if I say I want you to create a recipe for me. Okay. Well, that is a that is a task. All right. And if I ask chat GPT, please give me a recipe. It will probably give me a recipe. It\'s not going to give me the recipe I want, but it\'s going to give me a recipe. Then the second thing you need to be viable is the context in which you\'re going to perform it. And the more context you give it, the more it\'s going to get narrowed down. So if I say to Chad GPT, please give me a recipe. And then I say for a cake, and then I would like a chocolate cake and I would like vanilla frosting and I would like um it to be glutenfree. And by the way, I only have uh my oven only goes up to 120° F. You know, you can keep giving it more and more context. And the more context you give it, the more narrow and targeted the responses are going to be. So, it\'s very critical that you get the minimum viable prompt where you at least have a task and you 