l the large language model what the different parts of the prompt are. And I\'ll get to that where where you you can actually use XML tags. Whereas instead of saying here is an example of a recipe I want you to use, you could use tags and you could do an example tag. So you could say example one end of example one. So there\'s things you can do in the structure that help with both the spec specificity and simplicity. Okay. And then the next thing is strategy. Now, I\'m somebody who almost never walks into ad hoc prompting with a strategy. And maybe yesterday if I would have had more of a strategy when I was working with my network, uh Chad GPT wouldn\'t have caused me so much trouble. But I just went in and I just started typing. What I should have done to Chad GPT is said, \"We are doing something that is potentially extremely dangerous.\" Although I knew I thought it should have known that. Please be very careful and give me only one uh prompt at a time. So you can go in with a strategy. And if you go in with a strategy, you\'re going to be way better off. Now prompting also is beyond just text. So you can actually include images and audio and charts and graphs. Now you have to be using a multimodal model for this. And those multimodal models have a tendency to be more expensive. But in a lot of cases, the multimodal models are really important. And one thing is if if you\'re in Proxmbox, you and you\'re in the console, you cannot copy and paste. So as I was getting matter and matter at chatgpt yesterday, I did not want to keep typing the errors in. So I was just screen clipping and then uploading an image of the errors instead of um having to retype them all the time. And so that\'s where multimodal prompting comes in. And multimodal prompting is definitely very very powerful. Now multimodal is also both inputs and outputs. Now personally I have not found a way to prompt good enough that I get a decent image or a decent uh video out of it. I I\'ve my prompting has been uh mostly geared towards text, but I\'ve seen some pretty amazing prompts come out of people or amazing pictures come out of some really nice looking prompts. So now we\'re going to get into the prompting styles. And most of the time the prompting style has to do with how you\'re iterating and what you\'re giving it. So the first part of it are the zero shot, one shot and few shot. So zero shot you\'re giving it no examples. You\'re saying give bake me a cake. I want a chocolate cake with vanilla icing and you\'re going to get what you get. Now one shot prompting is I\'m going to give you one example. Now that is not allowing the large language model to do more than basically say okay this is kind of how I want the output to be and this gives me some ideas. It\'s when you get into fshot programming where you\'re giving it like two to five examples. So if I were to say, I want my chocolate cake, vanilla icing, sprinkles, um a lemon wedge, and here are five examples of um other cakes that I\'ve liked in the past. If you do that, you\'re going to get a pretty good output. And you\'re probably going to get a pretty good output on the first try. And if you don\'t, that\'s where you\'re going to get into a couple of the other prompting styles, which are the iterative prompting style. Now, chain of thought prompting, that is one of the most complicated parts of prompt or types of prompting there is because those are using the reasoning models. And that\'s where you have you\'re you\'re prompting to help out the reasoning model reason through a problem. And so chain of thought prompting I I wanted to include it here because I didn\'t want to leave anything out. Same thing with react prompting which is reasoning and acting. But there they\'re definitely some complex prompting styles that if you get into writing multi- aent systems or orchestrators or those kind of things, you\'re going to run into these different types of prompting. So hold on one second. Um, okay. So, after the prompting styles, let\'s look at fshot program prompting. So, fshot prompting is probably one of the most popular and it\'s probably um one of the easiest ways to do so. What you\'re doing is you\'re providing a short series of input outputs and then you keep giving the model new inputs. So, you pro you say here\'s some examples. Okay, now let\'s uh let\'s change it a little bit. Okay, I don\'t want to use this kind of flower. I want to use this other kind of flower. Few shot prompting is it\'s a method where the prompt includes a few examples, but it also helps generalize with new inputs. So, as you\'re going through the prompting, you\'re refining the output. So you\'re starting with a few examples, you\'re giving it more information, and you\'re just completely iterating until you get to a certain output. Now, one of the things you can do with Fshot programming is reducing the need for fine-tuning. So if I can give you examples of let\'s say JSON arrays or JSON objects of this is how I want the output to look the better the model the more likely it is to be able to do that without supervised fine-tuning. So in the case of like um let\'s say I want my model to constantly I want it to output a J JSON objects. If I can give examples of the JSON objects in my prompt then I may not need to fine-tune the model to give me decent JSON objects. Now, this is much more of a real thing when we\'re talking about the big models like the GPTs, the clauds because those models have so many parameters. They can get they can gain the nuance and they can figure things out just from the prompting. Whereas with um a small model, let\'s say it\'s a 2 and a half billion parameter model, that\'s where just giving it the prompting probably isn\'t going to work for you and you\'re going to need to do something more intense like a um supervised fine-tuning. So in chain of but in chain of thought prompting we\'re going to guide the tools through the process and um you\'re going t