INSERT INTO `lessons` (`id`, `course_id`, `title`, `description`, `content`, `lesson_type`, `duration_minutes`, `order_index`, `status`, `is_mandatory`, `video_url`, `video_duration`, `attachment_url`, `quiz_data`, `assignment_data`, `created_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `published_at`, `video_transcript`) VALUES
(618, 34, 'Live Event from August 9, 2025', 'In this live event we covered running local models.', '', 'text', 75, 2, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/t-0EDfavJec', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-08-11 21:27:48', '2025-08-11 21:27:49', NULL, 'So today I wanted to cover local models and this is kind of in celebration of uh OpenAI finally deciding to release an open-source model. I actually when they announced that they were going to do an open- source model right after Deep Seek released, I didn\'t actually think it was real. I thought it was Sam Alman trying to, you know, keep up with the neighbors and doing a uh just announcing it and I\'ve expected to see it. So since we got it and it it seems to be pretty good. I\'ve played with it to a certain extent. I did have some problems running tools with it which was a problem that turned out to just resolve itself. So um we\'ll uh go through it. So the idea with local models, okay, local models, if if you\'re familiar with using models in the cloud, if you go to uh chadgptt.com or claude.com or wherever you\'re going, you\'re used to running the models on the cloud, which means you\'re actually running it on the equipment of the foundational model provider. So you\'re running it on Chad GPT\'s equipment, which they have billions of dollars. So they are running much bigger models and much more capable models than you could possibly run at home. But local models have their use cases. And with the fact that a lot of companies do not want to send their proprietary information to the cloud for processing, especially after Open AI got sued by the New York Times and they have to turn over their prompts and their responses to a court. A lot of people aren\'t all that happy about the idea of sending your prompts to um to the cloud models. The other thing is the cloud models tend to get really expensive really fast. If you\'re using uh the claw API for writing code and you\'re not you don\'t have the max plan, you can run up your payer use uh really quickly. So local models actually run entirely on your equipment and they use programs like Olama, which was really the first one. Now, a llama I I really liked at first, but it has a tendency to crash my Mac. So, I started going and I switched over to LM Studio. And one of the things I really like about LM Studio is it\'s a lot easier for developers. So, if you\'re a developer and you want to use MCP tools or you want to do a pipeline, it\'s a lot easier to do that with LM Studio. Okay. So, let me get back to my slideshow. because my cat is actually hitting keys. So, we\'ll be moving that keyboard. So, um the your local model is going to run entirely on your local computer. And that is compared to models that run in the cloud like Chad GPT, Claude, Gemini, and Grock. Now, when you are running local models, you have some benefits. Now, the benefits for the biggest benefit to me is that all your data is staying locally on your machine. There\'s no third-party exposure. Now, if you\'re doing things like writing code with them, there\'s no cost per token or no subscription fees. So, once you\'ve got your hardware and you\'ve got it to run, you can run everything local. Now, latency is also a thing, but I have found that as the lo the cloud models get better and better, you can\'t really call this a benefit of the local models because the local models are going to be slower, especially if you\'re pushing the limits of your hardware. But you do get near instant response. You\'ve got no dependency. You have the internet and you\'re not subject to uh claude going down occasionally, chat GBT going crazy occasionally. You\'re you\'re not um you\'re not constrained by that. So the other thing is is you have full choice over you have your control of the model. You have your full choice. You can choose which model, which version, and which model you want to tune. Now the whole thing with Chad GPT5 is actually making this more important because one of the things that Chad GPT is not doing is being backward compatible. So one of the things that OpenAI announced is that when Chad GPT is out and stable, it\'s going to be the only model. You\'re not going to have access to 35 or 4 or 41 or whatever you\'re used to using. So if you build an application, let\'s say you build a chatbot and that chatbot is working really really well and it\'s working really well with chat GPT35, you got it exactly the way you want it and then all of a sudden open AAI says no, now you\'re using five. Well, just because a model is bigger and just because a model knows better does not necessarily mean that it\'s better for your use case. So if you\'re going to run an application and you need it to work day in day out for years, you\'re better off with a local model because you can choose to use the old version of the model and you can use it as long as you want to. As soon as you\'ve got it, it\'s it\'s in your inventory and you can use it. The other thing that is great is, you know, you\'ve got offline use. It works without network access. Now, this is not as big of a flex as you you kind of think it\'s going to be because in the end, you still want your model to go out and be able to search the web, be able to get resources from other locations. So, yes, it does work without network access, but that\'s not necessarily, you know, as big of a deal as you can make it out to be. Now, the other thing is customization. Now, because you\'re running a smaller model and you\'re running a local model, you can customize it. And there\'s definitely some benefits to customization. So, um, now the drawbacks include the hardware requirements. Now, I am running on a Mac M1, a MacBook Pro M1 with 32 gig of RAM. Now, the difference between the M1 and the M4 when it comes to Apple Silicon is like night and day. So, I have another box over here, which is my Mac Mini. And this has 24 gig of RAM. It was only about $1,500 and it runs about eight times faster than my Mac, my M1 MacBook Pro. So, it really does run a lot faster. It just doesn\'t have as much memory and I always have to jump through hoops in order to get it up and running. So, it\'s I I still am going to use my MacBook Pro. Now, the in addition to the hardware requirements, you also have the small model size. So when you\'re running a even a 20 billion parameter model is nothing compared to a 1.7 trillion parameter model. So you are getting you are going to suffer from the capabilities of the small model the small model sizes. Now with the new reasoning models and the fact that the models can run tools that gap is getting smaller and smaller. it even a a 1 point a trillion parameter plus model is not going to perform that much better than a small model if that small model has tool access. Now the cloud models also provide extra benefits and you don\'t realize everything that chat GPT does for you until you run it locally because Chad GPT4 is basically an AI agent wrapped around an LLM. when you go out and ask it relevant current events, it\'s running uh local agents to go out to the web to surf. You can even see in chat GBT where it says checking the web. So there\'s a lot of things that the cloud model providers are adding for you that you\'re not getting from the local models. And then another drawback is they\'re definitely slower. you there\'s no there\'s really no arguing that a local model is going to be as fast as it as something that has multiple billion dollars worth of hardware behind it. You can\'t make that argument but that is a trade off that is usually worth having. The the next problem now this is the problem I see the most especially trying to use local models if you\'re using it for coding is the size of the context window. Now, one of the beautiful things about the new GPT OSS is I think it\'s a 156k context window or it might be actually closer to 200k context window. So, you have a decent size context window, but the thing you have to realize about your context window is it has to fit in your memory. So, if you\'ve got it where it just barely fits in your um memory the way you\'ve got it, that is usually just barely fitting with a much smaller context window. So, I tried to bump my context window up to I think it was 10K today and it decided that it was not having any of that. So, uh that actually caused me to have to reboot my uh computer earlier. So the context window size is a problem and tool use is complex. You you have to understand how MCP servers work in order to effectively use MCP. So so let me make sure this is the right. Okay. So there are times that local models are definitely not going to be right for you. So if you need the most capable model, you need nuance. Now nuance is a big deal. So, a lot of times when you\'re doing something with a reasoning model, those big reasoning models are really good at just figuring things out and understanding subtle differences between things. You do not get that with the local models, even the biggest of the local models. Now, I have not been able to run the 120 billion parameter GPTOs, but look, 120 billion parameters still is not a trillion parameters. So, I doubt that you\'re going to get the nuance from the local models, even when they\'re a little bit bigger. And if you need a ton of built-in knowledge, these local models just aren\'t going to have them. Now, sometimes the fact that they don\'t have the built-in knowledge is a benefit for you because what you end up with is um the larger models have a tendency to be more opinionated. So uh Gemini is extremely opinionated and it\'s going if it knows something you are not going to be able to override it with like some retrieval augmented generation or some alternative facts. So you if you need the more capable models and you need the tools that they automatically provide. Again, when you start running local models, that is when you\'re going to figure out all the things that they don\'t do. And that\'s actually when you\'re going to figure out all the things that Chad GPT does for you automatically just because they\'ve decided they want to be nice. So local models come in formats that are different than you would be running it if you were running it in your data center. So the most common format you\'re going to download, and this is important, if you\'re going to hugging face to get your models, if you\'re going to.com to get your models, it\'s going to be in GGUF format because that\'s what uh Olama supports. If you go to LM Studio, it\'s going to be in either WLX, which is their format, or it\'s going to be in GGUF. So, um, LM Studio will run them both. And then you\'ve got some more um more some less common ones if you\'re actually running the model yourself. So, if you\'re writing your own code in Python, you can use PTH and BIN, which are PyTorch formats for running your own model. And then there\'s something called safe tensors that you can run. So there\'s a couple of things you have to look at when you\'re running local models. The first of which is the format. The second of which is the quantization. Now quantization is basically the rounding of real numbers. So when you first train a model, you\'re going to it\'s going to get trained in either 32bit or 16 bit. usually in 32-bit. 32bit has a lot of precision after the decimal point. So, think of weights and biases as being a number between 0ero and one. And how precise that number is is based on how many points you can get after the decimal point. So, if you\'re using 32bit floating point, you can get a lot of numbers after the decimal point. And that\'s very, very precise, but it takes up a lot of memory. So in this case it\'s going to take 32 bits for every weight and every bias. So every parameter. So if you um so if you are using let\'s say a 20 billion parameter model if you are going at u 32bit precision it\'s going to require 80 gigabytes just to load. So what you do in these local models is they quantize them down into local a lower precision. So you could go with a 16 bit, an 8 bit, and a 4bit. And in almost every case, you\'re going to find that um these local models are running at 4bit. And what happens when they\'re running at a lower precision? It means you\'re more subject to hallucination for one because it\'s doing the math and the math, the rounding of the math can get off. and you lose the nuance. So you lose you lose what you get by having more precision. So the goal of quantization is to reduce your file size and memory usage so that larger models can run on smaller hardware. So that\'s a good goal to have being able to run as big a model on as small hardware as possible. But it produces a tradeoff. So there may be situations where let\'s say you have 32 gig of RAM and you\'re better off running a 1 12 billion parameter model at full quantization or no quantization than you are running a 20 billion parameter model at 4bit. So this is where testing it for your use case comes in. Now mostly when I\'m talking about using local models, I\'m talking about using them in applications because personally uh I don\'t get the answers I\'m looking for from the local models in a time that I\'m looking to get them. So for example, the first query I gave to GPTOSS 20 billion parameter was if it\'s 100 p.m. in Central European time, what time is it in Chicago? And it struggled with that answer. And the reason it struggled with that answer is it did not know if it was daylight savings time or standard time in Chicago. So it didn\'t know if it was a 7-hour difference or a 6-hour difference. And just the process of it thinking about it meant that that answer took probably 3 minutes to get and it had to add another question which is what day are what day is it? Because when I was running it, it didn\'t have any access to tool use. So it couldn\'t go out and run a little Python script to go out and find out what day it was to know if it was six or seven hours different. So when I\'m talking about tool use, I\'m really talking about using it for applications, applications that you don\'t want to get hammered by expensive API usage because you\'re still in in a lot of cases and and I I\'d say in almost most cases, the free version of chat GPT is going to give you better responses than you know probably one of your best local models. But when it comes to applications where let\'s say you\'re running a call center or you\'re doing a ton of transformations on data, maybe you\'re doing vector embeddings, that is when a local model is definitely going to come in because you\'re getting you\'re getting u use out of it. You\'re you\'re getting you\'re you\'re getting the benefits of it not costing a fortune to run. So if we look at quantization, we have a whole bunch of different types of quantization. So full precision at FP32 is something you\'re almost never going to see because unless you\'re running your own data center with Nvidia GPUs, even the smallest model isn\'t going to fit at FP32. Um, now they say that FP16 and BF-16 have nearly the same accuracy. And I do believe that cuz there is a point at which the num the precision isn\'t going to matter that much if you don\'t have as many parameters. So if if you have a trillion parameters, then you\'re going to need the nuance of FP32. But if you\'re looking at something like even 120 billion parameters, you can probably get away with FP16. Now then you start getting to the smaller ones which is you get to int 8 which is it says it\'s a good balance of size and accuracy but it\'s much faster and that is actually another thing you have to realize when you\'re running local models the higher the number so if you\'re FP32 the math the math that it\'s running is more complicated it\'s going to use more resources and it\'s going to get considerably slower so what you\'re going to be used to is 4-bit quantization or um if you really just if you\'re just playing around, you can do two bitid quantization and it is pretty bad. So that\'s really not a direction you go. Now there is another option called mixed quantization and that is where different layers are quantized at or quantized at different precision levels. So instead of using uh like an uh FP4 for the entire model, you might use an FP16 at some of the top layers or the bottom layers or there\'s a whole different strategy. And there are a lot of different models out on hugging face that have been trained experimenting with different mixed quantization. And so mix quantization sometimes can get you really good accuracy and performance, but it doesn\'t actually uh hurt the model much. So parts of the model are getting higher pre um higher precision and the less important parts of the model are getting less quant uh quantization. Now when you\'re choosing a local model, you\'ve got a couple of main choices you need to make. And the first one is reasoning models versus non-reasoning models. Now reasoning models are really the thing now, but they are slow. So you have to realize that you know just asking the question of what time is it in Chicago when it\'s 100 p.m. Central European time. That takes several minutes reasoning. Now the reality though is it it\'s going to get you the right answer. Whereas the non-reasoning models are optimized for fast and direct responses. If I was using a non-reasoning model, it probably would have returned in just a couple seconds and said um it\'s six or seven hours depending on what day of the year it is and it would have just come back immediately. So you want to choose your model based on what you\'re doing. So if you\'re writing a paper and you\'ve already got all the content for the paper, so you don\'t need it going out and doing searches, a non-reasoning model is going to return just as good of results as a reasoning model. But if you\'re trying to write code then or you\'ve got multi-complex multi-step thinking tasks, you\'re going to need to use a reasoning model. Now the common reasoning models are deepsear 1. Um I\'ve never heard of open Hermes. So I just copy and pasted that. Mistral I\'ve used quento5 I\'ve used and then um obviously uh chat GP or GPTOSS is a reasoning model. And when you start running it you definitely can see that it\'s a reasoning model. Now the next important thing when you\'re selecting a model is can it use tools? And I\'m going to go to uh real quick. I\'m going to go to alama.com. So when you go to aluma.com to find models and you can do the same thing at open llm when you go into models you do want to use the models that support tools. So tools like GPTO OSS, Deepcar 1, Quen 3, Llama 3.1, they all support tools. Now think of tools like being able to take a test using a calculator. I remember when I was in high school, I was learning to do square root and my father was telling me how you can\'t you\'re not going to have a calculator. you you you have to learn how to do it because you have to know how to do it because you\'re not going to have a calculator. Well, this is like giving Chad GPT a calculator. And it does it by calling something called model context protocol. And the the model is actually trained to go out to MCP, get the tools and how the tools work and then structure a JSON call to actually call the tools. So MCP is a standard that lets your AI models call external tools. Now it is really critical with these small models because the small models don\'t know very much. They weren\'t trained with a lot of recent information. And if you look at, you know, Chat GPT is or OpenAI is really good at hyping things. And one of the things that they hyped was that their 20 billion parameter OSS model is almost as good as 03 as long as it has tool use because it\'s optimized to actually go out and write Python code. So if it so for instance if it needs to know what day of the year it is uh GPTOSS will write a quick little Python script that will return what day it is. And basically just giving um OSS Python access completely changes the precision and the quality of the model. So in almost every case, you\'re definitely going to want to make sure that the that the models you choose can use tools. Now tools are crazy important because of some of the examples that you can do with it. So the first thing is database queries. So you can actually have your tool go out to a local SQL database and fetch records for a report. And if you\'re using local models for building applications, which is what I highly recommend you use them for, this is something that you\'re going to want to do. So your the the MCP the uh your prompt says, I need this information. The MCP server says, oh, I know where that is. That\'s in the SQL database. It creates an SQL statement and then it calls the SQL database and it fetches the the records. Another use is web browsing. Now, this is where Chad GPT shines because so many things and it\'s actually also where Gemini shines shines. So many times when you go and ask it, hey, I I\'m trying to do this in um LM Studio. Well, what it\'s basically doing is it\'s going to the LM Studio website and reading the web pages and then summarizing it for you. So, web browsing is huge. anytime you need to access the file system, that is another time you\'re going to be using MCTP tools. And then anytime you want to execute custom scripts and this is kind of where the LLM itself is really good because chat GPT5 for sure, Claude for sure and then I and most of the local models are really good at writing Python. They write Python pretty much as good as any human and they can uh do write their own scripts to get the information that they need and they\'re pretty much smart enough to be able to do that. So in practice the way this works is you go into LM Studio or you go into your API or however you do it. You put in a user prompt. So you might say summarize all new orders for the week. The model then decides that it needs to be able to get all the new orders from the week because all the new orders for the week are not trained in the model. So the model says I need to go get fresh data and it has a list of all of the tools that it has access to and it says call the order API tool. It goes out runs a SQL query on the local database and then it basically takes that information runs a SQL query takes the result pushes it back into the model and then the model generates a natural language summarization using the retrieved data. So this is crazy powerful and local models are getting really really powerful. Now, uh, I\'m kind of with with Chad GPT 20 billion running as well as it does on only 14 gig of of RAM, I think that the local models are really getting to the point where in a ton of different use cases, they\'re really going to be models that you can get away with using. Now, the other thing that the real the one thing that used to be the selling point for local models when they weren\'t as capable was that you could, excuse me, make them more capable by supervised fine-tuning. And supervised fine-tuning has this mystique to it like it\'s this super highly technical process that you have to have years of Python experience to do. That is absolutely incorrect. Uh you can basically be a script kitty, copy and paste some code, go to hugging face and supervise fine-tune all day long. So supervised fine-tuning is the process of training an existing AI model on labeled data set. So you basically get the data. Now with supervised fine-tuning, the data is usually harder to get than the tuning itself. So for instance, let\'s say you\'ve got a chatbot and the chatbot is working for Best Buy and the chatbot wants to answer questions about your orders. Well, you\'re running a local you\'re running a small local large language model. So, what you\'re going to do is you are going to pass in a set of example conversations. You\'re going to say this was a conversation that we had. This is what good output looks like. And as the model get is trained, it gets better and better. So, basically what you\'re doing and the reason it\'s called supervised is it\'s guided by correct answers. So it says the the customer asked me this. I responded with this and it you respond with the correct answers. You don\'t try to outsmart it by having it try to figure out what a good answer and a bad answer is. You just give it correct answers. And then this is going to help the model specialize into a specific domain, follow instructions or improve the accuracy. Now a lot of so if you go to chat GPT a lot of times you start your prompt with you are a helpful assistant or you are a medical expert or you are an advanced reasoning coach or whatever it is. Those huge models have the nuance. They have the precision and they have the number of parameters that they can return the output like a helpful assistant, like a medical assistant, like a dental assistant, like a lawyer. It those big models are big enough that they have the nuance, but the local models are not. They are going to give you pretty much how whatever their mood is, that\'s how they\'re going to respond. So by fine-tuning the model, you are going to teach it to respond exactly the way you want to. So supervised fine-tuning is very good for helping the model specialize like being a medical assistant. And it\'s really um it\'s done by just feeding the model thousands of in in the case of medical, you just um feed it thousands of medical Q&A pairs. So it really becomes an expert as a virtual doctor. Now there is some different types of supervised fine-tuning. The one that almost everybody uses is called parameter efficient fine-tuning. And in that case, you\'re not actually changing the model itself. You\'re adding an additional layer to the model with your supervised with your supervised training data. So there are different ways to do it. and the different ways you do the fine-tuning depends on how much hardware you need to do it. But for the most part, let\'s say you are you\'ve got your AI agent business or you\'re selling tools or you\'re doing whatever it is you\'re doing with AI. Almost everything you\'re going to need to do with supervised fine-tuning, you can do it on Google Collab or on HuggingFace. It\'s going to be pretty rare that you\'re going to be doing enough supervised finetuning that you\'re going to need to go out and buy a a room full of Nvidia GPUs. For $20 a month with Google Collab, you have I think it\'s access to A100\'s and that is definitely enough to do any supervised finetuning that you need to do. The only problem with supervised finetuning is there\'s a lot of trial and error to it. So you can do a supervised fine-tuning, come back and just realize it was a train wreck and you need to do it again or and it could be because you don\'t have your data sets right. There is some trial and error to it, but it\'s not as big of a deal as people make it out to be. Now, the common problems you run into with local models when you don\'t have supervised fine-tuning is you get very generic responses. Sometimes very generic responses are perfectly fine but uh in a lot of cases you want your uh responses to be domain specific. So it also struggles with industry specific terminology. So if it\'s got you know if if you\'ve got words that you\'re only using in law or words that you\'re only using in medicine doing this supervised fine-tuning make sure that those words become a priority in the output. The other problem without supervised fine-tuning is it may not follow a fixed style. And you in a lot of cases, especially, let\'s say you\'re doing a chatbot, you don\'t want a chatbot that\'s going to act different every time somebody talks to it, especially if you\'re using it to generate a a realistic conversation. You want it to be consistent. And that\'s one of the problems with local models without supervised fine-tuning is you can\'t just put something in your prompt to make it consistent. So another issue with with local models without supervised fine-tuning is it is much more likely to produce incorrect or fabricated details if it\'s a very specialized task and the smaller models are definitely going to underperform compared to the cloud models without customization. So, personally, if I was doing, let\'s say I was doing a an automation and I was doing a chatbot where I wanted it to um answer consistently, the first thing I would look at is can I afford the token cost by using of using one of the cloud models and can I get it where I need it to be by prompting? If I can\'t get it to be where I need it to be by prompting or I need it to I I\'m just going to use it so much that I really can\'t afford the token costs, that\'s when I\'m going to go to a local model and I\'m going to add supervised fine-tuning into the mix. So the reasons for using supervised fine-tuning is you\'re basically adapting a very small general purpose LLM to your industry or business needs. It\'s going to enhance the instruction following. it\'s going to format the output better. In a lot of cases, that\'s the only thing you\'re going to do with supervised fine-tuning. So, you can teach a model to always return JSON objects or always return uh XML. You know, if you\'ve got a format that you want to be very specific, you can make it happen with supervised fine-tuning. And supervised fine-tuning is a way to make the smaller, cheaper model perform like the big model. Now the other thing you\'re going to do almost religiously with local models is retrieval augmented generation and retrieval augmented generation and MCP tools are the the definition or the line between the two are really starting to blur. So a year ago before MCP you would have called everything retrieval augmented generation that uh consists of going out getting information putting it in the prompt when you send the prompt to the LLM. But since MCP tools have happened most MCP tools do some sort of retrieval augmented 