ou\'re going to get that information is outside of the model because it\'s not it\'s not trained into the model. So it is really important for local models because the local models are very limited in the amount of training data that they have. So they really are not going to know recent or private information. So retrieval augmented generation is going to give them access to your current your proprietary or your regulator your regulated data without retraining. So it also with local models that\'s keeping all of your data on premise. So if you\'ve got something that you don\'t want sent to Chad GPT but it\'s local proprietary information that you\'re pulling out of a database this is the way to do it. So again the the benefits are privacy. It\'s always up to date. you\'ve got your domain expertise without SFT. Now, that\'s an interesting statement because the way I see it is you should try to do everything without supervised fine-tuning. If you can get it to work without SFT, do it. And then if so, if you start with retrieval augmented generation, it\'s possible retrieval augmented generation is going to be enough unless you\'ve got specific formats. And then when you determine that you can\'t do it with retrieval augmented generation, add supervised fine-tuning into the mix. Other thing about it for local AI is it\'s very hardware um friendly. You can basically use much more smaller amounts of data and you don\'t have to fine-tune nearly as much. So an example stack for retrieval augmented generation would be something like LM Studio, a vector database like Chroma DB or Pine Cone, an embedding model, and then an orchestration framework like Langchain or Llama Index. So let\'s talk about getting started because that\'s where I want to get into this and just kind of show you how we do it. In my case, I\'m using a Mac M series. Uh the thing about the M series is that it has the unified memory. So when an M series Mac says it\'s got 32 gig of RAM, that RAM can either 