 writing your own local MCP servers, especially if you\'re running local models, is the next natural step after just getting the models to run. So if um right now I\'m just working on testing a prototype for a product um and so as it scales up if it\'s going to be let\'s see um hardware uh technology that\'s going to use um LLM and um artificial intelligence to support it. Um then it\'s something to maybe how how large can you create an MCP? Can you create uh an MCP um that\'s as large as a as a a public you know open uh model? Um is that kind of so the MCP itself is actually pretty small cuz the so see that\'s the thing is an MCP is of infinite size because what the MCP is doing itself is it could be calling a giant API. It could be going to a giant data warehouse. The MCP server itself is really just something called a model card and the functionality. So the what the MCP server is doing is it is telling the large language model that I\'m available. These are the things I can do and this is how you call me. And so it\'s basically the MCP server itself is actually the documentation of what you\'re going to do as part of the tool. So you could create an MCP server that goes against a multi-terabte uh database in let\'s say Oracle or DB2 or something like that. But the the model itself, you don\'t have to worry about the model get or the the MCP server getting too big because you\'re going to actually it\'s going to go off and do its own thing either somewhere on the network or you know you know I I have local servers so I like to run things on on my own servers but the the size of the MCP server itself is actually not um the concern. Performance is the concern though because you do need your MCP server has to come back with a response in a reasonable amount of time or the LLM is going to give up on it. You can\'t go out and do something that\'s a full table scan of a six million row table. Uh do we have to think about hardware when it comes to that? Absolutely. Absolutely you do. I mean there\'s a MCP has its own architectural challenges and so you just have to you know work through um to work through them but they\'re they\'re not that heavy of a challenge and you when you have Claude to help you um you can you can pretty much get through it. So yeah that that\'s the way to go about it. All right. Hey Ed. Uh thanks again for your uh your sessions. They\'re awesome. So, I\'m a little fuzzy on how it goes from the prompt to the tool. And I it\'s what I\'m hearing is that the LLM is deciding to use a tool and which one if you know better in your prompt like through the system prompt, can you influence it? They use this tool because it\'s to me it\'s everything\'s so fuzzy like Yeah. Yeah. So, one of the things you can do, so notice I only, if you look down here at my screen, I have three tools installed. I only have this one except turned on. So, one thing to do is turn off and on the MCP tools, but the other thing to do is actually in your prompt tell it what tools to use. So what the the LLM is trained to do is when it comes to the conclusion either by you telling it, hey, we want you to use a tool or go out to the web and look for this. It\'s then going to look and say, all right, what tools do I have access to? Then it\'s going to read the model card for each of those tools and it\'s going to make the decision which of the tools it wants to call to get to the second part of the prompt. Right? That is why you need the reasoning models for the tool use because it\'s got to figure out a multi-step process. It\'s not a singleshot prompting. It\'s saying get me this information so I can do the thing I need to do. Yeah, that makes sense because everything being fuzzy, it seems like I know what I want it to do and I know we\'re not procedural in this world anymore like I am in my current world. But being being able to uh influence it to use a specific tool because I know it can do better using it. It\'s good. Yes. And you can put that up here in this in the system prompt. You could say you could give it like an order of priority. You know, check the hugging face MCP server first, right? If you cannot do what you need to do, use this server. You could you can definitely influence it or you could just make it so that only those models or only those tools are turned on for use. Thank you. So all right, John, how are you? Hi Ed. Um thank you so much. Uh this has been and has always been very wonderful experience. Um I\'m just curious. I know you you talked about Olama um needing it to restart your your PC but I just want to know have you had any experience with open web UI and if you have I just like to get feedback from you or something. Yes, I have open web UI installed on this box and I use it a lot for two things. One, if you want it to if you really want that um chat GPT look and feel, open web UI is great. The other thing I like open web UI for is it has more um it has more what\'s the right word? It has it has more API different API calls. So with Olama itself, you only have the default API calls of OpenAI whereas with Open Web UI, it has a whole set of other API calls and it also has um the Swagger documentation. And I don\'t know if you\'re familiar with Swagger, but Swagger makes it really easy to test API calls. And so I like using Open Web UI for that reason. Uh but since I started using LM Studio, I have not thought about open web UI as much as I used to because you know LM Studio really has the combination of running the model and having a pretty decent interface built into it. Um there are also certain things about open web UI that I like and some that I don\'t. One thing that I don\'t really like is the fact that you have to create an account and if you\'re calling it uh if you\'re calling the API, you actually have to give it an create an O token for a local uh LLM call, which I understand they\'re doing it to be compatible with the with the cloud models, but yeah, there\'s there\'s there\'s certain good things about it. I do highly recommend it if you\'re using Olama. If you\'re using LM Studio, it\'s it\'s not quite as necess as necessary. Okay. Vortex Vision Marketing. I always love how these uh things show up on Zoom. Thank you, Ed. Um yeah, so I just had a question. Um the VP, if you\'re using a local model, could you run it on a VPS? And I know obviously privacy would be a major drawback for this. Um there\'s no reason not to run a it on like a virtual private server. There\'s there I you got to realize what is the what is your hardware it okay a couple ways it you have to have access to GPU one way or the other. So if you\'re running like a virtual machine I\'m assuming that\'s what you mean by VPS. you need to have uh GPU access either through the unified memory of the Mac or access through um usually like if you\'re using Proxmox to do virtual machines, you\'re using something like a PCI pass through to make it work. But um it\'s it\'s going to be trickier and it isn\'t because you need like bare metal, you know, where you need to actually get to the hardware itself. It\'s more that virtualizing GPUs is is tricky. Did I answer your question? Because I\'m not sure I actually understood it 100%. I I think I think maybe that\'s more geared towards virtual machines which is like you know if you have the images and containers and so on. I I think what I\'m more referring to is like uh for example there\'s like Hostinger uh they have VPS servers that you could uh rent uh for a very cheap uh price per month and then you they provide you uh vCPUs and uh a lot of specs. You\'re you\'re just basically renting a private server, right? Gotcha. Um, that\'s going to be hard because those usually do not have GPU and they usually don\'t have nearly as much memory as you\'re going to need to run a local model. Now, and what about latency? The the network latency isn\'t going to be as big of a deal because in the end, all you\'re doing in that case is you\'re um the only thing that\'s coming over the wire is you\'re watching it do its work. So the latency isn\'t going to be as bad. I think it\'s going to be when you\'re using a virtual private server, the lack of GPU resources. So the one that I use, uh, I use a a thing called inter server. And the reason I use that is you can get bare bone bare metal proxmox hosting. So you can actually host your own virtual private servers from a virtual private server or from a private server. Um but I think for local models the performance is going to be so bad without GPU. Now you can run uh Olama with CPU only but I I think the the I think the performance will kill you and the need for that much RAM is going to kill you because you have to be able to fit all of the RAM in the memory. Yes. Yes. So um Okay. All right. Um, Vasco, did I Yeah, you\'re you\'re next or your hand is up. So, hi. Thanks so much for today. Um, my question is how unrealistic would it be to think of some sort of to add encryption to a communication with a cloud model? Almost not at all. Um, because in the end the cloud model has to get it in plain text. So you by default because Chad GPT is HTTPS and it\'s running over SSL. You are you are encrypted pointtooint. You\'re encrypted in transit. Uh so you don\'t have to worry about anybody stealing your prompts from your PC to the cloud model. you have to worry about the cloud model getting sued by the New York Times and then the New York Times turning over your prompts to uh to a to the Southern District of New York. That\'s really more of the worry than the encryption when it comes to sending your sending your data over. So, did I answer? So, unless you\'re speaking a language Well, but no, you couldn\'t because the LM\'s trained in English. It has to be in English. Yeah. I don\'t know. Well, actually, no. I mean, it depends. There\'s What\'s the The Alibaba models. One of the newer ones is amazing that it speaks, I think, 157 languages. Okay. And it definitely is not There are multilingual and multimodal models. The one thing it doesn\'t speak is encryption. So, you can\'t send it in encrypted. You can\'t send it in. Now, that is actually a really good idea, by the way. I just had I was like cuz that would solve a huge problem. Now now that I\'m actually thinking about it, unfortunately I don\'t have a billion dollars to train my own foundational model. Yeah, exactly. Actually sending a private key public key to a model um and then sending the prompts encrypted. Y is actually a really really good idea. And if Enthropic is listening, we want a commission. No, that\'s actually that\'s actually a really that\'s actually a really good idea. I just don\'t think we we could do it. So what what would you need to you would need to train an LLM from scratch on a certain encryption layer, right? or a language language. At some point you the last step before you go to the model has to be decrypting what you sent to the model. The problem is even if it\'s only a short wire from the time it\'s decrypted to the time it goes to the model it\'s still interceptable. If yeah, if you\'re a court, you would actually just need to build into the model itself a U public private key encryption algorithm that I I could actually see how to do it. I I just don\'t it\'s the training the model from scratch. Maybe it\'s something you could supervise fine-tune a local model to do it, but I think it would have to be done through the AP. I mean, I I I I see the value in it. I I think somebody will eventually do it, but I don\'t think I\'ll be the one to do it because I think that there\'s some challenges to it that that are above my pay grade. So, but yeah, that still is a a good idea for anybody who\'s listening. Um Juma, how\'s it going? What\'s going on? uh talking about the system prompts and let\'s say I\'m connecting this to my my application is there an autoated automated way to like constantly feed it information like is that through the MCP server like if I had documentation that my app wanted to use and just have that constantly getting updated and then pushed to the local model you all right so the thing is is if you\'re going to push it to the local model you have to have a capability to clear the local model. So because it think of it as being like a glass of water um when it fills up it just spills over, right? So it and that\'s why when you\'re using clawed or you\'re using Gemini, you\'ll see it say it\'s compressing its context. So, I actually had some really bad I had a pretty bad experience with this uh OSS until I increased the context window. So, I was just putting too much in. So, if you\'re talking about constantly pushing more and more data into the context window, you\'re going to want to clear the context window and then push new data in. So, that\'s really where context engineering comes in because yes, you need to do it. That\'s exactly what claude code does. So when I\'m working on multiple programs in claude code, it my especially for Skilix, the codebase is way too big to fit inside the context window of cla code. So claude is constantly pushing stuff into the context window and pulling stuff out of the context window. The thing that I don\'t know how to do is pull the stuff out of the context window without clearing the entire thing. So, yes, there\'s a use case for that. I just don\'t know exactly how to do it. So, all right. Um, I want to thank everybody for showing up. A bunch of you I\'ll see tomorrow. I think a big chunk of you I\'ll see you to tomorrow when we\'re going to try to build this MCT MCP server from scratch. Um, hopefully I will know exactly how to do it by then. But, uh, I want to thank everybody for showing up and, uh, we will, uh, be in touch soon. Thank you\'all. Thank you. Take care.');
