(619, 176, 'You NEED to Use n8n RIGHT NOW!!', 'This is a great beginning n8n video', '', 'text', 30, 1, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/ONgECvZNI3o', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-08-12 01:58:40', '2025-08-12 01:58:41', NULL, 'Automatic transcript retrieval requires OAuth authentication. Please use OAuth flow or manually add transcript. Video title: You NEED to Use n8n RIGHT NOW!! (Free, Local, Private)'),
(620, 176, 'How to run n8n locally.', 'Running n8n locally is an important skill for deploying automations in places that will not allow cloud services.', '', 'text', 15, 2, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/-ErfsM2TYsM?si=U_ZCZcVbCZzb6ZJB', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-08-12 02:00:04', '2025-08-12 02:00:04', NULL, 'Automatic transcript retrieval requires OAuth authentication. Please use OAuth flow or manually add transcript. Video title: How to Run n8n Locally (Full On-Premise Setup Tutorial)'),
(621, 177, 'Part 1 - Introductio', 'Part 1 of the Vibe Coding Master Class', '', 'text', 15, 1, 'published', 1, 'https://youtu.be/nkiP-G8e9lw', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, '2025-08-20 17:49:50', '2025-08-20 17:49:51', NULL, 'Hey everybody, welcome. So this is introduction to vibe coding an enterprise project. So the idea here is it is a project management methodology and structure and kind of an overview master class. So whether you are a project manager or you\'re a software engineer or you\'re a subject matter expert who is contributing to the project, you are taking the right course. So what we are going to cover today is vibe coding and how to manage an enterprise project from start to finish using vibe coding. We\'re going to talk about how the methodology needs to change for doing this type of project when AI is your coding team. So the first thing we\'re going to look at is just how did vibe coding start. So back when Chad GBT first came out, because programming languages are languages and large language models speak languages, Chad GPT turned out to be pretty good at writing code. And what you would do is you had an idea, something you needed to do. You would say to Chad GPT, \"Write me a Python program that does this.\" You would then get the Python program. You\'d run it. It wouldn\'t work. You\'d paste the errors in, and you just go back and forth. And it was actually fairly revolutionary because Chad GBT was a pretty good codew writer. Uh you could spend hours doing it though and most of that time you were spending was due to the inefficiencies of the way you needed to work because you were constantly copying pasting code back and forth and Chad GPT just had this it made a lot of mistakes. So this thing took a long time. So since large language models are, you know, based on languages and programming languages are a lot like spoken languages, it was good for small tasks, but it definitely reached its limits pretty fast. So of course, somebody very ingenious said, \"Okay, let\'s do GitHub copilot. So let\'s let\'s move the LLM closer to the IDE, the interactive development environment. Let\'s move it closer so that it could just, you know, you could just copy the code inside of the editor and you could uh it could do type ahead for you. It could help you with your code in real time. It could answer questions and so but it was basically just taking that LLM and putting it inside your IDE so that you could work with it. And that was pretty revolutionary at the time. I personally did not have very much success with it because I found that it was getting in the way of my coding because I was still primarily coding myself. I was just using the LLMs to help me out. So, GitHub co-op pilot was basically an extension to the interactive development environment. Now, then that expanded to the next level which was cursor and windsf. And what cursor did is they took Visual Studio Code and since it\'s open- source, they made a fork of it and then they built Cursor around the IDE. So instead of the IDE having an an add-on put into it, they basically said everything about the IDE is all about coding with a large language model first. And that worked out really well and that got us to where we are with these interactive tools like cursor. So this meant that the coding large language models were built into the IDE and it gave the LLM the visibility of the entire project. So now at this point there were still not MCP tools. So it the cursor and windsurf counted on the IDE and the fact that the IDE had access to all the files to be able to open up the files and read them. So that\'s why the IDE was a critical part of these tools. Now from there though we got model context protocol when and when Enthropic came out with model context protocol that basically changed everything because that gave large language models tools which meant these tools could go out and they could search the web, they could read and write files and they could actually run the code. And so that made it so that when you got to the command line tools like clawed code, it was really powerful. It was able to open up files. It was able to search through the files. It was able to determine the relationships between the files. So it really took MCP took five coding to the next level. And then reasoning models really changed everything because now the model has a chain of thought. So it can say I want to open this file. I want to look for this. I want to reference this other file. And so the combination of having reasoning models gave it the ability to plan and the power to execute the plan. So it made the IDE no longer necessary. So where cursor and windurf were leaning on the interactive development environment, the CLI, the command line interface didn\'t need that anymore. It didn\'t need to have the ability for the IDE to do things on its behalf. So, Claude Code took the whole thing onto the command line and that\'s where you got the CLIs like Claude code and Gemini CLI. So, when you look at what makes a good VIDE coding system, it\'s obviously the coding ability of the LLM. Now in the case of cursor and wind surf they were leaning on the coding ability of chat GPT and then Gemini and then Enthropic they were leaning on somebody else\'s coding ability but in addition to that it\'s the quality of the tools that the system has access to. So if you\'ve got MCP servers now those tools can get really really good so they can do different things. So you don\'t actually have to depend on the LLM to do everything. You can ask a tool to do something for you. So the quality of the tools that are built into the system is one of the key components that makes a vibe coding system a really good vibe coding system. And then the third part is the system prompts. So there\'s a lot going on behind the scenes that you don\'t see when you\'re vibe coding. So built into all of these vibe coding tools is system prompts that guide the LLM to writing the code. So I think the number for clawed code is it\'s 27,000 tokens in the system prompt itself. So that clawed code is a lot of instructions that it\'s giving the LLM when the LLM is coding on your behalf. So that\'s what makes a good vibe coding system. But what makes a good vibe coder? Well, a good vibe coder is really somebody who knows how to use the tools correctly. They don\'t want to fight with the tools. They don\'t want to have to use the tools. Well, I\'m going to use, you know, I\'m going to use the tools for task A, B, and C, but I\'m still going to do D, E, and F on my own. The ability to use the tools correctly is probably the most critical thing to making somebody a good vibe coder along with having the vision to see what the application needs to do. And in some cases, that\'s why the project managers and the subject matter experts make such good vibe coders because they\'re seeing the big picture and they have the vision to see what the application needs to do. And then the third thing that makes a a person a good vibe coder is the ability to prompt the application to get the perfect results. The you know this is prompt engineering. The better your prompt, the better the results that are going to come out. So you need to actually kind of pull these three three things together to be a really good vibe coder. You got to be able to use the tools. You got to know what you want and you got to prompt for it. So then we go into when do you want to vibe code. Now we are going to find out over the next couple years that this answer is always. But um it does actually break down into specific tasks. So if you\'ve got an existing application, are we going to add features to the existing application with vibe coding? It\'s a great use for vibe coding. Are you refactoring an an existing application? And there are so many old legacy systems out there that need refactoring from old programming languages into new programming languages. And that is something that vibe coding is just great at because vibe coding can know both languages. Okay. Are you building a new application? Now, there\'s a lot of different types of applications you might be building. You might be building a new application that\'s a SAS application, like a browserbased application with a database. You might be building an a hybrid application that\'s looks like a SAS application, but it also looks like an AI application where you\'ve got AI functionality in your application. You might be building a mobile application. Okay, so that those are the types of new applications you might be building and those would be to fully applications. Now, you might also just be building an MCP tool. So, VIPO is great for building MCP tools. You can pretty much do that in one sitting. If you\'ve got the methodology down and you got the structure down, you can build an MCP tool in pretty much it\'s an easy process to get that done. And then the next thing is building AI automations and AI agents. Those are great for vibe coding. Vibe coding is way superior in doing this than these visual tools like an nadn or a make.com. So building AI automations is another time you would want to vibe code. Now vibe coding definitely has its pitfalls. Okay. Now vibe coding writes code really well but it doesn\'t know what it doesn\'t know. It is a coder. It\'s not an infrastructure planner. It\'s not a uh it\'s not managing your team for you. It\'s writing the code and it doesn\'t know it. I if you leave stuff out of the requirements, it doesn\'t know how well let\'s just add this in because we think it needs it. That\'s not going to happen. Although with claude sometimes, especially the old sonnet, it would add things you didn\'t ask for. But it\'s, you know, it doesn\'t know what you don\'t know. But another pitfall is you can be an absolute beginner and write some really good-looking software. I mean, you run this thing, you run the software, it really looks good, but is it secure? Can you deploy it? How scalable will it be? And can it even be supported? So, we\'re always hearing these horror stories about these types of systems that are, you know, kind of going down in flames because they were vibe coded, you know, incorrectly. So, when it comes to selecting a vibe coding tool, you\'ve got a couple of choices. You\'ve got the IDE versus the CLI. So the IDE is the interactive environment where you you can see all the files and you can see them as they\'re being changed where the CLI is the command line interface. The CLI is much better for big projects where you need the planning. You want to just kick something off in the background and come back a couple hours later. That\'s much better with the CLI. But the IDE is better for features. So if you\'ve got an existing application and something doesn\'t work, IDE is really good for opening it up, analyzing it, making some changes. Okay. So the IDE versus the CLI that is your first um decision point and then price versus capabilities. Uh and this really does should not matter in the corporate environment because your your client or your company should be paying for the best models and the best tools because your time is more expensive than the cost of the LLM. So then you need to look at the differences in the coding model. So you\'ve got your reasoning models versus your non-reasoning models. you there\'s very few cases you want to use a non-reasoning model in 2025 and beyond. Then you\'ve got the context size. The bigger the context, the more that can be held in the uh context. But then also the bigger the context, the more you have this thing called distractors. And those are things that are in the context that are unrelated to what you\'re trying to do and then just kind of get in the way and cause hallucinations. So context size is important but what\'s in the context is even more important. And then you have two different kinds of coding models. If we look at uh Gemini, Gemini is very opinionated. Gemini is going to tell you how to write that code. You better not fight it because it\'s going to write it its way. And then overly helpful. Claude son at 3.7 was overly helpful. It was it would you\'d ask it for something and it would look out in some other sample code that it finds and says, \"Oh, there were 17 features you\'re missing. So, we\'re just going to add that. So, that\'s opinionated versus overly helpful. So, now when we get to the point where we\'ve decided that we\'re going to do vibe coding, there are phases, okay? And I\'ve broken this down into three phases. And I\'m pretty confident in the three phases, although there is the thing where you\'re going to want to split it into multiple projects. And I\'ll get into that a little bit later. But the first phase is the planning and the initial build. And this is more like a traditional waterfall application. You can only do the initial build once or once at a time. You could always do the initial build, decide you don\'t like it, and start over. But once you pass the initial build and you go to that initial deployment and you get into the third step, which is the feature refinement and the continuous build, you can\'t redo the initial building again. So you\'ve got to make sure that when you get past that initial build to the initial deployment, you\'ve got it right. And that\'s why initial deployment is second. I see a lot of people making mistakes in vibe coding where they do the initial build, they start putting in the continuous build cycle. It\'s like, well, my initial build doesn\'t have all my features in it, so I don\'t want to deploy it. But then you get to the point where you find out you can\'t deploy it. Because if you make a few mistakes early on in the initial planning when it comes to your tech stack, it may be impossible to deploy your application in a secure manner, which is what you need in order to get it to a final production stage. So what we do is we say let\'s do the initial build and it doesn\'t have to be it it has to kind of have one of everything. It\'s it\'s got to test out your architecture. Then we deploy it to make sure it works. And then once we know it works, we get into the re the feature refinement and the continuous build cycle. And that\'s your traditional agile type of methodology. So we\'re going to start out as an overly structured waterfall at the planning in the initial build and then we\'re going to uh transition into an agile where we\'re going to do a continuous build cycle and ongoing refinement through sprints at the the right side of this chart. So let\'s start with the planning phase.'),
